        {
            "article_id": "7437726285a5b35c",
            "title": "PatMen reps the PH flag as PRX conquers Masters Toronto - Inquirer Esports",
            "published_at": "Wed, 25 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Inquirer Esports",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports.inquirer.net/35999/patmen-reps-the-ph-flag-as-prx-conquers-masters-toronto",
            "query_players": [
                "f0rsakeN",
                "PatMen"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "d4v41",
                "f0rsakeN",
                "mindfreak",
                "PatMen",
                "runneR",
                "something",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "PatMen reps the PH flag as PRX conquers Masters Toronto\nPatrick “PatMen” Mendoza emerged as a driving force for Singaporean Valorant powerhouse Paper Rex after they conquered the Toronto, Canada, edition of the Valorant Champions Tour (VCT) Masters on Sunday night.\nThis comes after Paper Rex dismantled European squad FNATIC, 3-1, to get the coveted trophy – the Southeast Asian powerhouse’s first-ever after years of heartbreak as runner-ups on the international stage.\nPatMen also becomes the first ever Pinoy VCT Pacific player to win a global title. Flex player, Indonesian Jason “f0rsaken” Susanto was hailed the Grand Finals MVP, collecting an average combat score (ACS) of 223, according to vlr.gg on Sova, Astra, and Omen.\nOn Map 1, Paper Rex snuck past FNATIC on Sunset, 13-11, denying overtime in full, Paper Rex fashion after putting up a grim, yet aggressive Sage wall play orchestrated by Wangg Jie “Jingg” Jie (Raze) and Khalish “d4v41” Rusyaidee (Sage).\nFNATIC evened the series on Icebox, breaking the deadlock on the third overtime and ending the map, 17-5.\nPaper Rex would get the next couple of maps next, overwhelming FNATIC on Pearl, 13-10, before winning on overtime in Lotus, 14-12.\nDefying the odds at VCT Pacific\nPatMen and Paper Rex defied odds, bucking a lackluster performance in the group stages of VCT Pacific to ultimately finish the regional tournament in third place, before conquering the world’s biggest Valorant tournament so far in 2025.\nIn the regional playoffs, Paper Rex defeated Masters: Bangkok champions T1, top-seeded Boom Esports and DRX to get the last ride ticket to Toronto, before losing steam to eventual champions RRQ Hoshi.\nPatMen made his debut in Paper Rex’s loss against Boom Esports, replacing Indonesian Aaron “mindfreak” Leonhart.\nPatMen, the only Pinoy in a team stacked with SEA’s greatest, admitted to experiencing growing pains as he adjusted to his new team.\n“I’m gonna be real, when I joined Paper Rex, since I’m the new guy. I went [through] a depressing phase. I always show that I’m happy. I’m nice with the team with a camera and on stream, but during practice, I know, like I underperform a lot,” he said in a post-match interview.\nBut his team was there to support him throughout.\n“My teammates, my coach, they always encourage me. It’s not like they did something to me that’s why I’m down, but because sometimes I’m being hard on myself, you know but I’m so happy that I have teammates like this. They always encourage me, especially Alecks. They don’t give up on me. They say I deserve to be here,” PatMen shared.\nSubscribe to INQUIRER PLUS to get access to The Philippine Daily Inquirer & other 70+ titles, share up to 5 gadgets, listen to the news, download as early as 4am & share articles on social media. Call 896 6000.",
            "content_length": 2798
        },
        {
            "article_id": "a24b2830258b326b",
            "title": "Paper Rex Triumphs as 2025 Valorant Masters Toronto Champions - The Chronicle PH",
            "published_at": "Wed, 25 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "The Chronicle PH",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMipwFBVV95cUxNV1REai1uT1ozVkxyMXVqTkRtOUp1WlZMUHJnZjhrbkVNaE9EN2M1MTNiaVRmdXdHM1huVmlPRHNBdGtPbFF2VlRUaXAtbHlhcUR4MEx0UHZlbERDRzMwNmRpOXM4ZVVCV0xEbnRUY2l1TTZqRUk3VjNDVXo0RWNTck1na3c4eVhOR0NTcUJvTzhJOEFXRXlHWEhBbEFNdkp4R0g1SUcwVQ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://thechronicle.com.ph/third-times-the-charm-paper-rex-finally-takes-home-the-trophy-and-the-glory/",
            "query_players": [
                "f0rsakeN"
