            "source": "VPEsports",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMickFVX3lxTFB0eDAxdDFaMWxSUkZSeXZMMzc1cWQwVU9Od2EtbXgycUl1WG5kWHVHa25seDZLYjdRenVHWFBRclVHNEJkNTJLZnpZTzlGX2NmQnkzMW8yOGFVb01IYnp5dzA1SG5DZGVmS1JLZ3IwNGxLZw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://vpesports.com/more-esports/aspas-contract-extension-mibr",
            "query_players": [
                "aspas"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "aspas",
                "cortezia",
                "Mazino",
                "Verno",
                "YOU",
                "zekken"
            ],
            "content": "Brazilian club MIBR has secured a long—term deal with its main duelist, Eric aspas Santos. The contract was extended until the 2027 finals, and this immediately led the community to discuss how such a move would affect the entire Valorant Champions Tour.\nTable of Contents\nAspas is the same player who has long been called the “final boss” in the regional Valorant. Now he has become the first confirmed participant of the VCT 2026 roster, which is another signal that the team is building a foundation in advance.\nOn social media, the club briefly wrote: “We’re not done yet, this story continues.” And honestly, it feels like it is.\nWhen aspas joined in November 2024, the changes came almost without delay. The player helped boost the effectiveness of the roster so that the team closed VCT 2025 Americas Kickoff and Stage 1 with two bronze medals. It was a rare chance for MIBR to make a name for itself right at the start of the season.\nFurther — more. Local achievements brought the club to their first Valorant Champions event. And already in Paris, MIBR managed to enter the top five finish, which became a serious statement of intent for the organization. Aspas himself turned out to be the highest rated player of the entire tournament — the numbers spoke louder than any Twitter threads.\nThe awards were not long in coming either: for the third consecutive season, he received the titles of Player of the Year and Duelist of the Year in VCT Americas. If you add to this the victory at Champions 2022 as part of LOUD, where he won the MVP, and two regional titles, you get a portrait of one of the most productive players in the region.\nMIBR’s stability makes building a team around their star duelist a smart move. They’re planning their 2026 VCT roster with the goal of killing it in their region and competing internationally, where things get tougher each year.\nThe club has not yet revealed the full list of players, but the first details have already appeared in Sheep Esports reports. According to insiders, the organization is going to retain the young IGL and initiator, Andrew “Verno” Maust, who has performed well in previous stages.\nThere were also rumors in the press about two possible additions. The first is Zachary “zekken” Patrone, a former duelist from the Sentinels. The second is Roberto “Mazino” Rivas, who previously played for KRÜ Esports. If these transitions are officially confirmed, the roster will become much more flexible and deeper in roles.\nMIBR has already been informed about the transfer of Gabriel ‘cortezia’ Cortez to the Sentinels in November, so the restructuring of the squad is moving forward. Judging by the team’s activity, new announcements may arrive literally soon — fans are already vigilantly following every hint on social networks.",
            "content_length": 2792
        },
