            "article_id": "1a350630a6e3ad38",
            "title": "VALORANT Masters Toronto Kicks Off June 7 with 12 Top Regional Teams - Inven Global",
            "published_at": "Thu, 29 May 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Inven Global",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisAFBVV95cUxNX3RkY01hd1BCRUZCZGh5dnBDSjBySHkxY2ZrME1lTGRIU2t2X3hQdmFLTzh5NXEtQXpiSFpVODFjby1YUko3N19fWXNLVEtpdmlYNG1VVDNmMVNRWEtFdHNibVEtV0tINEtqTUE4NnQ4UURJVGEzRzZsQkY1WXlRRTNLbjFzeFpHOVJyMjN3MDBaa2E3eGIwQmh2UzVUWFVhV09EYV8zb0VSR0ZCN001OQ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.invenglobal.com/articles/19355/valorant-masters-toronto-kicks-off-june-7-with-12-top-regional-teams",
            "query_players": [
                "Kicks"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Kicks"
            ],
            "content": "Riot Games has finalized the field for VALORANT Masters Toronto, set to run June 7–22 at the Enercare Centre in Toronto. Twelve teams representing four global regions will vie for a share of the sizable prize pool and valuable Championship Points over the three-week event.\nThe competition brings together the top three squads from Stage 1 of each international league: VCT Pacific, VCT China, VCT Americas and VCT EMEA. Pacific league champion XERXIA Regnum Qeon, China’s XLG Esports, Americas titleholder G2 Esports and EMEA winner Fnatic earn byes into the Playoffs. The remaining eight teams begin in the Swiss Stage on June 7, where squads must secure two wins to advance or suffer elimination after two defeats.\nVCT Pacific will also send Gen.G and Paper Rex into the Swiss bracket after they finished second and third in the regional finals. From VCT China, Bilibili Gaming and Wolves Esports join XLG Esports after Edward Gaming fell short of a top-three finish. VCT Americas is represented by Sentinels and Made in Brazil alongside G2, while Team Heretics and Team Liquid fill the second and third seeds from VCT EMEA.\nThe Swiss Stage features matchups between teams with identical records, narrowing the field to four survivors who will face the four direct Playoff qualifiers. Playoffs commence June 13 under a double-elimination format, with best-of-three matches until the Lower Bracket Final and Grand Final, both best-of-five, conclude the tournament on June 22.\nChampionship Points likewise reward the top finishers, with the winner earning seven points, five for second and four for third. Remaining prize money and points are distributed based on final standings, ensuring every team competes for both financial and seasonal stakes.\n- \n \n I write. I rap. I run. That’s pretty much it. \nSort by:\nComments :0",
            "content_length": 1824
        },
        {
            "article_id": "3f71ad98988a401b",
            "title": "Team Liquid nAts could miss Valorant Masters Toronto - Esports News UK",
            "published_at": "Thu, 29 May 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports News UK",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihwFBVV95cUxNWFhMUFEtSGZNaS1ERjRYV1lTYkFpLWN3Z0dkUzBORHVwNDFqdnM3bXMzblVQTXBTeEJYUzl4UXZaZEQ5LV9pS2piNHdvcGo0QnA4V0JGNlVHcG16UHlqNi01cnpEZ1BIMENPV1FTR3EyR2FtZW95VmJleXJMcUF5SVBkN3o4LTQ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports-news.co.uk/2025/05/30/team-liquid-nats-valorant-masters-toronto/",
            "query_players": [
                "nAts"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Alfajer",
                "Chronicle",
                "nAts",
                "penny",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Team Liquid’s in-game leader Ayaz ‘nAts’ Akhmetshin will miss out on at least the opening match of Valorant Masters Toronto due to visa issues.\nMasters Toronto is one of the largest Valorant events of the year, with 12 of the best teams from around the world.\nThey’ll compete for a significant portion of the $1m (£742k) prize pool and crucial circuit points towards qualifying for the season’s grand finale, Valorant Champions 2025.\nThe Russian pro has been unable to fly over to Canada with his teammates, who will be training hard ahead of their first game against China’s second seeds, Bilibili Gaming, on Saturday, June 7th.\nHowever, nAts and Team Liquid are hopeful he will be able to join back up with the team later in the tournament.\nnAts reassured his social media followers: “There is still a chance that I will get visa but most likely not before the first match. I hope I will be able to show up to the tournament.”\nHowever, he may not have a lot of time as Masters begins with a Swiss Stage in which two defeats will spell elimination from the event and Liquid were already going into the event as underdogs as EMEA’s third seeds.\nWho will replace nAts at Valorant Masters Toronto 2025?\nUntil the visa is sorted, nAts will be replaced by Canadian pro Erik ‘penny’ Penny, who will get to play in front of a home crowd.\nThe 22-year-old previously impressed with Version1 and G2 Esports, but most recently helped his team YFP finish fourth in Valorant Challengers North America Stage 2.\nHe is well known for his Jett play but this year has also played a lot of Yoru and Neon.\nIt’s a good opportunity for Penny who tweeted about his excitement following the announcement:\n“I will be playing with Liquid at Masters Toronto. Excited to play in front of the home crowd and I will make sure to do my best for Liquid fans everywhere.”\nMeanwhile, fans of the VCT EMEA Stage 1 champions Fnatic, worried that their Russian pro Timofey ‘Chronicle’ Khromov could also face the same problems.\nBut Fnatic team director Colin ‘CoJo’ Johnson quickly reassured everyone on X (formerly Twitter), announcing: “Just in case this worries anyone re: Chron, he has his visa as well as Alfajer so we are all good.”\nHe also revealed that teams started the visa application process on February 20th with Fnatic receiving theirs by April 16th.\nJack Stewart, Senior Editor\nIn my seven years of esports writing, I've introduced esports coverage to newspapers, interviewed some of the biggest names in the industry, and driven viewers mad with the puns in my YouTube scripts. I'm most proud of the latter.\nFrom breaking news and in-depth match analysis to exclusive interviews and behind-the-scenes content, we bring you the stories that shape the esports scene in the UK.\nMonthly Visitors\nEsports Covered\nYears experience",
            "content_length": 2804
        },
