                "Chronicle",
                "Derke",
                "Jinggg",
                "TenZ",
                "yay"
            ],
            "content": "Top 10 Best Valorant Players Around the World\nValorant, developed by Riot Games, has quickly become one of the biggest names in the esports industry. The tactical shooter combines traditional FPS mechanics with unique agent abilities, making every match unpredictable and exciting. To excel in this competitive environment, players must master shooting accuracy, reflexes, positioning, and most importantly, teamwork. This is why the Best Valorant Players Around the World are celebrated, they have mastered every aspect of the game and set new standards for skill and strategy. The future of Valorant looks incredibly bright as it continues to expand globally with million-dollar tournaments, brand sponsorships, and massive online viewership. The Best Valorant Players Around the World are not just skilled individuals but also role models for aspiring gamers, showing what dedication and passion can achieve. Their ability to perform under pressure and deliver highlight-worthy plays has made them superstars in the esports scene.\nLet’s see the Top 10 Best Valorant Players Around the World:\n10. Marved\nJimmy “Marved” Nguyen is widely recognized as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World when it comes to support roles. Known for his ability to control the pace of a match, Marved excels at healing teammates, placing walls strategically, and providing clutch revives that often change the outcome of rounds. His calmness in high-pressure situations allows his team to survive and regroup when it matters most.\nWhat truly makes Marved stand out as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World is his unselfish style of play. While duelists usually get the spotlight for their flashy kills, Marved works silently in the background, creating opportunities and ensuring the team has a strong foundation. His clutch utility usage has earned him respect from teammates and fans alike, proving that support players can be game-changers.\n9. Sacy\nGustavo “Sacy” Rossi is another name that belongs among the Best Valorant Players Around the World. Known for his excellent Viper plays, Sacy controls entire maps with toxic walls and poison clouds, shutting down enemy advances with precision. His setups are carefully designed to force opponents into uncomfortable positions, making his team’s defense nearly unbreakable.\nSacy is celebrated as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World because of his sharp tactical mind. He can predict enemy pushes before they happen, giving his team an edge in preparation and strategy. His ability to suffocate opponents and turn sites into fortresses makes him invaluable for competitive play, and a nightmare for anyone trying to break through.\n8. Jinggg\nWang “Jinggg” Jing Jie has quickly made a name for himself as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World with his high-energy duelist performances. Famous for his explosive Jett plays, Jinggg thrives in aggressive scenarios, taking risky fights that often pay off in style. His quick reflexes and ability to escape danger make him a constant threat to opponents.\nJinggg’s bold and fearless approach to matches has solidified his status among the Best Valorant Players Around the World. His daring style inspires fans and upcoming duelists to take initiative and create pressure on opponents. By combining raw speed with confidence, Jinggg has changed the way people think about Jett gameplay.\n7. BcJ\nBrendan “BcJ” Jensen earns his place among the Best Valorant Players Around the World with his outstanding Astra gameplay. His Gravity Wells, Nova Pulses, and other abilities are perfectly timed to trap opponents and create easy kills for his teammates. With his wide control of the map, BcJ often dictates the tempo of games.\nAs one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World, BcJ is admired for his creativity. Instead of using Astra only for defense, he blends offensive and defensive strategies, confusing opponents and keeping them guessing. His ability to shut down enemy movements and control vital areas of the map shows his importance in competitive Valorant.\n6. TenZ\nTyson “TenZ” Ngo is easily one of the most famous names on the list of the Best Valorant Players Around the World. A former CS:GO professional, TenZ became a Valorant superstar thanks to his unmatched aim and gun control. Whether playing Jett, Reyna, or Phoenix, TenZ always delivers performances that are nothing short of breathtaking.\nHis reputation as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World comes from his consistency and confidence. TenZ thrives in aggressive roles, often taking risks that lead to highlight-worthy plays. His ability to handle pressure while producing magical moments makes him a role model for aspiring duelists everywhere.\n5. Boaster\nJake “Boaster” Howlett has established himself as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World through his exceptional leadership. As the in-game leader for Fnatic, Boaster has guided his team to numerous victories by crafting smart strategies and making precise calls. His experience and adaptability make him one of the most reliable captains in Valorant.\nWhat makes Boaster one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World is not only his tactics but also his personality. He inspires teammates with his calmness and confidence, even in stressful situations. Boaster shows that leadership, discipline, and positivity can be just as important as mechanical skills in achieving success.\n4. Derke\nNikita “Derke” Sirmitev is one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World thanks to his deadly precision and mastery of Sova. His recon arrows provide vital information for his team, while his Operator shots regularly eliminate enemies before they even get a chance to fire back. His plays often decide the outcome of entire rounds.\nDerke is respected among the Best Valorant Players Around the World because of his intelligence and consistency. His unique ability to mix utility with sharpshooting gives his team the edge in both offense and defense. For fans and aspiring players, Derke’s Sova gameplay has become the ultimate standard to follow.\n3. Chronicle\nTimofey “Chronicle” Khromov deserves his place among the Best Valorant Players Around the World due to his versatility. Capable of playing agents like Kay/O and Sova, Chronicle can adapt to his team’s needs with ease. His ability to switch roles without losing effectiveness makes him a rare and valuable asset.\nHe is regarded as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World because of his tactical brilliance and mechanical skill. Chronicle reads the game like few others, predicting enemy movements and shutting down strategies. His flexibility and reliability are what separate him from most players in the competitive scene.\n2. ardiis\nArdis “ardiis” Svarenieks is another former CS:GO pro who has proven himself as one of the Best Valorant Players Around the World. His Jett plays are legendary, mixing sharp movement with flawless shooting. He consistently pulls off incredible kills that leave opponents frustrated and spectators amazed.\nArdiis ranks among the Best Valorant Players Around the World because of his confidence and bold decision-making. His risk-taking often secures key rounds for his team, while his energy inspires teammates to push harder. Watching ardiis in action is always entertaining, as he thrives under the spotlight.\n1. yay\nAt the top of the list of the Best Valorant Players Around the World is Jaccob “yay” Whiteaker. Known as the GOAT of Valorant, yay dominates with Reyna and Jett, consistently topping the scoreboard and striking fear into his opponents. His ability to clutch rounds and deliver impossible plays has made him the face of competitive Valorant.\nYay’s status as the best among the Best Valorant Players Around the World is not just about stats, it’s also about influence. He inspires both his teammates and fans with his confidence, skill, and composure. For anyone who follows Valorant, yay is the ultimate role model, showing what it means to truly master the game.",
            "content_length": 8059
        },
        {
            "article_id": "a5886efc5a0d8a9f",
            "title": "Game Changers Championship: Americas Teams Make Up Final 4 After Eliminating G2 And KC - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Thu, 27 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMie0FVX3lxTE1PQy0yb2haOVpZbVpQTVB2dkNYWXBESUN6TG9wYko3RjlCQ2FzNEJxUzhqU3ExeHlSelNtTzZvZDRmOHdlbUM5S0JoWk9DY2VSWGFwUTBIQXlUdnBTcWZYLS1SZzNFeGs2RkJxR1A1SFlxdERkaGpFU3VwZw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/game-changers-championship-day-7",
            "query_players": [
                "Cloud"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Cloud",
                "ComeBack"
            ],
            "content": "Day seven of the VALORANT Game Changers Championship in Seoul produced some of the most dramatic elimination matches of the tournament so far, with G2 Gozen, MIBR GC, Karmine Corp GC, and KRÜ Blaze all fighting to stay alive in the lower bracket. By the end of a tense afternoon, both EMEA representatives were knocked out of the event, while two LATAM/BR squads moved one step closer to the grand final.\nThe elimination match between MIBR GC and G2 Gozen began under a cloud of controversy. The previous evening, following a loss to Team Liquid, G2 controller player Amy made a post on X in reaction to the match, ending by saying that the team will ‘go again MIBR tomorrow’, followed by an orangutan emoji. The imagery was widely considered racist within the Brazilian community and received immense backlash, which continued after she quickly removed the post and issued a follow-up, stating she was unaware of the cultural context.\nFollowing the conclusion of the series against MIBR, she has since posted again, this time apologising for the emoji use and reaffirming that she ‘did i know about the history and culture to brazillian people’, and that she had previously used the emoji amongst friends as a ‘chill emoji.’ The tension surrounding the matchup was impossible to ignore, giving the series an added emotional edge.\nMIBR chose Bind to start the series and opened on attack with immediate confidence, winning the pistol and establishing a 4–1 lead. Their duelist duo of bstrdd and sRN surged early, consistently finding space and entries to push MIBR into an 8–4 halftime advantage. The Brazilian side extended their lead to 10–4 after picking up the second pistol as well.\nHowever, once G2 moved to attack, their controller pairing of Vania and Amy on Brim and Viper respectively transformed the momentum of the map. Their consistent frags and late-round composure brought G2 back from the brink, round by round, until the European side reached map point at 12–10. MIBR refused to break, clawing back the final two rounds of regulation and carrying that energy into overtime, where they prevailed 14–12. Despite the loss, Vania posted the highest ACS on the server, reflecting how hard she worked to keep G2 alive.\nThe series moved next to Sunset, one of G2’s strongest maps of the year and a staple of their playbook. They began on defense and immediately looked far more comfortable, taking a 4–1 lead as Vania, Amy, and Petra shut down nearly every angle of MIBR’s approach. Their defensive reads were nearly flawless, and the half ended with a commanding 10–2 advantage.\nAlthough MIBR claimed the second pistol, G2 stole the anti-eco with firm discipline, stripping away the Brazilians’ hopes of a comeback. The final round of the map ended with an ace from Vania, who finished the map with 26 kills, a +15 differential, and a staggering 391 ACS on Omen. G2 closed out the map 13–5 and sent the series to a decider.\nJust as in their upper-bracket loss to Team Liquid, Split once again became the site of heartbreak for G2. They started with an excellent defensive half, winning the opening four rounds cleanly, surrendering one, and then reasserting their dominance to end the half up 10–2. It was one of G2’s strongest halves of the entire tournament.\nMIBR won both pistol and anti-eco after the swap, but G2 answered with the bonus to reach 11–4. What followed was a near-perfect comeback attempt from MIBR. Round by round, supported by clutch plays and unshakeable confidence, they dragged the score back to 11–8. A critical Showstopper 3K from Akita gave G2 a thrifty win and put them at match point, but MIBR still refused to fade into the night. They won the next rounds through sheer resilience, even clutching a 2v4 at one point to force overtime.\nOnce extra rounds began, MIBR were the team in control. The Brazilian squad closed out the map 14–12 and completed one of the most dramatic comebacks of the tournament. Bstrdd delivered a star performance with 29 kills on Split, securing multikill rounds throughout the comeback. This sets a new comeback record in GC history as well.\nAcross the full series, Vania was G2’s standout player with 68 kills and a 287 ACS from controller, but the day belonged to bstrdd, who finished the match with an ACS of 267, 11 first kills, and a +17 kill differential. With this loss, G2 Gozen exited the Championship in 5th–6th place, once again at the hands of MIBR similar to last year.\nThe day’s second elimination match pitted Karmine Corp against KRÜ Blaze, both coming off decisive victories the previous day. KRÜ have qualified for every Game Changers Championship to date, and they once again showed they were unwilling to exit early, delivering a dominant performance to eliminate the last remaining EMEA team.\nKRÜ opened with Split, opting for a Sage–Viper–Raze–Omen–Skye composition that gave them great map control. KC answered with Tejo and Waylay substitutes for Sage and Raze, but the LATAM representatives looked far sharper from the outset. KC won the opening pistol, but KRÜ immediately took the anti-eco and rode the momentum to a 4–1 lead.\nKC recovered briefly, taking four of the next five rounds, but KRÜ finished the half ahead 7–5. After the sideswap, KRÜ’s defensive setups were suffocating. They won round after round en route to a 10–5 advantage, and although KC picked up one more, KRÜ closed out the map 13–6. Dods, Conir, and rookie mmonch all surpassed 230 ACS, while KC’s best performer was Jiex with 240 ACS on Omen.\nKarmine Corp chose Haven, a map they had used just the day prior to defeat global champions SRG. But KRÜ immediately dismantled any expectations of a KC bounceback. Starting on defense, they claimed the pistol and the next five rounds, showing impeccable layering between their Waylay aggression and their Killjoy–Viper site anchors.\nKC found only two rounds in the half—one of which came from an explosive Neon ultimate from Alkyia—but KRÜ still entered halftime with a 9–3 lead. Without a pistol win to begin the second half, there was no window for KC to recover, and KRÜ stormed through the remaining rounds to take the map 13–3 and the series 2–0.\nConir provided veteran stability, while Dods emerged as the frag leader for KRÜ, ending the series with 34 kills, five opening picks, and 249 ACS. This is the LATAM team’s best placement at a GCC yet, after failing to break the 5th–6th barrier for years.\nAfter the match, KC’s in-game leader Jiex spoke candidly about the need for greater support within the Game Changers ecosystem. She emphasized that despite the high level of skill and strategic depth displayed on stage, organizations still hesitate to invest due to lower prize pools and inconsistent viewership. “Every day, we showcase our talent and gameplay. We deserve the same respect as the men’s scene,” she said. “I want to compete more than anything, so I ask Riot to invest in the inclusive scene—we’ll make this investment worth it.”\nWith today’s results, the final two EMEA teams, G2 Gozen and Karmine Corp, have officially been eliminated, and the top four now consists entirely of Americas-region squads. Tomorrow will determine the first grand finalist as Team Liquid Brazil face Shopify Rebellion Gold in the upper-bracket final. In the lower bracket, KRÜ Blaze and MIBR GC will compete for the right to play in Saturday’s lower final and keep their championship dreams alive.",
            "content_length": 7412
        },
        {
