            "article_id": "addec49cbf901ff4",
            "title": "The golden era of collegiate esports begins: DLSU-D’s biggest convention with Charess and PatMen - Cebu Daily News",
            "published_at": "Thu, 13 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Cebu Daily News",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://cebudailynews.inquirer.net/669658/the-golden-era-of-collegiate-esports-begins-dlsu-ds-biggest-convention-with-charess-and-patmen",
            "query_players": [
                "PatMen"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "PatMen",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "The golden era of collegiate esports begins: DLSU-D’s biggest convention with Charess and PatMen\nWhat began as a small university esports project has steadily grown into one of De La Salle University-Dasmariñas’ most anticipated student events. The College of Information and Computer Studies Student Government (CICSSG) is set to host Chasing Victory Golden: The National Esports Tournament and Gaming Convention on November 18. Now on its third installment, the annual Chasing Victory series has become a tradition organized by CICSSG to celebrate gaming, creativity, and community.\nThis year’s edition has taken both students and fans by surprise with its expanded scale and lineup. Two well-known figures in the cosplay and gaming scene, Charess and PatMen of Paper Rex, have been announced as special guests, giving the student-organized event an even greater sense of excitement and anticipation.For a University-led event, having guests of this level is an unexpected honor. Chasing Victory Golden will mark Charess’s first time accepting an invitation from a university organization, a moment that brings excitement and pride to the student team behind the project. As one of the country’s most recognized figures in the cosplay and content creation community, her participation reflects her openness to support student-led initiatives and the growing enthusiasm for collegiate esports.\nAlso joining the lineup is PatMen (Patrick “PatMen” Mendoza), professional player and member of Paper Rex, who will hold his first Philippine fan meet at the event. PatMen broke into the global VALORANT Champions Tour spotlight by signing with Paper Rex in early 2025 and becoming the first pure Filipino player to win both a regional VCT title and the international Masters Toronto championship together with the team.\nFor the CICSSG, the announcement is more than a highlight. It represents months of work, planning, and collaboration from a team of students who envisioned an event that connects the academic community with the professional gaming industry. What was once a modest college tournament has now evolved into a national esports and gaming convention, supported by major brands and recognized personalities.\nSet to take place at Ugnayang La Salle, Chasing Victory Golden will feature esports tournaments for Valorant and Mobile Legends, a cosplay competition, a guest alley, gaming booths, food stalls, exhibitors, and a merchandise showcase. The program will also include live performances, interactive segments, and special guest appearances designed to engage both gamers and casual fans alike. It will serve as the grand finale of a nine-day series of online tournament preliminaries streamed through Facebook Live and Discord, culminating in the much-anticipated onsite convention.\nThe event lineup includes well-known gaming and cosplay figures such as Asurai, Rebyczko, John Weak, Haru Nadine, and Sunny Rose, all of whom are expected to join the festivities and interact with fans. Musical performances by Mahiyaing Mananayaw, Jergens Del Rosario, EL1XIR, and ICON will keep the energy high throughout the day, blending esports, entertainment, and creativity into one massive celebration of student-driven innovation.\nGaming booths will feature a variety of titles, including Valorant, Tekken 8, Street Fighter 6, 2XKO, Tekken 7, Guilty Gear Strive, Dragon Ball Fighter Z, X-Men vs Street Fighter, Street Fighter II, Mortal Kombat 3, The King of Fighters 98, SVC Chaos, Capcom vs SNK 2, AR Catcher, Beyblade, and Sim Racing. These attractions aim to immerse attendees in both competitive and casual gaming experiences, while a 360 glambot and photobooth will capture the excitement of every moment.\nSupported by Lenovo Legion, Mountain Dew, AcadArena Education, Motivo Philippines, and Aseprite, among other partners, Chasing Victory Golden demonstrates the strength of collaboration between academia, industry, and community. The partnerships reflect a growing recognition of esports as a legitimate and influential space where students can showcase leadership, creativity, and technical skill.\nThe goal of Chasing Victory Golden is not only to entertain but also to inspire. The event aims to show that large-scale projects can emerge from student initiative when combined with teamwork and dedication. By hosting both online and onsite components, the CICSSG hopes to reach participants from across the Philippines, promoting inclusivity and engagement within the gaming and creative communities.\nWhat makes Chasing Victory Golden truly stand out is its origin. Every element, from logistics and marketing to partnerships and production, was developed and executed by students. The success of securing prominent guests and major sponsors has shown what can be achieved through collective effort and vision. It serves as proof that creativity, determination, and collaboration can lead to outcomes that surpass expectations.\nPurchase tickets: https://tinyurl.com/2dv98fm5\nAccess event FAQs here: www.tinyurl.com/CVGFAQs\nFor more information and event updates, visit the CICS Student Government Facebook page: https://www.facebook.com/DLSUD.CICSSG\nThis article is brought to you by DLSUD CICSSG.\nDisclaimer: The comments uploaded on this site do not necessarily represent or reflect the views of management and owner of Cebudailynews. We reserve the right to exclude comments that we deem to be inconsistent with our editorial standards.",
            "content_length": 5455
        },
        {
            "article_id": "da501462d525cb2d",
            "title": "FNC crashies: “I had interest in NA and EU, I just think re-signing was the best decision for my career” - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Thu, 13 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMibkFVX3lxTE54Q0t2c3NHdjJESXlpWTZfQ0xFSTEwNkFxQjVBMHFoYjRtSFZabmlLd1pBSnhESjVWOW1YclNyYi1TNmZHbEZQOUplTVdIaENaN29LM2xocXA1SjZUN2tFWVN4aG1zaEFwYXFvWWRR?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/fnc-crashies-interview",
            "query_players": [
                "crashies"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Boaster",
                "Chronicle",
                "crashies",
                "Derke",
                "kaajak",
                "Less",
                "s0m",
                "something",
                "Veqaj",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "When crashies joined Fnatic for the 2025 season, it appeared to be a stopgap as the organisation awaited Leo’s hopeful return from illness. But with the American eventually playing all the way through the year, including in their historic Champs run, fans urged Fnatic to keep the former NRG player on. Their wishes were eventually granted, as crashies re-signed with the Black and Orange for another year. Ahead of his team’s appearance at Red Bull Home Ground, Hotspawn was able to speak to crashies about his stay on Fnatic, looking ahead to 2026.\nHotspawn: During the off season, the consensus was that you’d be returning to NA for personal reasons, having only expected your stay in EMEA to be temporary. But eventually you did end up sticking with Fnatic.\nCrashies: Going into the off season, I just kind of took a lot of time to reflect and really decide what I want in my career and what I want to do. Obviously, with our loss at Champs, it was a little bit of an emotional time for me, so I really tried to not bring too many emotions into it and just purely base my decision on what I think is best for me and my career going forward.\nObviously I had interest in NA and Europe as well, so I just really, really, thought deeply [about] what I really wanted. And Fnatic was able to give me a good renewal contract while also keeping me in the loop with roster decisions and what they decide to do going into next year. I just think it was the best decision for my career.\nAnd only time will tell, right? But talking about it now, I’m not going to regret my decision or anything because I think the year that we spent together was very lovely, and it flew by. And I think when you find a team as special as we were and as close as we were, I think it would be kind of a shame just to throw that away just for one year.\nHotspawn: Speaking of roster decisions, Chronicle eventually left for Team Vitality and has been replaced by Veqaj.\nCrashies: He’s a very lovely guy. It’s always nice to get to know different players and stuff, especially for me being an NA player; I don’t really know many European players, especially on an individual level. So getting to meet new people and different cultures, it’s always special.\nBut getting to hang out with him and meeting him for the first time, which was our London clash which we just did with Fnatic, it was just very special, and I think he has a very good head on his shoulders. I’m very excited just to get started with him and play the season and everything.\nHotspawn: With Chronicle leaving as one of the all-time Valorant greats, it’d be normal for people to expect less from Fnatic in 2026.\nCrashies: Honestly, I love when people underestimate us and don’t give us expectations and stuff. It makes our jobs and lives so much easier because we just play. I said this to Veqaj and the team; he’s in a very similar situation that I was in joining Fnatic at the time where people were saying I can’t replace Leo, and it’s impossible to make finals and win stuff. And Fnatic just proved they have a very good structure.\nAnd Boaster, this is kind of the GOAT IGL and can do it with many different players. So I think going to this year, like, obviously we’re just going to focus on ourselves and focus on what we know we can do to succeed.\nAnd I think it definitely helps that, [as] I said, Veqaj is in a very similar situation as I was — and kaajak, for example, when Derke was also going to Vitality. I think we can just kind of guide him and give him some tips and knowledge on how to deal with expectations and pressure and stuff.\nincase you missed it yesterday…\nboaster is a raze main now pic.twitter.com/89vL28KFst\n— FNATIC (@FNATIC) November 10, 2025\nHotspawn: Speaking of Boaster, he gave everyone a bit of a surprise with his Raze performance against GIANTX the other day. That said, he’s a player who often faces criticism for his in-game performance, hampered by his focus on his role as an IGL.\nCrashies: Honestly, it was pretty funny, because when he said he was gonna lock in Raze, I’m like, ‘Oh God, here we go’. I didn’t seem to know how it would go, but once I saw him playing and satcheling — I think the hardest thing with Raze is just getting the movement down, and he was moving on that agent and I was pretty impressed.\nAnd not only was he moving, he was shooting. And honestly, I think the 300 ACS got to his head a little bit. He’s moving a little different these days. He’s overconfident. So I think it was a good thing to happen. I think he enjoyed it a lot, and I think a lot of people enjoyed it as well. So I think it was just overall just a very enjoyable moment.\nHotspawn: Anything else interesting for New York?\nCrashies: I don’t know. We’ll have to see, we’ll have to see. Probably not on the Raze, but maybe some other things. Maybe a Veto game or something in there for him.\nHotspawn: When we spoke in Paris, you talked about being in close contact with s0m, your former NRG teammates. He recently announced his break from pro play, expecting to return at some point in the future.\nCrashies: I talked to him a little bit at the after party, and he wasn’t 100%. He was very unsure. And I think as soon as you’re unsure on if you want to keep playing or not, I think it was the right move for him to step down. Especially because you have four other teammates, and coaches, and there’s so many people involved that it wouldn’t really be fair to everyone involved to not give 100%.\nAnd he won the biggest trophy that there is to win, so he kind of beat the game in a sense, and he already has such a big stream and a big brand and personality outside of that. So I think for him it was probably the right move.\nAlthough I’ve talked to him a lot of times and told him, like, ‘you can’t compete forever’ and ‘you shouldn’t really take your skill for granted’ and, like, ‘streaming will always be there’ and stuff like that. So it definitely like was a little sad to see him retire in that sense.\nBut I think there is a world where he’ll probably come back depending on what happens. But I don’t know, I wouldn’t be surprised that he’s just chilling now with even a bigger stream now and just watch partying and enjoying those vibes.\nHotspawn: Is that your plan, to keep competing as long as you can?\nCrashies: My plan is for sure just keep playing as long as I can. I think I’m a very self-aware human, where I kind of know when my time will be up. But I think I still have a lot to give, and I still have a lot of passion and hunger to win.\nAnd I want to win with this team because I feel like we all have such a very special connection where I want to lift a trophy with them and feel the emotions and everything. Even though we won a regional trophy, I just want to win another international because the last time I actually won an international, there wasn’t a crowd in the venue. So I don’t know what winning with a full crowd would be like.\nSo I think just, to answer your question on a short basis, yeah, I just want to keep playing for as long as I can, and then after that I’ll probably just turn into full content like streaming. Watch partying with FNS, s0m, all those guys because we have so much history and stuff together.",
            "content_length": 7221
        },
        {
            "article_id": "c3970d8b2aa954f3",
            "title": "Valorant Mobile: Release timeline, features, gameplay leaks, and what to expect in 2025 - HT Tech",
            "published_at": "Thu, 13 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "HT Tech",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://tech.hindustantimes.com/gaming/news/valorant-mobile-release-timeline-features-gameplay-leaks-and-what-to-expect-in-2025-71739449519759.html",
            "query_players": [
                "BuZz"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "BuZz",
                "Suggest"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant Mobile: Release timeline, features, gameplay leaks, and what to expect in 2025\nValorant's mobile version is generating buzz as leaks reveal possible release details, gameplay features, and beta testing. Here’s what we know so far.\nValorant, the popular tactical shooter from Riot Games, has quickly become a favourite among PC and console gamers. The game's success has led to speculation that a mobile version might be in the works. Although there is no official confirmation yet, leaks have hinted that Valorant could make its mobile debut soon, possibly within the next year.\nExpected Release Timeline for Valorant Mobile\nWhile Riot Games has not officially confirmed the release date, reports suggest that Valorant Mobile may launch in the fourth quarter of 2025. Earlier reports had indicated a late 2024 release, but it seems that the game may face delays. At this time, no details are available regarding its availability in India, though the mobile version’s arrival is anticipated globally.\nValorant Mobile Beta and Testing Leaks\nLeaked information suggests that the developers are focused on delivering a smooth experience similar to the PC version. Riot Games reportedly invited gamers in China for closed beta testing, where they could experience the game’s gameplay, agents, skins, and weapons. These leaks indicate that the mobile version aims to meet high standards before its official release, with attention to detail and gameplay quality.\nFeatures and Gameplay of Valorant Mobile\nValorant Mobile is expected to closely resemble its PC counterpart, retaining familiar skins, weapons, and maps. However, it will feature shorter match durations to accommodate the mobile format. The mobile version may also introduce new features like in-game replays, which are not yet available for PC players. A deathmatch mode is also expected, adding more variety to the gameplay.\nAlso read: 5 PC games to play in 2025: From Warframe, and Baldur's Gate 3 to Black Myth: Wukong and more\nHowever, it’s important to note that Valorant Mobile will not support cross-play with the PC version, meaning mobile players will only compete against others on mobile devices.\nWhile many details about the game’s features, release date, and gameplay remain unclear, the information available so far is based on leaks and rumours. Riot Games has not yet made any official announcements.\nCatch all the Latest Tech News, Mobile News, Laptop News, Gaming news, Wearables News , How To News, also keep up with us on Whatsapp channel,Twitter, Facebook, Google News, and Instagram. For our latest videos, subscribe to our YouTube channel.",
            "content_length": 2629
        },
