            "content": "Cebuano Valorant pro 2GE on VCT: ‘I carry that pride with me’\nLAPU-LAPU CITY, Philippines — Ever since the franchised league for the Valorant esports ecosystem began in 2023, James “2GE” Goopio has been competing at the highest level of Valorant with Filipino-laden rosters.\n2GE was with Rex Regum Qeon (RRQ) — an Indonesian esports organization — when it was accepted into the Pacific franchises for the Valorant Champions Tour (VCT) Pacific’s inaugural season in 2023.\nAt RRQ, he played alongside fellow Filipinos Eroll Jule “EJAY” Delfin and Emmanuel “Emman” Morales, as well as the Indonesian trio of David “xffero” Monangin, Saibani “fl1pzjder” Rahmad, and Hagai “Lmemore” Tewuh.\nHaving made it into the big leagues, 2GE remains the sole Cebuano to have reached the franchise league thus far.\nREAD: Team Secret drops Cebuano Valorant pro 2GE from roster\n“I feel incredibly honored and proud to represent Cebu on one of the biggest stages in Valorant,” 2GE shared in an exclusive interview with CDN Digital.\n“Coming from the province and making it this far just proves that no matter where you’re from, with enough hard work and dedication, anything is possible,” 2GE continued.\nBefore franchising, 2GE played with Cebuano rosters during his time with Zeal Esports and the Talisay-based organization 6045 Pirates.\n“I carry that pride with me every time I compete,” 2GE shared when asked about what it was like being the lone Cebuano player to have made it into the franchise league.\nIn 2024, 2GE left the Indonesian organization to join his Filipino brethren in Team Secret.\n“It was truly a proud moment in my career to be part of an all-Filipino team like Team Secret, especially in the franchise league,” 2GE said.\n“Representing the Philippines alongside such talented teammates was an honor. I learned a lot, and I’ll always be grateful for the experience and the brotherhood we built together.”\nUnfortunately, Team Secret struggled in the VCT Pacific for most of 2GE’s tenure. Eventually, he was dropped from the roster on Tuesday, May 13.\n“To my former teammates, I’m truly grateful for the time we spent together, and I wish each of them nothing but success in their careers moving forward,” 2GE said in a message to his former teammates.\nNow a free agent, 2GE’s competitive fire has not burned out. He remains eager to compete in Valorant, regardless of the tier.\n“Right now, I’m open to anything. Whether it’s staying in the franchise league or joining a [Tier 2 or Tier 3] team, I’m ready to go wherever people believe my talent can grow and contribute the most. I’m confident I can bring something valuable to any team I join,” 2GE revealed.\nWith other former Valorant pros shifting to different game titles, 2GE made it clear that he isn’t following that route.\n“I’m not looking into switching to another game, I’m still fully committed to improving in Valorant,” 2GE revealed.\nHe also left a heartfelt promise to his fans: “To my fans—this isn’t the end. This is just another chapter. I’ll be back, stronger than ever. I hope you continue to support me in whatever path I take next. Thank you for believing in me.” /clorenciana\nDisclaimer: The comments uploaded on this site do not necessarily represent or reflect the views of management and owner of Cebudailynews. We reserve the right to exclude comments that we deem to be inconsistent with our editorial standards.",
            "content_length": 3385
        },
        {
            "article_id": "357f69ae055cfae0",
            "title": "Exclusive: Stuck in rank? PRX something has the best advice for you - ONE Esports",
            "published_at": "Sun, 17 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "ONE Esports",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMie0FVX3lxTE0xMXZRakJMc2xKYTJWUHlqajJQaDlZREVuUGx1bDZZSURqc0c0ZHNtMThMSXZGaXUzNk1hUE41OElQRnNOQklCdkVnTHRHV3pmN2owMHhnRnMzdklTUmtEbEtUZmR0MnR5aVFNTFU2MldueEI2N0l3b2NhTQ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.oneesports.gg/valorant/stuck-valorant-ranked-prx-something/",
            "query_players": [
                "something"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "something",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Updated on August 17, 11:24 a.m. (GMT+8): Improved relevancy.\nThis year’s inaugural VCT Pacific finals MVP is PaperRex’s duelist Ilya “something” Petrov.\nIn the five-game series against powerhouse DRX, he racked up an astounding 95/64/25 KDA on Breech, Reyna, and Jett with an average 241 ACS.\nOn the deciding map Bind, he shone bright on Reyna, hauling his team over the finish line with 292 ACS and a positive 22/12/3 KDA.\nJust three months earlier, he made history at another playoffs event — Valorant Challengers Japan Split 1 — where he scored 120 kills followed by 136 kills in two matches in two days.\nThese achievements on the big stage are a result of the hours upon hours he’s put into Valorant ranked where he built a strong foundation playing Jett. In this exclusive interview with ONE Esports, something reveals his best ranked habits and how he hit rank one multiple times.\nSomething is doing something you’re not on the Valorant ranked ladder\nSomething played two competitive titles before Valorant. In Dota, he accumulated 5,000 MMR and reached Divine, which is just one tier below Immortal, the highest rank in the game.\nIt is also through Dota that he got his in-game name. “To be honest, I stole this nickname from a Dota player from Ukraine. He was playing pubs on a smurf account and his nickname translated to ‘something’ in English,” he recalled, but can’t explain why he adopted it. “I don’t know. Just random. I like his nickname and I steal.”\nMoving on from the MOBA, he then jumped into CS:GO. While he never went pro, he did participate in competitions on FACEIT and did well. Even in CS:GO, something was playing with high sensitivity, which he continues to adopt in Valorant. Combining his experience in these two titles, he now has 12 years of FPS experience under his belt.\n“In Valorant, I have been playing for three years, 10 hours a day, so it’s not hard to reach rank one,” something told ONE Esports. “I did this on two occasions. It’s not hard. Just play and you’ll get it.\nWhen he ranks up, he instantly reviews his plays as he progresses. For example, if he pushes an angle and fails, he won’t repeat his actions. Instead, he will attempt to make more plays, try something different, and “analyze what’s the problem” by asking himself why he did it and why his agent died as a result.\nEven though he sometimes feels tired of grinding ranked, it’s rare. “Because I want to win. I enjoy playing Valorant,” he said.\n“It’s not a secret – just play, just grind. That’s all you need. Just spend a lot of hours, and one day you will grow and rank up,” something added.\nWhile his name is now synonymous with Jett in the pro scene, the very agent he set those kill records on, he actually started out playing Viper during the beta, then Reyna, before settling on Jett where he developed his own truly unique individual style.\n“To be honest, I never watch how other Jett players play,” something said. “I just play my game, never stealing anything from other players.”\nFollow ONE Esports on Facebook and Twitter for more Valorant news, guides, and highlights.\nREAD MORE: Exclusive: Paper Rex CigaretteS’ agent tier list places unconventional picks in S-Tier",
            "content_length": 3187
        },
        {
            "article_id": "8bc53f56f929e5ef",
            "title": "Every Valorant Skin Rarity Tier Explained - GameTyrant",
            "published_at": "Sun, 16 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "GameTyrant",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMieEFVX3lxTE43d2dtcE8yVHdoLUFyVFZIRUg1N3Azem1vOXZjVjU5VTBKemQtMExOLTBqSFJYOVRoU2ZUaHpIODR6Z1hlbVFkeHUwanMxQnBRcktPd0ZybkhpMWxnM0tjOFg2YXEwaVFDYjhxZ0p4RkhfaXVtRE9wTA?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://gametyrant.com/news/every-valorant-skin-rarity-tier-explained",
            "query_players": [
                "something"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Art",
                "bang",
                "Less",
                "NaturE",
                "something",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant skin rarity can feel like a maze when you first encounter the store. One day you're looking at a clean 875 VP skin, the next you're staring at an 11,900 VP bundle wondering what exactly makes it worth more than some people's monthly gaming budget.\nThe tier system makes more sense once you understand what you're actually paying for. Each rarity level comes with specific features and price points, ranging from the affordable Select Edition skins at 875 Valorant Points to the premium Ultra Edition bundles costing up to 11,900 Valorant Points. Some skins focus purely on visual redesigns, while others transform your weapons into animated spectacles with custom sound effects and finishing moves, making it special in some Valorant maps.\nHere's where it gets interesting: Select and Deluxe skins can be purchased individually or obtained through the Battle Pass, while Premium, Exclusive, and Ultra rarities are generally only available for direct purchase. The most coveted skins, like the Champions Collections and Arcane Sheriff, fall into special time-limited categories that may never return to the store. Miss them, and you might be waiting forever for another chance.\nKnowing these distinctions helps you make smarter decisions about where to spend your VP. This guide will break down each rarity tier so you can figure out which skins deliver the best value for your playstyle and budget.\nSelect Edition\nSelect Edition marks your first step into VALORANT's cosmetic world. These are the most budget-friendly skins you'll find, offering clean visual changes without breaking the bank. Think of them as the foundation tier perfect if you want something different from default weapons without committing to premium prices.\nSelect Edition Features\nSelect Edition skins keep things simple and straightforward. You'll recognize them by the blue circle icon in the store interface. What you get is pure visual redesign no flashy effects, no custom animations, no special sounds. Just clean, well-designed weapon makeovers that change how your guns look without messing with their core feel.\nThe appeal here is simplicity. These skins focus on solid aesthetic modifications that stand the test of time. You won't get bored of a well-designed Select skin because it doesn't rely on gimmicks to look good.\nHere's something worth knowing: all agent gear weapon skins fall into the Select Edition category. These particular skins can be unlocked at Tier 10 of an agent's contract using Kingdom Credits once you've unlocked the agent. Essentially, you can grab some Select-tier cosmetics for free just by playing with specific agents.\nSelect Edition Price Range\nSelect Edition skins maintain consistent pricing across the board. Individual Select Edition weapon skins cost 875 VP (approximately USD 10.00) each. Complete Select Edition bundles typically range from 2,930 VP (approximately USD 34.00) to 3,500 VP (approximately USD 41.00).\nBundle math works in your favor here. Most Select bundles include 4-5 weapon skins, sometimes with a melee weapon included that typically costs 1,750 VP on its own. If you want multiple skins from the same collection, bundles deliver significantly better value than individual purchases.\nNot all Select bundles cost the same, though. Collections like Prism II and Sensation are priced at 2,930 VP, making them somewhat cheaper than other Select tier bundles that cost 3,500 VP. Smart shoppers pay attention to these price differences.\nBest Select Edition Skins\nSeveral Select Edition collections stand out from the pack:\nPrism II delivers a clean, fade-style aesthetic reminiscent of CS:GO skins but at a fraction of the cost. Available for Bucky, Sheriff, Shorty, Stinger, and Vandal at 2,930 VP for the complete bundle.\nReverie offers classic, minimalistic design that works especially well on the Ghost and Operator. At just 875 VP per skin, it provides excellent value for players who prefer elegant, understated aesthetics.\nInfantry satisfies military enthusiasts with its WW1-inspired design that feels authentic and tactical. The Operator and Ghost variants are particularly popular for their realistic military appearance.\nEndeavor features an astronaut theme that honestly looks more premium than its Select Edition price suggests. Available for Ghost, Bulldog, Vandal, Operator, and Ares.\nLuxe represents one of the original weapon skin sets from VALORANT's beta period, featuring an elegant blue pattern design. The collection includes skins for Vandal, Ghost, Judge, Operator, Specter, and a knife melee weapon.\nSelect Edition skins prove you don't need to spend big to personalize your arsenal. While they lack the animated spectacle of higher tiers, many of these designs offer lasting visual appeal that won't leave your wallet crying.\nDeluxe Edition\nThe step up from Select Edition brings you to Deluxe skins, marked by their green rhombus icon in the store. These weapons sit comfortably in the middle ground between basic visual redesigns and the flashy effects of premium tiers.\nDeluxe Edition Features\nDeluxe Edition skins focus on refined artistic design without the interactive bells and whistles of higher-tier options. What sets them apart from Select skins includes:\n- Multi-dimensional visual designs that catch the eye \n- Glowing visual effects on select collections \n- Refined artistic elements that elevate gameplay esthetics \nMost Deluxe skins stick to visual improvements rather than interactive features. You won't find inspect and reload animations or special sound effects in this tier. Collections like Tigris exemplify what makes Deluxe skins appealing, offering weapons that are \"visually drawing and hold value\" while avoiding the higher cost of animated skins. The variety spans everything from Hawaiian-inspired Holomoku to the clean lines of Minima.\nDeluxe Edition Price Range\nDeluxe Edition maintains consistent pricing as the second tier in VALORANT's structure:\n- Individual gun skins: 1,275 VP (approximately USD 16.00) \n- Melee weapons (knives): 2,550 VP \n- Complete bundles: 4,270-5,100 VP (approximately USD 51.50-62.00) \nStandard Deluxe bundles with four gun skins plus a melee weapon cost 5,100 VP. The Silvanus bundle offers better value at 4,270 VP (approximately USD 51.50), making it one of the more affordable Deluxe sets. Some specialized knives like the Minima Karambit and Luna's Descent command a higher price of 3,550 VP each instead of the standard 2,550 VP.\nFor players who \"do not want to spend a huge amount of money on Valorant skins\" but still want something beyond basic Select offerings, Deluxe Edition hits the sweet spot.\nBest Deluxe Edition Skins\nSeveral Deluxe collections have earned their reputation for distinctive design elements:\nTigris Collection - The standout Deluxe set, featuring weapons with a \"fine finish that makes the gun have a multiple-dimensional look\" with \"glowing red visual effects\". Available for Shorty, Specter, Phantom, Operator, and includes the Hu Else tactical knife.\nLuna Collection - Popular for its unique esthetic and adorable buddies, covering Ghost, Specter, Vandal, and Marshal. The Luna's Descent knife commands a premium 3,550 VP.\nChromedek Collection - Sleek, modern design for Shorty, Bulldog, Phantom, Marshal, plus the Chromedek Gauntlet tactical knife.\nOrion Collection - Space-themed elegance for Frenzy, Odin, Phantom, and Vandal, with the Orion Sword.\nCombat Crafts - Workshop-inspired creativity for Bulldog, Frenzy, Judge, Phantom, and the Combat Crafts Ax.\nMinima - Clean, stripped-down design for minimalist enthusiasts, available on Ares, Operator, Phantom, Sheriff, and Specter.\nDeluxe skins deliver enough visual flair to stand out from basic options while staying accessible to budget-conscious players. They represent solid value for players seeking distinctive visual upgrades without premium pricing.\nPremium Edition\nPremium Edition is where things get serious. You'll spot these skins by their upside-down pink triangle icon in the store, and they represent the moment VALORANT skins stop being simple makeovers and start becoming experiences. This tier sits right in that sweet spot where visual flair meets reasonable pricing.\nPremium Edition Features\nPremium skins come with the full package that makes higher-tier weapons worth the investment. Each skin can be upgraded through multiple levels that unlock increasingly impressive features:\n- Custom animations that transform reload and equip sequences \n- Visual effects (VFX) that add particle effects and distinctive visual flair \n- Enhanced audio design with unique firing and impact sounds \n- Finishers that create spectacular visual displays when eliminating the final enemy \nMost Premium skins feature color variants that allow for personalization beyond the base design. These variants typically include 3-4 different color schemes, such as the Oni Phantom's distinctive red, black, and white options. The upgrade levels form an essential part of the Premium Edition experience, allowing players to progressively unlock animations, VFX, and finishers to enjoy the skin to its fullest potential.\nWhat makes Premium skins special is how they balance impressive features with accessible pricing. Many players consider this the ideal tier for getting the most bang for your buck. Plus, some of VALORANT's most iconic skin lines call this tier home, turning them into genuine status symbols within the community.\nPremium Edition Price Range\nPremium Edition maintains consistent pricing that won't completely destroy your wallet. Individual weapon skins cost 1,775 VP (approximately USD 23.00) each. Complete Premium Edition bundles typically cost 7,100 VP (approximately USD 82.00-85.00), though some collections like XERØFANG (6,355 VP) and Doodle Buds (6,992 VP) are priced slightly lower.\nMelee weapons (knives) in Premium collections generally cost 3,550 VP individually, though some specialized designs command higher prices. This represents a significant step up from lower-tier options, yet remains more accessible than Ultra or Exclusive editions.\nCertain bundles offer better value than others if you're watching your spending. Collections like Doodle Buds (6,992 VP) and XERØFANG (6,355 VP) provide the Premium experience at a somewhat lower price point. Older Premium collections occasionally return to the store rotation, giving you another chance to snag these popular skins.\nBest Premium Edition Skins\nThe Premium tier houses some of VALORANT's most beloved collections. Several have achieved legendary status among players:\nPrime Collection - The gold standard featuring premium white and gold aesthetics with enhanced one-tap audio confirmation. The Prime Vandal has become genuinely iconic. At 1,775 VP, it represents excellent long-term value that never feels outdated.\nReaver Collection - A community favorite offering unique Ghost and Guardian weapon skins alongside the popular Vandal variant. The collection's dark purple and ghostly appearance creates a distinctive aesthetic that many players find irresistible.\nIon Collection - Sleek futuristic design built around an energized core. Ion exemplifies the \"more with less\" philosophy with its clean white and blue color scheme that perfectly executes the sci-fi aesthetic.\nOni Collection - Ancient Japanese folklore meets sharp modern design. The Oni series features ornate oni masks and spiritual audio effects. The Phantom variant remains one of the most sought-after skins years after its initial release.\nCelestial Collection - Standing out for its unique Celestial Fan melee weapon and intricate cosmic designs. This collection showcases cosmic themes with stunning detail and satisfying sound effects.\nOther notable Premium collections include Magepunk, Sovereign, Neptune, Origin, and the popular Gaia's Vengeance series. The Premium tier represents the sweet spot where most of VALORANT's most beloved skin designs reside, balancing impressive features with relatively reasonable prices compared to higher-tier options.\nPremium Edition offers enough visual and audio enhancement to significantly transform your gameplay experience without reaching the extravagant pricing of Ultra or Exclusive collections.\nExclusive Edition\nThis is where things start getting serious and expensive. Exclusive Edition skins represent the point where VALORANT cosmetics stop being simple visual upgrades and become full weapon transformations that justify their premium price tags.\nExclusive Edition Features\nExclusive Edition skins deliver exceptional value through their comprehensive suite of upgrades and modifications. These high-end cosmetics come equipped with:\n- Premium animations that transform equip, reload, and inspect sequences \n- Advanced visual effects that substantially alter the weapon's appearance \n- Custom sound effects that create a distinctive audio signature \n- Unique finishers that display spectacular visual sequences upon match-ending eliminations \n- Multiple color variants allowing for personalized customization \n- Special transformation effects in some collections \nWhat distinguishes Exclusive skins from lower tiers is the comprehensive nature of their enhancements. These skins effectively transform weapons into entirely new experiences many players consider them \"new weapons\" due to their distinctive animations and sound signatures. Collections like Kuronami and RGX 11z Pro feature RGB lighting effects that activate upon inspection, creating a truly personalized experience.\nThe level of detail in these collections is genuinely impressive. Each weapon feels completely different from its base version, with animations and effects that make every reload, inspect, and elimination feel special.\nExclusive Edition Price Range\nHere's where your wallet might start feeling a bit lighter. The pricing structure for Exclusive Edition skins follows a relatively consistent pattern, though some variation exists between collections:\nIndividual weapon skins typically cost between 2,175-2,675 VP (approximately USD 24.00-28.00). Melee weapons in this tier command significantly higher prices, ranging from 4,350-5,350 VP (approximately USD 45.00-55.00).\nComplete bundles vary more substantially in price:\n- Most standard Exclusive bundles cost 8,700 VP (approximately USD 85.00)[153] \n- Select collections like RGX 11z Pro are priced at 6,700 VP (approximately USD 70.00) \n- Premium collections such as Evori Dreamwings reach 9,900 VP (approximately USD 105.00) \n- Kuronami, among the most sought-after bundles, costs 9,500 VP (approximately USD 95.00) \nExclusive Edition bundles typically include four weapon skins plus a melee weapon, along with complementary items like player cards, gun buddies, and sprays. These represent substantial investments that offer comprehensive cosmetic transformations across multiple weapons.\nBest Exclusive Edition Skins\nSeveral Exclusive Edition collections have earned legendary status within the VALORANT community:\nKuronami Collection - Widely regarded as \"the best bundle in-game\" featuring what many consider the best melee skin and best Vandal skin available. Its popularity prompted Riot to announce a forthcoming Kuronami 2.0 collection. The katana melee weapon alone makes this bundle worth considering.\nRGX 11z Pro Series - A fan favorite featuring RGB lighting effects that actually change colors during weapon inspection. The latest Episode Nine iteration includes a highly coveted Karambit melee weapon. Perfect for players who want their weapons to feel as futuristic as they look.\nPrelude to Chaos - Notable for its chaotic, edgy aesthetic with green, white, and blue variants. The collection includes a distinctive Flail melee weapon and impressive visual effects that live up to the \"chaos\" name.\nNocturnum Collection - A spooky, League of Legends-inspired design featuring a transforming Scythe melee weapon and distinctive black, blue, and yellow color variants. The gothic aesthetic appeals to players who prefer darker themes.\nPrimordium Bundle - Priced at 8,700 VP, this collection offers a distinctive aesthetic for the Phantom, Shorty, Specter, and Vandal. The prehistoric theme creates a unique visual identity that stands out from other collections.\nExclusive Edition skins represent significant investments that genuinely transform both the visual and audio experience of gameplay. These collections justify their premium positioning through comprehensive feature sets that make every match feel more engaging.\nUltra Edition\nWelcome to the top of the mountain. Ultra Edition skins represent the absolute pinnacle of what VALORANT cosmetics can achieve, marked by a yellow square icon in the store interface. These aren't just skins anymore they're entirely new experiences that transform your weapons into living, breathing spectacles.\nUltra Edition Features\nUltra Edition skins redefine what's possible when developers decide to go all out. The level of detail and animation work here is honestly ridiculous:\nComplex animated transformations fundamentally alter how weapons look and behave. Comprehensive visual effects feature extensive particle systems and dynamic elements that make your screen light up. Custom sound design gives each weapon unique firing, reload, and impact audio that you'll recognize instantly. Interactive animations respond to your actions and game states, while some collections like Protocol 781-A even include voice lines that give weapons distinct personalities.\nMost Ultra skins come with multiple color variants for personalization. What sets these apart from every other tier is the sheer level of visual transformation. Take Elderflame skins they literally turn weapons into dragons that crane their necks, look at players, and breathe smoke when shots miss. Each collection offers fully upgraded animations for using and reloading weapons, plus finishers and typically three color options.\nUltra Edition Price Range\nUltra Edition pricing reflects their status as VALORANT's most elaborate cosmetics. Individual weapon skins typically cost 2,475 VP (approximately USD 25.00-30.00). Melee weapons demand even higher prices, ranging from 4,950 VP (approximately USD 45.00-50.00) to 5,950 VP for the Radiant Entertainment System Power Fist currently the most expensive single skin in VALORANT history.\nComplete Ultra Edition bundles vary significantly. Standard Ultra bundles like Elderflame and Protocol 781-A cost 9,900 VP (approximately USD 99.99-119.00)[202]. The Radiant Entertainment System collection commands 11,900 VP, making it the most expensive bundle available.\nYou'll need to spend £99.99 (or regional equivalent) to get 12,000 VP for most Ultra collections. Individual purchases mean spending around £34.99 for 4,000 VP. These are serious investments, but the visual impact justifies the cost for many players.\nBest Ultra Edition Skins\nSeveral Ultra Edition collections have achieved legendary status:\nElderflame remains the gold standard. Released in Patch 1.03, these dragon-themed designs for Vandal, Operator, Frenzy, Judge, and dagger melee maintain extraordinary popularity years later. Many players still consider it their \"number-one favorite\" for its distinctive animations.\nProtocol 781-A brought robotic-styled weapons with unique voice lines that change pitch based on color variants. Available for Sheriff, Specter, Bulldog, Phantom, and includes a tactical knife.\nRadiant Entertainment System stands as the most expensive collection, featuring arcade game-inspired designs for Bulldog, Ghost, Operator, Phantom, and the PowerFist melee at 5,950 VP.\nPhaseguard offers futuristic military styling for Ghost, Bulldog, Outlaw, Vandal, plus the Phaseguard Splitter melee. Features distinctive SFX, animations, and finishers with blue, black, and gold variants.\nEvori Dreamwings showcases adorable Dreamwings familiars across Ghost, Odin, Specter, Vandal, and includes the Evori's Spellcaster melee. Comes with green, pink, orange, and blue variants.\nUltra Edition skins represent the ultimate cosmetic investment. They completely transform both appearance and feel of weapons, creating experiences that genuinely enhance gameplay through their visual and audio impact.\nBattle Pass Skins\nThe Battle Pass stands out as VALORANT's most budget-friendly way to build up your skin collection. Rather than dropping serious VP on individual purchases, this time-limited progression system gives you multiple skin sets and extras for a single, reasonable price.\nBattle Pass Skins Features\nEach Battle Pass delivers a solid mix of cosmetic rewards across both free and premium tracks. The premium track typically includes:\n- Three new weapon skin collections (two Select Edition and one Deluxe Edition) \n- 13 gun skins (with only 1 available in the free track) \n- 10 gun buddies (4 in free track) \n- 13 player cards (4 in free track) \n- 15 sprays (2 in free track) \n- 3 player titles (all in free track) \n- 180 Radianite Points (60 in free track) \nWhat makes Battle Pass skins particularly appealing is their exclusivity once an act ends, these weapon skins cannot be obtained through any means. The Tier 50 reward for Premium Track owners is always a melee skin, which adds solid value to the pass. Recent passes like the PERCH collection have featured seasonal color variants and animated weapon art, showing how Riot keeps evolving the Battle Pass experience.\nBattle Pass Skins Availability\nThe Battle Pass maintains consistent pricing at 1,000 VP (approximately $10) per act. Each act brings a fresh pass with completely new content, and once it's over, those items become permanently unobtainable.\nEven if you don't want to spend money, every season offers some exclusive cosmetics through the free track typically one weapon skin, player titles, and some Radianite Points. For those who do purchase the premium track, the Battle Pass serves as the most cost-effective source of Radianite Points in the game, offering up to 160 points when completed.\nBest Battle Pass Skins\nCertain Battle Pass collections have earned recognition for their exceptional designs throughout VALORANT's history:\n- Ruin Dagger (Episode 1, Act 3) - Often considered the most iconic Battle Pass melee weapon with its sleek, dark design and glowing red accents \n- Velocity Karambit (Episode 5, Act 3) - Brought an affordable karambit option to players with sleek, futuristic design and vibrant color options \n- Kingdom Collection (Episode 1, Act 1) - One of the original weapon skin sets, featuring elegant blue pattern designs for Phantom, Classic, Specter, Bucky, and melee \n- Infinity Collection (Episode 2, Act 1) - Highly regarded for its clean esthetic across Classic, Bulldog, Specter, Guardian, and Phantom \nThe Battle Pass delivers exceptional value for regular players who want cosmetics without breaking the bank. You get multiple weapon skins and exclusive items that would cost significantly more if purchased individually.\nTime-Limited Skins\nHere's where things get really interesting and potentially heartbreaking for collectors. Time-limited skins represent VALORANT's most exclusive tier, appearing briefly before disappearing into the digital void. Miss them, and you might be stuck watching other players flex skins you'll never own.\nTime-Limited Skins Features\nTime-limited skins operate on a completely different level from regular store offerings. Once removed from the store, these collections typically never return, which is why many collectors turn to buying Valorant accounts with rare skins already unlocked. The exclusivity factor alone makes them worth considering, but many come with unique features you won't find anywhere else.\nChampions collections feature the remarkable \"Champions Aura\" effect a visual enhancement that makes your weapon glow when you've secured the most kills in a game. It's like having a built-in bragging system that activates when you're popping off. Special event bundles often include exclusive accessories unavailable through other means, making them genuine one-time opportunities.\nThe Night Market represents another time-sensitive opportunity, appearing once per act and offering six randomly selected skins at discounts ranging from 10% to 49%. You never know what'll show up, which makes it either a pleasant surprise or a frustrating wait. The Esports Store showcases limited-time capsules celebrating competitive Valorant teams and leagues. The Give Back Bundle combines community voting with charitable contributions, allowing players to select which previous skins return briefly while supporting good causes.\nTime-Limited Skins Pricing\nPricing for time-limited collections varies considerably based on their exclusivity and features. Champions bundles consistently command premium prices at approximately 6,265 VP (roughly USD 66.00). Tournament-specific collections like VCT LOCK//IN were priced at 5,440 VP. The RiotX Arcane bundle, celebrating the Netflix series, was more affordable at 2,380 VP.\nThe VCT 2025 Season capsule represents the most recent offering at 5,850 VP, featuring the VCT Karambit with regional-themed designs. Night Market discounts can make premium skins significantly more affordable, yet their random nature means you cannot predict which skins will appear. Some players wait months hoping their dream skin shows up at a discount, only to cave and buy it at full price anyway.\nMost Iconic Time-Limited Skins\nChampions collections remain the most prestigious time-limited skins in VALORANT. The Champions 2021 Vandal and Karambit have achieved legendary status as the inaugural championship collection. These skins carry serious bragging rights they're instantly recognizable and signal that you've been around since the early competitive days.\nThe Arcane Sheriff holds similar prestige as one of the rarest skins, available only during the 2021 Arcane event. If you own this one, you're part of an exclusive club that gets smaller every day as more players join the game.\nOther notable collections include the VCT LOCK//IN bundle with its four regional knife variants and the IGNITE collection celebrating Valorant's Chinese launch. Each represents a specific moment in VALORANT's history that you either experienced or missed forever.\nQuick Reference Guide\nHere's a breakdown of all the skin tiers we've covered, laid out in one place so you can compare pricing and features without jumping between sections. This should help you figure out which tier offers the best value for your specific budget and preferences.\nThe Battle Pass clearly offers the best bang for your buck if you play regularly. For 1,000 VP, you get more skins than any other option, plus extras like player cards and sprays. The catch? You need to grind through the tiers, and once the act ends, those skins are gone forever.\nTime-limited skins sit in their own category since pricing varies so much. The Champions collections are expensive, but that Champions Aura effect when you're match MVP makes them worth it for many players. Just remember - miss them, and you're probably out of luck permanently.\nConclusion\nVALORANT's skin ecosystem makes a lot more sense once you understand what drives the pricing. Each tier serves different types of players, from those who want simple visual changes to collectors chasing the most elaborate animations and effects.\nThe sweet spot for most players tends to land somewhere in the Premium tier. You get enough visual flair and custom effects to feel like you're using something special, without the wallet-crushing prices of Ultra collections. That said, there's nothing wrong with sticking to Select or Deluxe skins if you prefer clean designs over flashy animations.\nBudget-conscious players have solid options. The Battle Pass delivers exceptional value if you play regularly, and Select Edition skins prove that good design doesn't always need expensive effects. Meanwhile, serious collectors will find Ultra and Exclusive tiers offer genuinely unique experiences that transform how weapons look, sound, and feel.\nWatch for opportunities to maximize your VP spending. Night Market discounts can make premium skins much more affordable, though you can't control which ones appear. Battle Pass seasons offer multiple collections for the price of a single Premium skin, making them hard to beat for value.\nFocus your spending on weapons you actually use. That gorgeous Operator skin loses its appeal if you prefer playing close-range agents. Similarly, consider whether you value visual effects over clean aesthetics some players find elaborate animations distracting during clutch moments.\nTime-limited collections create tough decisions. Missing a Champions bundle or special event skin means it's likely gone forever, but don't feel pressured to buy everything that appears. Stick to collections that genuinely appeal to you rather than purchasing based on rarity alone.\nThe skin system rewards patience and planning over impulse purchases. Build your collection gradually, targeting specific weapons and themes that match your preferences. Most importantly, remember that no skin affects your gameplay performance buy what brings you joy, not what you think you should own.\nVALORANT offers something for every budget and taste. Armed with knowledge about each tier's features and pricing, you can make decisions that feel right for your wallet and playstyle.",
            "content_length": 29392
        },
        {
            "article_id": "798b05cccf6f0234",
            "title": "NRG Benches Verno and Coach Bonkar After VCT Americas Kickoff - Insider Gaming",
            "published_at": "Sun, 16 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Insider Gaming",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikwFBVV95cUxQeW14em5xc3NCMVRWWi1uZXgtMDZfLXhNOUlwOGItTzFOTnZYUXdKRlFnSUY0N2lsWEFLVG9lenNEeFhldXgxYV8yeGFaQmRtN2hMTW1SZXhGNFZ0N3VoTm1jWEt4alRWWlVxMjVLNWRjaGVqeWZlZ3pJM1FkNzlhRXJQSXVscTVxWW9MNDNFWWo1c0E?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://insider-gaming.com/nrg-benches-verno-and-coach-bonkar-after-vct-americas-kickoff/",
            "query_players": [
                "Ethan",
                "Verno"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Ethan",
                "mada",
                "NaturE",
                "s0m",
                "Verno"
            ],
            "content": "After 12 official matches as a team and only three during VCT Americas, NRG has benched Andrew “Verno” Maust and VALORANT coach Malkolm “bonkar” Rench. Following a strong performance at offseason events that resulted in high expectations, NRG has already made significant changes.\nVerno and Bonkar Out From NRG After Only a Dozen Official Matches\nRoster changes to the NRG VALORANT roster had been rumored since the conclusion of Kickoff, but were confirmed by the organization today. NRG’s Chief Gaming Officer Jaime Cohenca says the benching is a result of a lack of cohesion that has been brewing for months.\n“What I can say is that we put together a roster that was poised to contend for international championships but ultimately failed in terms of team cohesion,” Cohenca wrote on X. “Winning SEN City and SOOP [in the offseason] only delayed the effects of the issues brewing. The team no longer could function as constructed, so continuing with the current five players/head coach was not an option.”\n“Given the nature of the VCT schedule, this break is pretty much the only window to make a roster move, so we are doing things now to be ready for when a Champs slot is on the line.”\nVCT Roster Update: Verno and Bonkar have been moved to the NRG VALORANT reserve roster. They will remain a part of NRG as we prepare for the remainder of the 2025 VCT season.\nLong-time veterans of NRG in Sam “s0m” Oh, Pujan “FNS” Mehta, and Ethan “Ethan” Arnold will seemingly stay on the starting roster. Adam “mada” Pampuch, who joined the team during this past offseason, will also stay following an exceptional statistical performance at Kickoff. Some fans speculate that former coach Chetan “Chet” Singh could return when his suspension expires at the end of Masters Bangkok. Chet was suspended by Riot Games for streaming VODs deemed “confidential” on his personal channel.\nNRG lost to Cloud9 and KRÜ at Kickoff, while managing to win against 100T in between those matches, but earned no points towards Champions qualification. Their season resumes when VCT Americas Stage 1 starts in late March.\nWho should NRG bring on to the starting lineup? Let us know down below or on the Insider Gaming forum.\nFor more Insider Gaming esports coverage, check out the news that a $400,000 Marvel Rivals tournament is coming.",
            "content_length": 2310
        },
        {
            "article_id": "2ad5404c25cfc5cf",
            "title": "Keiko's Valorant homecoming - Team Liquid",
            "published_at": "Sun, 16 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Team Liquid",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiakFVX3lxTFBwQTdCQllKcHJubTMtTnZROFpCLXNiWjlnY2RMQ3RORjdmaTlETVJ4bm96MngyLWRKNXBkTjZlZUZzT0ZzOHVuNEdpcl9xX25NR3Nja3hvZmQxRFpSYnlZT2g4TDhjaFFpNGc?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://teamliquid.com/articles/keikos-valorant-homecoming",
            "query_players": [
                "keiko"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "BerLIN",
                "hype",
                "keiko",
                "something",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "With the first VCT Masters event of the year just around the corner, it’s been a whirlwind time for Team Liquid Valorant, who qualified to the event on February 8 and flew out to Thailand a week later. The team successfully defied expectations with a full lower bracket run through the Kickoff tournament, but Masters Bangkok will be a different kind of challenge for them. Still, just the act of qualifying feels pretty good for a brand new team, especially after Team Liquid wasn’t in attendance at any of the international events in 2024.\nFor Keiko, the team’s breakout star, it’s an especially significant accomplishment — he’s half-Thai on his mother’s side, which means his family will be there to watch him play in his first international VCT event. We caught up with Keiko right before Masters to ask about how he’s feeling, how his family have reacted to his success, and how it feels to constantly be proving people wrong.\nYou qualified to Masters Bangkok! How are you feeling?\nI’m so excited to go, to be honest. I heard about Masters in Bangkok last season, we got told pretty early, and I was so hyped for it. I really wanted to qualify, and… yeah, we qualified. I’m super excited to be in some warm weather, finally.\nI know a lot of your family is in Thailand. When you found out that [Masters] was going to be there, did that light a fire under you?\nIt definitely gave us a lot more motivation. The last time I went was the Christmas before last season, and it was the most fun experience being there, seeing family that I haven’t seen for a long time. And now I’ve qualified, they’re going to be coming to watch the first match — and the second match, hopefully. My mum’s flying there, and my grandma’s coming to watch, and my mum’s two brothers are coming to watch as well.\nIt’s pretty funny because they saw, and they were super excited, and they were reading all these Thai articles. My mum keeps sending me these articles and her family is all commenting underneath them, which is embarrassing, but, yeah.\nThat’s so sweet. I can’t wait for your grandma to get her first taste of professional Valorant.\n[laughs] Yeah, that’s going to be crazy.\nWhen we spoke last year, you told me that your trip to Thailand was one of your favorite vacations ever, because you got to see family you hadn’t seen since you were a little kid. What does it mean to you that you can play there and actually show your family what it is you do for a living?\nIt’s pretty exciting. I’m just happy to go there because I think they’re super proud of what I’ve done, qualifying to this event. I don’t think they really understood what I did before, because we didn’t qualify to anything last year, so I couldn’t go and tell them that I qualified for Shanghai, or Korea, or anything like that. So I think that it makes it more real for them, basically.\nWas your mom watching when you qualified to Masters?\nShe watches, I think, every game. She took a lot of paid leave to watch my games because we were making a really good run, and she was so excited, because last year we didn’t really win, and she was so sad for me. This year we finally started winning and I was playing well, and she was so happy. She took a lot of days off to watch me.\nYou told me that sometimes she’ll call you and ask you why you whiffed, even though she doesn’t understand the game. Do you think she understands the game better now and is therefore better equipped to roast you?\nYeah, definitely. My sister and my dad and sometimes my girlfriend all watch the games together. My girlfriend actually plays Valorant, she knows a lot and she understands everything really well. She’ll tell me that my mum knows everything that’s happening in the game — she knows when I do something good, and when I do something bad. Like, when I lose a round, she’ll ask, “Why is [Keiko] doing this?” She’s just saying this round is my fault because I did this, why am I dying here, stuff like that.\nYour mom reviewing your VODs aside, you personally had a pretty incredible run through Kickoff. What’s it been like for you adjusting to this mostly-new team, with new coaches and teammates?\nThe way we’re playing is something that fits me a lot better. There's a lot more freedom for a player, and I think that it's a very loose but directed system. Each player can make their own decisions. It's not like, “let's do this, and if that happens, do this,” super structured gameplay. I think that everyone has the freedom to do their own thing, and it fits me more ‘cause I'm a player that likes to make my own decisions and learn from my mistakes.\nI think I'm not the best in that structured system, to be honest. I'm better with the looser style. The coaching staff, [LohaN] and [yaotziN], they do a lot of work outside of the server for us, and they just trust us to play the game.\nI saw you Tweeted a joke about how you guys must have gotten lucky six times after beating Heretics. After dropping to that lower bracket, what was your mindset when people were kind of writing Liquid off? How did that affect your mentality going into the rest of the tournament?\nThe game we lost against FUT… I didn’t play great. I think it was the worst game I had in Kickoff in terms of my decision-making. We kind of just lost to ourselves. I think everyone understood that we lost to ourselves. They didn’t play better than us. I don’t think they play better than us. We were making decisions due to nerves and things like that.\nAnd everyone was saying, “oh, Liquid got lucky against GIANTX, BBL will take care of them.” And game after game, we’re beating all these teams that everyone expects to beat us. We were pretty dominant against GIANTX, against FUT the second time, against Heretics, and everyone’s saying Liquid just faces teams who are in bad form. That’s really funny to me. No matter what we do as Liquid, we’re never going to get, “Liquid are playing good Valorant.” It’s never going to happen. And I think we have to accept that.\nI like proving people wrong. I think it was really funny to watch back people’s reactions to the games, to see what they’re saying about us. That’s the best part for me.\nMaybe it’s a good thing that people are always underrating Liquid, because that means you’ll always be proving something to someone. But let’s talk about that Heretics match specifically. After a while, it seemed like the momentum was unstoppable. What did it feel like to go through that match and realize at the end, shit, we qualified for Masters?\nHeretics are really good players, all of them. But during the Lotus game, everyone on our team felt that they were really scared, in terms of the style they were playing. I remember one round where I just walk out B and kill someone on the jiggle, and I’m just thinking that they’ve lost the feel of the game. But we played some completely random comp on the fly — like, we said we were going to play these five agents one day before the game — we had one practice with this comp, and we were like, “let’s just run it. Let’s just see what happens.”\nWhen we won Lotus convincingly, 13-4, everyone knew that we were gonna win the series, because we shouldn’t be winning with that comp. We shouldn’t even be getting six rounds with that comp. But just the way we were playing as a team and the way they were playing… I don’t think they were in the best shape, to be honest. I would say that’s the only team that had actual bad form against us. But after playing Lotus, everyone was super chill. Like, we kind of understood that we were just going to win the game.\nWe didn’t really celebrate too much after the game, ‘cause I think everyone was happy, but we expected it to be a much harder game. I think everyone was just more excited that we had the opportunity to go to grand finals.\nUnfortunately, grand finals didn’t go quite as well as the Heretics game. What is the biggest lesson you learned from the game against Vitality that you’ll be taking into Masters?\nI think we just need a stronger map pool. And, to be honest, the reason we lost was we started getting scared to lose in the Vitality match, just not making plays because we were scared to make a mistake. I think there were a lot of nerves after we lost Lotus. I think everyone was thinking, “what if we get reverse swept?” It kind of bled into Bind, we weren’t proactive, we weren’t talking as much, and it just snowballed on Fracture.\nIt’s just more experience for the team. I’m not as sad that we got reverse swept, because I think we’re a team that learns pretty fast.\nI’m excited to see how you guys do in Masters. Have you improved your Thai? Could you communicate in it now without being embarrassed?\nNo. Impossible. I was thinking last year, like, “I need to practice, I need to learn, I’m going to ask my mum to only speak to me in Thai and practice and actually learn because it’s so embarrassing that I can barely speak it, I can only understand it…” But then the season came along, I had to start playing, I had no time to learn.\nIt’s such a shame because we actually qualified! So now it’s like, “fuck, I hope I don’t get brought on stage and asked to say something.”\nAt least you’ll be able to understand when fans say something to you.\nActually, the worst part is that I can only understand when my mum speaks to me in Thai. I can’t even understand different accents or anything like that. It’s only when she speaks to me that I understand fully what she means. It’s going to be really bad.\nDon’t worry, I’m sure you’re going to be very popular there.\nI hope not!\nWell, it’s your first Masters event — what are you looking forward to?\nJust the fact that we’re playing with a crowd. In Berlin, there’s, like, five people watching us. So I’m sure that the arena is going to be much more exciting, much more hype. It’s the first time I’m going to be playing on a stage where there’s loads of people.\nIs there anything you want to say to your fans and to Team Liquid fans?\nTo all the Team Liquid fans that have believed in us from the start, thank you. We made Masters this year and we hope we have a good showing there. And to all the new fans, I hope you guys watch us in Bangkok. We’re going to prove more people wrong.",
            "content_length": 10198
        },
        {
            "article_id": "0d8b95ec6da295ef",
            "title": "Valyn: “I’d regret it if we lost and I didn’t give 100% energy” – G2 get their first win at VCT Masters Toronto - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Sun, 15 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidkFVX3lxTE5HVzJxU19ncUxlaGxBWnVFS3ZyN2QwYmhnUEowLUJzYzRpMUpaanhLZG1zQm5lNldSeVZtSTEzaFdpVDhrMTd5WlBsODNGVEFnby1fZGp0QWNZQmNsNF9yYW9sYUVvb192QlBIWkhvSGJHOVQ4MXc?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/masters-toronto-g2-beats-xlg",
            "query_players": [
                "valyn"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "leaf",
                "Life",
                "something",
                "valyn",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Their opening loss to Paper Rex was a shock to G2 after going to Masters Toronto as tournament favourites, but the wider context of first-seed defeats gave the team an out. Excuses or not, their next match, against XLG, was already do-or-die. Thankfully for the Americas first seed, they avoided disaster, winning a clean 2-0 and starting to build some momentum of their own for the lower bracket.\nThe win saw G2 quickly bounce back following their PRX defeat, and in the post-match press conference coach Josh “JoshRT” Lee was asked on the adaptations made as a result of that loss.\n“We needed to focus on ourselves. I think the biggest difference between the PRX game to XLG, for us, it’s just maintaining some discipline on defence, making sure our plans are in order, having clarity in our situational awareness. That fell against Paper Rex, which is kind of uncharacteristic. And we all kind of agreed that it’s, like, early tournament mistakes.\n“Generally you tend to have those in your first game just to get used to the environment. There’s a lot of new things going on, and in the early tournaments usually we would scrape by and win. And then we learn and we’re like: “Alright, let’s make sure we’re more disciplined in the next one”. But Paper ReX was just really, really strong. They abused those gaps and took us apart. So that was solved in our VOD review into XLG. We showed two different maps this time, but the same principles are probably applied.”\nThat mid-tournament learning could not be more pressing in Toronto following the nerfing of Tejo, subsequently removing him from the meta entirely. When asked how it impacted G2 strategically, JoshRT said it would be “delusional” to think that it wouldn’t have had an impact on his team.\n“Obviously we had to change a lot of things and I think every team in this tournament has the same problem to solve. So I won’t ever point to that be like: “Oh, that’s the reason why we lost”. I think every team has that problem to solve.\n“So yeah, I think we’re just trying our best to figure out what the new meta is. And sometimes your best foot forward won’t always work out in your first attempt and you just have to adjust afterwards.”\nIn the server, it was G2’s Nathan “leaf” Orf who was the standout performer, going +25 across the two maps, with 42 kills and a silly 303 ACS. Asked how he prepared prior to such a performance, leaf revealed that he simply likes to take things easy.\n“I think just keeping it light. I don’t like to overstress about anything, but match days, I try to just keep it light. Keep it easy mentally because, you know, at the end of the day, the game’s already stressful as it is. Pre-game, you don’t need to make it even harder on yourself, and you know, just making sure that I’m doing my job as a player in-game.\nIn-game leader Jacob “valyn” Batio was noted for his energy during the match, seen giving animated reactions to rounds in contrast to his usually quieter demeanour. This was by design, with valyn explaining how he made the conscious effort to bring up the energy for his side.\n“Traditionally I’m more of just like a- I don’t even like giving fist bumps. I just stay focused and don’t move. But this time, Josh said: “Guys, we can’t leave with any regrets”. And I feel like I would regret it if we lost and I didn’t give my 100% energy. And I told them guys, before the match, I was like: “Guys, if we win pistol, take off your headsets because I’m going to destroy you”. And I lifted my mic up and just went crazy.\n“And it felt good. It felt like it brought the fire to the team and it’s just a small part of why we won today. But you know, it’s just some energy to bring to the boys — something new. And I think I’m going to try to carry that on more.”\nThe win not only saw G2 progress in Toronto, but it also guaranteed the team qualification to VALORANT Champions later in the year — the first team in the world to qualify. For coach JoshRT, Champs qualification also brings relief, with Ascension sides all but securing their place in their respective leagues if they manage to reach VALORANT’s marquee event.\n“Of course, that’s guaranteed work, job secured. It’s hard, this life is hard because you only have three [global] events, really four now. So any qualification, we’re just very proud of.”",
            "content_length": 4307
        },
        {
            "article_id": "ed370b678bd6d2e5",
            "title": "DRX continue their 11-game unbeaten record at VALORANT Champions Paris - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Sun, 14 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMijAFBVV95cUxPTFVoOUl4Mk9Qb2w1eHkzRTVUS3o0SVpSdThaNUdpSkI2b1F6blRQbmlZVDFkSnR0YXI4ZjQtYnlNOTladDM5S1lORk5JZmF6OVpkSW1SRkROeEQtN0k2emJRc3Q3YlJjcUR1MTNWWHhLR1k4Z0NEd2ZqZmgzaW9KWm5DeFBkaWZDLXFaZQ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/valorant-champions-nrg-beats-edg-drx-beats-tl",
            "query_players": [
                "Flashback"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "BeYN",
                "brawk",
                "ComeBack",
                "Flashback",
                "Life",
                "mada",
                "MaKo",
                "s0m",
                "skuba",
                "slowly",
                "ZmjjKK"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant Champions Paris Day 2 started off with the Group C games. Two high-stakes matchups were on the line, the first between last year’s Champions EDG and NRG, a team with three rookies who were on the international stage for the first time. The second game had a fully in-form Liquid going up against a consistent DRX, yet a DRX that had shown signs of weakness during the Pacific playoffs and had squeaked in off the back of circuit points.\nNeither of these matchups had happened in the past, meaning both teams were facing off for the first time. While EDG and Team Liquid might have been favorites, NRG and DRX each secured 2-0 wins over strong opponents. They did so in very different ways, however: NRG with narrow margins and clutch finishes, DRX with more controlled dominance. For DRX especially, this puts their record of success in their opening game at international events 11-0, a record no other team are close to matching.\nNRG went up against Edward Gaming in the opening series of Day 2 of Champions Paris and came away with a 2-0 victory. The first map was Abyss, where the defending Champions, who had won last year’s event after winning this very map, found a devastatingly strong start on their attacking side. Their combination of Yoru and Neon enabled them to get to a 10-2 half, a dominant scoreline.\nWhen EDG won the next pistol, it seemed like it was the end, but after that, NRG clawed back the game round by round, pushing it to overtime where NRG eventually eked out an extremely tight win with a score of 17-15 after several momentum swings and a crucial 4K in the penultimate round by s0m.\nEdward Gaming looked strong early, but NRG hung on, winning crucial rounds toward the end of regulation and in overtime to take the map. On Abyss, the standout performance for EDG came from ZmjjKK, who posted an ACS of 208 despite the loss, but the real star of the map was rookie duelist mada, a player who was playing his first game at an international major. For NRG, both of their other rookies also contributed heavily to the comeback shown by the team, with consistent impact across the board.\nOn the second map, Corrode, NRG again edged out EDG, this time 13-11, maintaining pressure and leveraging rounds where EDG’s defense flinched under assault. Despite an even first half, NRG slowly won round after round, and at 11-11, got to match point while leaving EDG’s economy looking shaky. The match was similarly tight to Abyss, but the result was the same; NRG capitalized on EDG’s mistakes in mid-rounds and kept calm during critical late-round situations.\nSkuba was NRG’s MVP on the defensive half, with his anchoring abilities denying EDG access round after round to the B site, and brawk with his Odin was a major menace on A. Despite ZmjjKK having an ACS of 282 on the map, overall NRG had more impact as a team. Ultimately the overall MVP of the series was skuba on the Sentinel/Flex role, netting himself 47 kills across both maps and a 79% KAST.\nWith this win, NRG are just one more game victory away from qualifying for the playoffs. Meanwhile, EDG will have to fight against EMEA’s #1 seed Team Liquid for survival, a similar situation to Masters Bangkok.\nIn the second series, DRX faced off with Team Liquid and also won 2-0, but with more room to breathe in their victories. The first map was once again Abyss, which DRX took 13-8. The map started slow for Team Liquid, as DRX shot off to a 4-0 start, one they pushed to a 6-1 lead before TL stabilized. TL came back to end the half 6-6, but DRX, well known for their defensive prowess on the map, leveraged their extremely mobile double duelists perfectly to cut off rotations and get early information.\nFrom the start, DRX imposed pressure, especially in opening duels and in securing key rounds after defensive switches. Liquid attempted to mount comebacks in the second half but couldn’t fully stabilize against DRX’s aggression and coordinated utility usage, with DRX winning four rounds in a row at the end to take the map 13-8. DRX’s Flashback put up the stat line that earned him the map MVP with 21 kills and an ACS of 252, leveraging his Yoru picks to split defenses and create space.\nThe second map, Bind, was more contested. Liquid came into it looking to leverage their expertise on that map, and early rounds went back and forth, once again culminating in a 6-6 half. However, DRX held steady, especially on defense, and closed the map 13-10. Several rounds where Liquid seemed poised to gain momentum were broken by Flashback and MaKo with impact plays that swung major rounds in favor of DRX.\nOn Bind, BeYN turned in a very solid game on Fade, but for DRX, the overall MVP of the series was Flashback. He helped set the pace for DRX both maps, had big multi-kills, and generally forced Liquid to adjust their gameplan thanks to his positioning and operator skills. DRX now has to defeat NRG to qualify for playoffs, while Team Liquid will fight for life in the lower bracket vs EDG.\nToday’s matches in Group D promise just as much intrigue as what we saw in Group C. Dragon Ranger Gaming (DRG) face off against T1, marking their first-ever match against one another at Champions. The lack of history means DRG will have to bring fresh plans as they face off against the Masters Bangkok winners, while T1 will lean on their experience in high-pressure international stages to try and avoid an upset.\nMeanwhile, G2 Esports take on Team Heretics, a pairing loaded with history: they’ve met multiple times in Masters events, including Shanghai 2024 and other international matchups. G2 have generally had the edge in those encounters, though Heretics scored a commanding 3-0 win over them in Shanghai in one of those past matches.\nFor DRG, they need to place higher than fellow Chinese team XLG to guarantee their stay in the VCT, while for T1, they want to show off their international prowess again. For G2 and Heretics, every single round matters — these teams know each one might be a stepping stone or a stumbling block.",
            "content_length": 6029
        },
        {
            "article_id": "4ccea4a63a0fe8ea",
            "title": "Team Heretics have won a grand final, beating Fnatic to become EWC VALORANT champions - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Sun, 13 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiekFVX3lxTE5PQVNkOEM0R3dMYzJTUVVDRmU2SHowN09NWm5xWlJlYVVkcHZRdml2dklZXzV1Z1dHeHM2QVRDaDFTcjhvaGw3NEc3X3NYUW1zNTRmZk1BZy1NMm5XR3lWYkx3THpvMzJVTEhvWnhjNEVVNTFJb2FBTzl3?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/team-heretics-wins-ewc-valorant",
            "query_players": [
                "Wo0t"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "ComeBack",
                "Wo0t"
            ],
            "content": "Team Heretics have finally ended their grand final curse, winning at the sixth attempt to become inaugural EWC VALORANT champions in a sensational 3-2 reverse sweep against Fnatic.\nFinally, after five doses of grand final heartbreak over the last year-and-a-half, Team Heretics have gotten one over the line. They are Esports World Cup winners.\nA final matchup with Fnatic saw Los Niños unsurprisingly go in as underdogs. Fnatic were fresh from their runners-up finish at Masters Toronto — a tournament that was disastrous for Heretics — and had won this matchup in the last two occurrences, including the 3-0 EMEA Stage 1 final.\nThe opening two maps looked like more of the same. Blowouts from Fnatic saw them take a 2-0 series lead, in which TH only managed a measly 5 combined round wins. A Fnatic title seemed inevitable heading into map three.\nBut then Team Heretics woke up on Icebox. Led by an inspired Wo0T, the side finally managed to string rounds together. The teams were level at half time, before Heretics took control on defence. Despite Fnatic picking up a few late rounds to keep things tight, their opponents did eventually close out at 13-11 finish.\nEven at 2-1, a Fnatic win felt likely. It looked it too when they were 9-3 up at the halfway mark. However, this time it was their attack side that turned the tide for Heretics. A stunning back half saw them take 10 out of the final 11 rounds to complete a turnaround, secure a 13-10 map win and move on to a fifth game.\nFinally, after turning the series on its head, Team Heretics found an all-important map win that would, at last, see them become champions. On Ascent, Los Niños clinched a 13-10 victory, making them map, series, and championship winners.\nBy far the key factor in Team Heretics’ historic grand final comeback was Wo0t. The Turkish player was in inspired form during the series, crucially the difference-maker in his side’s first two map wins that set up the comeback, and finished the series with a 70/74/27 KDA and 186 ACS.\nWo0t’s efforts not only saw him become an Esports World Cup champion, but his tournament MVP title also earned him a cool $10k.\nFor Fnatic, another global grand final defeat within a matter of weeks will be heartbreaking. The side is perennially compared to its two-time global winning peak era of 2023. A new global title would finally see the side break out from under that shadow.\nThis final was an ideal chance to do that, against a familiar opponent whom Fnatic had handily beaten in their last two matchups, including the one that saw the Black and Orange crowned EMEA Stage 1 champions.\nWhile, for now, they’ll still be licking their wounds, the side should be able to take confidence from two great global tournament runs. As long as the losses do not dent their confidence too much, then the side will be confident that they’ve a shot at making another deep run, maybe winning a title, at VALORANT Champions late this year.",
            "content_length": 2945
        },
        {
            "article_id": "a8253f003109e8f9",
            "title": "Team Heretics Clinch 2025 VALORANT Esports World Cup Title - Gaming Amigos",
            "published_at": "Sun, 13 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Gaming Amigos",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMihgFBVV95cUxPeEpmTk9KczFHMVg1UWliV2NDODB3V2hXaTFnaWkydnlsT3dZSHB6c3lZaFhleEVkLVZ5Sm9RYmstY1ZLU1NUOFY4djltODdlLUMtZ1NUZUx6RU5fdmhOZlJ4WGNzYWxnZlZZLWFmQ0lIdlJ0OWdKRWFjMHczMzZoQjJBckNQdw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.gamingamigos.com/post/team-heretics-2025-valorant-esports-world-cup",
            "query_players": [
                "Wo0t"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Chronicle",
                "ComeBack",
                "crashies",
                "MiniBoo",
                "Wo0t"
            ],
            "content": "Team Heretics have emerged as the champions of the 2025 VALORANT Esports World Cup, defeating Fnatic 3-2 in a thrilling best-of-five grand final hosted in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia.\nThe win not only marks Heretics’ first-ever international VALORANT trophy but also delivers a poetic twist of redemption against the very team that reverse-swept them last year during the Champions Tour EMEA Stage 1.\nFnatic came into the series with momentum, crushing Team Heretics in the first two maps. They claimed a dominant 13-4 win on Lotus and followed it up with a ruthless 13-1 victory on Sunset. At that point, it looked like a clean sweep was on the cards, with Fnatic’s synergy and firepower overwhelming the Spanish roster.\nBut Team Heretics weren’t ready to give up the fight. The tide shifted dramatically on Icebox, where Heretics clawed back with a 13-11 win, setting the stage for a remarkable comeback. They carried that momentum into Haven and Ascent, securing back-to-back 13-10 wins to complete one of the most memorable reverse sweeps in VALORANT history.\nThis victory is a particularly sweet one for Team Heretics. The squad had come close to major wins before, falling in the grand finals of both VALORANT Masters Shanghai and the 2024 VALORANT Champions. This time, however, they didn’t let history repeat itself.\nMert “Wo0t” Alkan of Turkey was named the MVP of the tournament, with standout plays across all maps. Lithuanian player Dominykas “MiniBoo” Lukasevicius was also pivotal, dropping 21 kills in the final map and leading the scoreboard in key rounds.\nFnatic's path to the finals was solid, finishing first in Group D and eliminating Karmine Corp and Paper Rex in the playoffs. But their performance in the final three maps exposed cracks in their coordination. Despite flashes of brilliance from Austin “crashies” Roberts and Timofey “Chronicle” Khromov, they couldn’t hold back Heretics' late-game surge.\nOn the other hand, Team Heretics finished second in Group C and had to work through a tougher playoff bracket, defeating BBL Esports and Gen.G to reach the grand final. Their victory not only earns them the championship trophy and a $500,000 prize but also 1,000 Club Points — putting them in strong contention for the EWC Club Championship later this year.\nAccording to Esports Charts, this grand final drew a peak viewership of 451,808, the highest of the entire VALORANT World Cup segment. The tournament overall logged over 8.2 million hours watched, with an average of 163,726 viewers across approximately 51 hours of airtime.\nThe stakes of the event were high, and the $1.2 million prize pool distribution was as follows:\nThe tournament format featured 16 of the world’s best VALORANT teams, grouped into four double-elimination brackets. The top two from each group advanced to the playoffs, which culminated in a single-elimination knockout leading to the best-of-five grand final.",
            "content_length": 2910
        },
        {
            "article_id": "9ff67c484fff0260",
            "title": "News - Esports World Cup",
            "published_at": "Sun, 13 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports World Cup",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMicEFVX3lxTE9CVUFSUk54Q1JqaHBrbjBfVFE2V1QwcHRuUXRRS2Z4TTZCaWFFTGtzV0tzMG1PUzlPZjdXN05kNUQ5b1hzMVg2ZndsbXlRLXMwTG9NMGhUUjhuY0I1Y2hVQTI2T0V2X2ZDd0Q3RFlUZmM?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esportsworldcup.com/en/news/week1-champions-recap-ewc25",
            "query_players": [
                "Wo0t"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "ComeBack",
                "Kicks",
                "something",
                "Wo0t"
            ],
            "content": "EWC 2025 Week 1 Champions Recap\nJul 14, 2025.\nForty clubs. Only one champion. Catch up on the Club Championship leaderboard here!\nJul 14, 2025.\nForty clubs. Only one champion. Catch up on the Club Championship leaderboard here!\nThe first week of Esports World Cup 2025 concluded with epic finals in Rennsport, FATAL FURY: City of the Wolves, VALORANT, and Apex Legends. As the second week kicks off, it’s the perfect time to catch up on the Club Championship leaderboard.\nOf these Club Championship ranking leaders, Gen.G is the organization competing in the most titles, with seven teams participating throughout the Esports World Cup. That gives them a better chance of finishing the entire event with a higher ranking.\nOther organizations, including Team Redline and VK Gaming, are only competing in one tournament, making it unlikely they’ll remain high on the Club Championship ranking beyond the first week of competition. Stay tuned for next week’s ranking.\nComing into the Esports World Cup 2025, Team Redline entered as title defenders. Every other squad wanted to leave them in the dust – but none could. The storied organization continued to write their names in history, building one of the first EWC dynasties.\nTeam Redline is the Club of the Week.\nAfter falling behind 2-0 in the VALORANT at EWC 25 finals, Team Heretics’ Mert \"Wo0t\" Alkan became the spark that lit a fiery comeback. His electrifying plays on Icebox – where he had an incredible 26-kill statline – and Haven gave his squad hope, turning the tide and shattering Fnatic’s chances at tournament glory.\nWo0t is this week’s Player of the Week.\nTeam Redline made history on July 11th by becoming the first EWC 25 champions and the first back-to-back winners of Rennsport at the tournament, after also taking home the 2024 edition trophy. The racing squad dominated track after track, defeating teams like Porsche Coanda and Team Vitality before crossing the finish line.\nLuke Bennett from Team Redline became the Sony MVP thanks to his multiple podium finishes, taking home an extra $10,000 in addition to his share of Team Redline’s $200,000 prize. This victory added another major achievement to Team Redline’s 20-year legacy.\nMeanwhile, Goichi \"GO1\" Kishida represented DetonatioN FocusMe well with Kain against Zeng \"Xiaohai\" Zhuojun’s Billy in the grand final of FATAL FURY: COTW at EWC 25. He earned $300,000 and cemented his place in history. Although GO1 was a strong contender going into the tournament, he was not the favorite to win. However, he proved every doubter wrong by combining his signature defensive style with instinct to take out Xiaohai.\nTeam Heretics earned the trophy for VALORANT at EWC 2025 after a reverse sweep against Fnatic. The tournament was tough, with early exits from top teams like G2 and 100 Thieves. After placing last at VCT Masters Toronto, Team Heretics regrouped with something to prove. In-game leader Mert \"Wo0t\" Alkan led their comeback in the finals, which also resulted in his own MVP award of $10,000 in extra prize money.\nClosing out the first week of finals was VK Gaming taking the crown at the ALGS Midseason Playoffs at EWC 2025. VK Gaming weren’t the favorites compared to teams like Alliance, Team Falcons, and Ninjas in Pyjamas, but they had resilience and consistency on their side while under pressure.\nThis victory earned the team an impressive $600,000 and a spot in the upcoming ALGS Championship in Sapporo. It also put the APAC South region firmly in the spotlight as a region that should never be underestimated. As the Sony MVP, Chen \"QQ\" Yin-hong also earned a $10,000 bonus.\nWith hundreds of matches set to take place over the next few days, new legends will rise as champions are crowned and rivals are toppled. Watch the Esports World Cup 2025 live or check out the ticket options to watch in-person this week!",
            "content_length": 3856
        },
        {
            "article_id": "68fb4dbc4a1bdbd3",
            "title": "The highest-earning Valorant esports players - Esports Charts",
            "published_at": "Sun, 08 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports Charts",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidEFVX3lxTFBsZDJwTzZTY3Z2bEVpZjhRaEE4QTJqakQ4UFB0NlotM1ZRV3VyOG8zMC1TeU5KYlFoZnZfZ2czYzFkMGU3a1ZnUFBFUk1VenMzaGJQRXlXa0RBTVloUVZ5NGs1YnpPdlZUcnhvdmtxTGFXa001?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://escharts.com/news/highest-earning-valorant-esports-players",
            "query_players": [
                "Alfajer",
                "aspas",
                "CHICHOO",
                "Chronicle",
                "Ethan",
                "jawgemo"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Alfajer",
                "aspas",
                "BerLIN",
                "Boaster",
                "Boostio",
                "C0M",
                "CHICHOO",
                "Chronicle",
                "Derke",
                "Ethan",
                "jawgemo",
                "NaturE",
                "nobody",
                "s0m",
                "stellar",
                "t3xture"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant has exploded onto the esports scene, rapidly becoming one of the most-watched and competitive games in the world. Its unique blend of Counter-Strike-style tactical shooting and character-based abilities has captivated a global audience, fostering a professional scene ripe with high-stakes tournaments and massive prize pools. From the global Valorant Champions Tour’s (VCT) International Leagues (IL) to prestigious Masters events, the competition has enabled a new generation of esports professionals to achieve fame and financial success.\nIn this article, we will take a deep dive into the top 10 highest-earning Valorant players. We’ll explore their careers, celebrate their landmark victories, and break down the major tournament wins that have propelled them to the top of the financial leaderboards.\nIt is important to note that the figures presented here are based solely on tournament prize winnings. They do not include other significant income sources such as team salaries, sponsorships, or individual streaming revenue, which are not publicly disclosed. These players have earned their spots through sheer competitive results.\nTop 10 Highest-Earning Valorant Players\n \n \nA significant portion of the top earners hail from the Americas and EMEA regions, with the 2023 Evil Geniuses championship roster and the consistently dominant Fnatic lineup featuring heavily throughout the top 10.\nThe members of the Evil Geniuses squad owe a massive portion of their earnings to a stellar, underdog 2023 season. Their run culminated in a spectacular victory at Valorant Champions 2023, where they secured the grand prize of $1,000,000. This single event catapulted them to the top of the earnings list. Their second-place finish at VCT 2023: Masters Tokyo, where they lost to Fnatic, further padded their winnings.\nThe European powerhouse Fnatic was consistently a title contender, throughout much of the VCT’s history, leading to high earnings for its core players. Their victories at VCT 2023: LOCK//IN São Paulo and Masters Tokyo were monumental in securing them hundreds of thousands of dollars in prize money and cementing their legacy as one of Valorant's most successful teams.\nProfiles of the top Valorant players by tournament winnings\n#10 - CHICHOO ($256,296)\nWan \"CHICHOO\" Shunzhi has been a foundational piece of China's most formidable Valorant team, EDward Gaming. His meticulous and disciplined playstyle has been the bedrock upon which EDG built their domestic dynasty. Before the official VCT China League was established, EDG were the undisputed kings of their region, a reputation they carried onto the world stage.\nCHICHOO and his squad first made major international waves in late 2022, however, it was in 2023 that they truly cemented their status as global contenders. A significant portion of CHICHOO's earnings comes from their incredible lower bracket run at Masters Tokyo 2023, where they eliminated top-tier opponents and proved China was a region to be feared.\nOf course, most of CHICHOO’s prize money came from Valorant Champions 2024, where EDward Gaming claimed their first world championship. As a calm and collected presence, CHICHOO’s mastery of Sentinels agents has been crucial in enabling the explosive plays of his more aggressive teammates, making him one of the most reliable and respected players in his role.\n#9 - Alfajer ($257,257)\nEmir \"Alfajer\" Beder was the unshakable backbone of Fnatic’s championship-winning roster, bringing a level of consistency and composure rarely seen in a young player. Joining the team in 2022 at just 17 years old, the Turkish prodigy quickly made a name for himself as one of the premier Sentinel and flex players in the world. His clutch potential, impeccable aim, and ability to hold sites under immense pressure made him an irreplaceable pillar of Fnatic’s success.\n \n \nIn 2023, Alfajer’s steady performances were crucial during Fnatic’s legendary back-to-back international victories at the 2023 LOCK//IN and Masters Tokyo. His ability to thrive on high-impact agents like Killjoy and Cypher cemented him as one of the best defensive minds in the game.\nAlfajer has been a crucial part of Fnatic's 2025 successes, adjusting to the role needed from him on each map. At Masters Toronto, he ended the event as one of the highest-rated players of the entire tournament and recorded the second-highest Average Combat Score, just behind Gen.G's Kim \"t3xture\" Na-ra. His raw firepower has been a driving force of Fnatic when the hours are darkest and someone needs to step up.\n#8 - nobody ($257,540)\nA longtime partner-in-crime to CHICHOO, Wang \"nobody\" Senxu has been the versatile Initiator and in-game leader at the core of EDward Gaming's success. Known for his intelligent use of utility, nobody consistently delivers high-impact plays that swing the momentum in his team's favor. His journey mirrors that of his team: from absolute regional dominance to becoming a feared dark horse at international tournaments.\nWhile CHICHOO sets the strategic foundation, nobody is often the catalyst for action. His performances at global VCT events demonstrated his world-class talent. He was instrumental in EDG’s victories over established teams, showcasing a level of mechanical skill and game sense that rivaled the best Initiators in the world. His earnings are a testament not only to EDG's domestic championships but also to their success in placing high at the most lucrative international events, where he has repeatedly proven he is far from a \"nobody\" on the server.\n#7 - aspas ($261,619)\nErick \"aspas\" Santos is a Brazilian force of nature, a superstar Duelist who became a global icon with the legendary LOUD roster of 2022. Crucially, he’s the only entrant on this ranking to be here without any of his teammates. Known for his flashy mechanics and an almost supernatural ability to win unwinnable fights, aspas embodied the ‘duelist diff’ that led his team to glory. After rising through the ranks of the Brazilian scene, he joined LOUD and immediately began a campaign of dominance.\nThe pinnacle of his career came at Valorant Champions 2022, where aspas put on a masterclass performance, leading LOUD to the world championship title and securing the $300,000 first-place prize. His team's near-flawless run through the tournament cemented his legacy as one of the greatest Duelists to ever touch the game.\nFollowing this success, he made a high-profile move to Leviatán for 2023, becoming the centerpiece of a new Latin American super-team. Unfortunately, things didn’t work out for him with the team, and he has since moved to represent Brazilian organization MIBR for the 2025 season. His earnings reflect not just a world championship but consistent high placements across numerous international and regional events.\n#6 - jawgemo ($264,066)\nAlexander \"jawgemo\" Mor carved his name into Valorant history as the aggressive and tenacious Controller for Evil Geniuses. He originally competed for several smaller North American teams before finding his home with EG. Initially, the 2023 Evil Geniuses roster was not considered a serious contender, struggling in the domestic VCT Americas league.\nHowever, the team's fortunes turned dramatically mid-season. Jawgemo’s fearless and confident playstyle perfectly complemented the team's high-pressure, chaotic tactics. He and his teammates embarked on a legendary underdog story, first securing a hard-fought second place at VCT Masters Tokyo, then carrying that momentum to a Champions title.\n#5 - Boostio ($276,137)\nKelden \"Boostio\" Pupello established himself as Valorant's ultimate champion IGL as the fiery, unapologetically confident leader of the 2023 Evil Geniuses roster. Never afraid to engage in trash talk and exuding an unshakable belief in his team, Boostio's leadership was the engine behind one of esports' greatest underdog stories. He guided a team that was initially written off, fostering a powerful \"us against the world\" mentality.\nThis mental fortitude was on full display during their miraculous runs at Masters Tokyo & Valorant Champions 2023. Boostio's sharp mid-round calls and ability to empower his teammates to play their aggressive style were critical to their success. After conquering the Valorant world, Boostio began a new chapter in his career, bringing his championship experience and vocal leadership to 100 Thieves.\n#4 - Ethan ($301,267)\nEthan \"Ethan\" Arnold brought a champion’s pedigree to Valorant, having already established himself as a top-tier professional in Counter-Strike with top teams like NRG and Evil Geniuses. His transition to Valorant saw him first join 100 Thieves, but it was with the 2023 Evil Geniuses roster that he became a world champion.\nAs the team's primary Initiator, Ethan's veteran poise, flawless utility usage, and calm demeanor were the perfect stabilizing force for his younger, more aggressive teammates. He provided the crucial support and experience needed to navigate the immense pressure of high-stakes international matches.\nAfter the championship, Ethan joined NRG, where he has competed among other former Counter-Strike professionals like Sam “S0m” Oh and Pujan “FNS” Mehta.\n#3 - Boaster ($304,422)\nJake \"Boaster\" Howlett is more than just Fnatic's in-game leader; he is the team's strategic mastermind and charismatic heart. Famous for his elaborate walkouts, infectious positivity, and brilliant tactical mind, Boaster has been with the roster since its inception under the SUMN FC banner, guiding them through their evolution into a global powerhouse. He endured the team's early \"so close, yet so far\" moments, using them as fuel to build a resilient and mentally tough roster.\nBoaster is celebrated for his creative anti-stratting and his ability to adapt mid-game, often out-thinking the best in-game leaders from around the world during their magical 2023 run. The substantial prize money he has earned is a direct result of his unwavering dedication and his transformation of Fnatic from a top contender into a legendary, title-winning dynasty.\nFor 2025, Boaster has re-built Fnatic’s roster after losing Derke in late 2024, and Leo \"Leo\" Jannesson, who moved to the bench in mid-2024 due to health reasons. With new fresh talent, Boaster has already claimed a VCT EMEA title in 2025, proving he still can lead teams to the top of the game. Mid-season results for 2025 included two 2nd places at Masters Toronto and the EWC, both of which boosted his ranking here greatly.\n#2 - C0M ($327,367)\nSecond at the top, Valorant's second-highest-earning player is Corbin \"C0M\" Lee, the Initiator and sometimes smokes for the world-champion Evil Geniuses squad. C0M is another Counter-Strike professional who made the jump to Valorant, quickly building an ability to perform at the highest level.\nC0M’s earnings skyrocketed during the 2023 EG season. He was a critical component in EG's surprising run to the Masters Tokyo finals and was instrumental in their subsequent domination at Valorant Champions 2023.\nProving his own personal talent, and boosting his total winnings, C0M has since become an integral part of Leviatán’s roster. The American brings a wealth of experience to the Latin American team, and he helped them not only claim a VCT Americas title in 2024, but also a third-place finish at Champions.\n#1 - Chronicle ($328,572)\nTimofey \"Chronicle\" Khromov holds a legendary and unique status in Valorant esports: he is the only player to have won multiple Masters trophies with two different teams. His journey to the top began with the iconic Gambit Esports roster. As their incredibly versatile flex player, he was a key factor in their victory at VCT 2021: Stage 3 Masters Berlin and their near-miss in the grand finals of the inaugural Valorant Champions 2021.\nHis blockbuster transfer to Fnatic for the 2023 season was seen as the move that would create a super-team, and Chronicle delivered immediately. He was immediately hailed as the final piece of the puzzle, the calm and versatile veteran who could elevate the team from contenders to champions. He helped Fnatic secure their back-to-back titles in 2023, and his top-three ranking reflects his incredibly unique career and legacy. \n \nFittingly, Chronicle's strong regional results and international showings with Fnatic in 2025 have seen him fly ahead of the competition and claim the top spot as the highest-earning Valorant player ever.",
            "content_length": 12433
        },
