        {
            "article_id": "75011464ee5bf70f",
            "title": "NRG halt reverse sweep versus Fnatic to win VALORANT Champions 2025 - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Sun, 05 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAFBVV95cUxNMU45WndzcDZJT05KS3pfejhWWjR3Y2NLc2YyS0licGJ4RlZJeGtTbGJOQS1FUFdBMzNReWpfanBBSld0dEZMMHJzXzk0R0ZzZ2xkaU8tS2djOUdrRi0wcVFVOUJkUkVYVGhvU01LNnRSN01WYlJhQUIxWlgwUVc5cDRkYmJMTjNK?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/nrg-beat-valorant-champions-2025-vs-fnatic",
            "query_players": [
                "brawk"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Alfajer",
                "Boaster",
                "brawk",
                "Chronicle",
                "ComeBack",
                "crashies",
                "kaajak",
                "mada",
                "NaturE",
                "stellar",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Americas second seed NRG have won VALORANT Champions Paris. After crushing Fnatic to take a 2-0 lead, a reverse sweep seemed imminent when the EMEA side levelled at 2-2. But NRG held on, convincingly winning the decider to complete a 3-2 series victory.\nThe first few rounds on the opener were cagey, but soon NRG began to build momentum with eight consecutive round wins. At the half, NRG led 9-3 — the Americas team with yet another superb start on the Accor Arena stage, and Fnatic with work to do once again.\nThe second half didn’t bring a change in proceedings. NRG quickly closed 13-3 without dropping a round in the second half, starting the grand final 1-0 up. Brawk’s infamous Odin was a nightmare for Fnatic. He ended the map 24/7/2.\nNRG’s momentum continued into map two, beginning with three round wins before Fnatic could find their first. Kaajak aside, Fnatic’s usual hitters were extremely quiet, an untimely drop in form from the likes of Alfajer and Chronicle, who had been so stellar throughout the event prior.\nThe rounds kept coming for NRG, and two first-half timeouts from Fnatic saw them desperately trying to halt their opponents’ momentum. Even with a 4v2 advantage in round nine, Fnatic could not close things — NRG could do no wrong.\nOnce again, the Americas second seed had a 9-3 lead at the switch. This time, though, Fnatic won the next round, beating their tally from the first map, at the very least. Then a 1v2 clutch from veteran IGL Boaster closed the round after, and, finally, Fnatic had reason to believe.\nBut NRG were having none of it, quickly shutting down any comeback attempt to win 13-6; one map away from being crowned as VALORANT Champions.\nKaajak was the best performer, despite Fnatic’s struggles, ending 20/15/4, but mada too was on fire and brawk’s Odin a nuisance.\nQuickly in map three, it was looking like a miracle comeback was not to arrive. 11 round wins for NRG sandwiched the solitary round for FNATIC as bonkar’s team led 11-1. FNATIC again used both timeouts within the first half, desperate to get their mental into a state where they’d be capable of competing. And somehow they did.\nFinally, at a point where you’d have bet your house in NRG closing out, Fnatic woke up. Alfa woke up. Chron woke up. Crashies woke up.\nRound after round started going their way, and the momentum did not stop despite NRG nicking one to reach Championship point.\nEventually, with the crowd behind them, Fnatic took it to overtime, causing an eruption in Paris. Though NRG fought back, dragging on what was now the inevitable, Fanatic ended it at 15-13 to complete the greatest single-map comeback in VCT global history.\nNRG did well to keep their cool into Ascent, despite their complete capitulation on the third map, starting with a handful of opening round victories. But this time Fnatic answered back quicker, and as soon as they did, the crowd was again behind them.\nThe Black and Orange went from 0-3 to 2-3, still behind, but very quickly triggering an early timeout from their opponents. It was NRG’s turn to try and stem early momentum, a clear sign that the nature of the series had shifted, if it wasn’t obvious already.\nThe timeout failed, Fnatic won the next round. Then another. And another. For the first map in the series, they managed a lead at half time, 8-4 up at the interval.\nWhile there was some back and forth, Fnatic held on, winning the map 13-8 to level the tie 2-2 and take it to a third map. Alfajer, having been a passenger through most of the series, was finally playing like his usual self, ending the map 23/14/4\nWhile they’d have been forgiven for crumbling completely on the receiving end of such a turnaround, NRG started strongly on Sunset, the decider. Their 4-2 start led to another early timeout for Fnatic, who went back to being in a round deficit, as they had been through the majority of the BO5.\nThat deficit only increased; by halftime NRG were up 9-3. Fnatic’s momentum had totally dissipated and did not return. In the back half the European representatives could only find two more rounds as NRG closed to win 13-5, completing the series and being crowned world champions.\nHis Odin shenanigans on full display again today, brawk was crowned MVP of the tournament after leading his side to a VALORANT Champions trophy. The American player finished 84/61/40 with an ACS of 244 and +23 differential.\nAn honorable mention goes to kaajak, who would’ve surely gained the title had Fnatic won on the day, as he earned a 90/75/14 scoreline.",
            "content_length": 4523
        },
        {
            "article_id": "e01274a4e7ec3ce2",
            "title": "NRG Esports triumphs at VALORANT Champions 2025 in Paris: Early viewership insights - Esports Charts",
            "published_at": "Sun, 05 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports Charts",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMidEFVX3lxTE1EN2RBUEtSaGZuYXpURXcteUZRTmlXMUlwTlAtekR4dVJrdlhadzBrVDdpSVRSa0tTMGZDSUl2QlYzdF95cThPS3B5cHVnSm4zbXJTcE5MaGFjRHNQbjBfRnpKUi1nSTFkWVJvUjAwSmhtdTVa?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://escharts.com/news/valorant-champions-2025-early-viewership",
            "query_players": [
                "Ethan"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Ethan",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "NRG Esports triumphs at VALORANT Champions 2025 in Paris: Early viewership insights\nVALORANT Champions 2025 — the final event of this year’s Valorant esports season — has officially wrapped up in Paris. NRG Esports claimed the title after a 3:2 victory over Fnatic in the grand final. The atmosphere was electric, with the Accor Arena filled to the brim with fans, though the tournament’s livestream viewership was close to hit record highs. Esports Charts has shared the first insights from the event.\nHeading into the tournament, few analysts saw NRG Esports as a serious contender. Only 3.9% of Pick’Em predicted NRG as the winner. The team had a fairly modest track record in Valorant and had never cracked the top three at international events. The lone exception was team captain Ethan “Ethan” Arnold, who previously won the VALORANT Champions 2023 title with Evil Geniuses.\nTROPHY LIFT MOMENT FOR @NRGgg #VALORANTChampions pic.twitter.com/FdNTmnAoDt\n— VALORANT Champions Tour (@ValorantEsports) October 5, 2025\nDuring the group stage, NRG cruised past EDward Gaming and DRX to top their group with ease. In the playoffs, Ethan’s squad made quick work of GIANTX before grinding out a tough overtime victory against MIBR. They then confidently took down Fnatic in the upper bracket final — and did it again in the grand final to secure the championship. With this win, Ethan became the first player in VALORANT Champions history to lift the trophy twice — and with two different teams.\nVALORANT Champions 2025 Viewership Statistics\nThe organizers of VALORANT Champions 2025 went all out to create an amazing experience for fans at the Accor Arena. Attendees enjoyed a lively musical opening ceremony and a striking stage setup. Riot Games also unveiled a new agent, Veto, ahead of the grand final — set to launch in-game on October 7.\nPOV: You're at the #VALORANTChampions Grand Finals pic.twitter.com/ZFlcL90o0u\n— VALORANT Champions Tour (@ValorantEsports) October 5, 2025\nPreliminary data shows that the tournament’s peak online audience just topped 1.47 million concurrent viewers. The grand final drew the largest crowd, outpaced the Paper Rex vs. Fnatic match on the fourth day of the playoffs. While the full viewership analysis is still pending, it’s already clear that the tournament didn’t quite meet expectations when it comes to audience numbers.\nA big part of VALORANT Champions 2025’s viewership struggles came down to the finalists. The tournament notably missed out on NRG fans and North American viewers, with the grand final kicking off around 4 a.m. in Los Angeles. Meanwhile, fans in China and the Pacific regions had little incentive to tune in, since no local teams were playing in the final.\nVALORANT Champions 2025 saw 2nd peaks in online viewership. Across all three metrics, VALORANT Champions 2022 remains the most-watched tournament. Full statistics for all VCT season events are available on the report page.",
            "content_length": 2941
        },
        {
            "article_id": "445c7aed496bf182",
            "title": "All VCT and Valorant Champions winners in history - Dexerto",
            "published_at": "Sun, 05 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Dexerto",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilgFBVV95cUxQeDI4YmlpUlRWcWE0U2tkSC0weFBwU0JiZkxFUDdGT1FwTi1GNTI0dDZWQTFSQmJhUmVJSjE0TGlSMk5Dbk1zdFcxekFUZ1FWOUdzbEVwbkFLbXVPM09xQS1OVWQ2ckFmMzBGM2M5anZSZ0tIX2F0SDNWWTViTGltbUF1TnN6WF81Nk1sUjc3emx5dDlXLWc?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.dexerto.com/valorant/all-vct-and-valorant-champions-winners-in-history-2186894/",
            "query_players": [
                "Chronicle",
                "Meteor"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "BerLIN",
                "Chronicle",
                "Life",
                "Meteor",
                "TenZ"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant has seen multiple stars and exciting international tournaments come out of its esports circuit, and a new team of all-time greats has just been crowned at Valorant Champions. Check out which teams & players have taken home Valorant’s biggest internationals here.\nInternational Valorant Champions Tour tournaments are the pinnacle of the esport. Teams are always gunning to make VCT Masters or Valorant Champions every year to cement their legacy against international opponents, win prize money, and take home a trophy.\nValorant esports is still in its infancy compared to other top-tier competitive titles, like CS:GO and League of Legends. However, multiple teams have already managed to win international titles in the esport’s short life span as Riot Games has hosted several events across the world.\nValorant esports has gone through some changes due to the maturation of the competitive circuit and the introduction of its partnered leagues. Generally, teams have three chances to lift an international trophy within a calendar year, with two VCT Masters events and Valorant Champions to end the season.\nFollowing the Valorant Champions Paris Grand Finals between NRG and Fnatic, NRG were crowned champions, taking home the trophy in a hard-fought 3-2 victory.\nHere’s every Valorant winner in history as we head into an exciting 2025.\nAll VCT and Valorant Champions winners\nVCT and Valorant Champions winners by number of trophies\nFnatic star Timofey ‘Chronicle’ Khromov holds the record for most international trophies won with three. In June 2023, he lifted the VCT Masters Tokyo trophy, adding it to the VCT 2021 Masters Berlin (with Gambit) and the VCT LOCK//IN 2023 successes.\nRelated\nOther than Chronicle’s Fnatic teammates, Sacy and TenZ, were the only players in the Valorant scene who have managed to win more than one international trophy before Meteor’s run at Masters Bangkok 2025.",
            "content_length": 1908
        },
        {
            "article_id": "6d17498f0ca60687",
            "title": "VALORANT Kicks off Season 2025 // Act I with Tejo, New Initiator from Colombia - Games Press",
            "published_at": "Sun, 01 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Games Press",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMingFBVV95cUxOQjh1MEFqLWlrMVZBV2ptaUNaaV8wVVdLZWVIdTEycEtMX3pyZ0pZanRrODhvb0gwZ04wTTAxX3RaQ3QyQzlBMXlLZzVFX1A3UVFZVEIwUTJaZU1ZTU90V3ExTnlvVEF2NFhNb2dGRHhVRHN1akdscVpfZUFDdWFTMWRobEJFS1NOTUJYc2s4dHdXZDJYcDRhelBndnVyQQ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.gamespress.com/VALORANT-Kicks-off-Season-2025-Act-I-with-Tejo-New-Initiator-from-Colo",
            "query_players": [
                "Kicks"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Kicks",
                "stellar"
            ],
            "content": "Riot Games and VALORANT today revealed Tejo, a veteran intelligence consultant from Colombia. Tejo’s ballistic guidance system pressures the enemy to relinquish their ground – or their lives. His targeted strikes keep opponents off balance and under his heel. Tejo’s abilities include:\nSpecial Delivery\nEQUIP a sticky grenade. FIRE to launch. The grenade sticks to the first surface it hits and explodes, Concussing any targets caught in the blast. ALT FIRE to launch the grenade with a single bounce instead.\nStealth Drone\nEQUIP a stealth drone. FIRE to throw the drone forward, assuming direct control of its movement. FIRE again to trigger a pulse that Suppresses and Reveals enemies hit.\nGuided Salvo\nEQUIP an AR targeting system. FIRE to select up to two target locations on the map. ALT FIRE to launch missiles that autonomously navigate to target locations, detonating on arrival.\n(ULT) Armageddon\nEQUIP a tactical strike targeting map. FIRE to select the origin point of the strike. FIRE again to set the end point and launch the attack, unleashing a wave of explosions along the strike path. ALT FIRE during map targeting to cancel the origin point.\nVALORANT is also introducing Flex, the first new content type since launch in the form of handheld objects that gives players more ways to express themselves in-game. It is equipped via Collections and appear in game in a player’s Spray Wheel. To celebrate the launch of Flex, players will receive a free Flex with a second Flex available in the Season 2025 // Act I Battlepass.\nThis act also brings players a new skinline, EX.O. Inspired by futuristic and sci-fi themes, weapons included in EX.O are the Vandal, Sheriff, Spectre, Outlaw and Katana – the EX.O Edge. The all new Battlepass will feature items including the Celestia Ghost, Epilogue: Tactical Spacewar Card, Hieroscape Blades (Dual Melee), Moon Scout Outlaw, Scaredy Cow Gun Buddy and Stellar Dendrite Flex.\nSeason 2025 // Act I begins January 8, 2025 with EX.O available to players on January 9.\nABOUT VALORANT\nVALORANT is a competitive 5v5 character-based first-person tactical shooter developed and published by Riot Games for PC, Xbox Series X|S and PlayStation®5. VALORANT is a precise and lethal multiplayer game with high-fidelity gunplay, a diverse arsenal of guns, agents with unique abilities, and competitively tuned maps for unlimited hours of play. In VALORANT, players lock in their agent for the entire duration of the match and then attempt to win a round-based, attackers vs. defenders, best-of-24-rounds fight.\nVALORANT is a free-to-play game and optimized to perform on a wide variety of PC and console platforms, allowing for a global audience to compete. Demonstrated by the game’s best-in-class game server quality, global infrastructure, and proprietary anti-cheat technology, Riot Games is committed to preserving the core tenets of the tactical shooter genre and upholding the competitive integrity of the gameplay experience across all available platforms.",
            "content_length": 3005
        },
        {
            "article_id": "2a9084f91e228884",
            "title": "Pulsar Susanto-X Review – Lightweight Esports Mouse Designed with f0rsakeN and Xccurate - xiaomitoday.com",
            "published_at": "Sat, 30 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "xiaomitoday.com",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirwFBVV95cUxNRDRDSGs2VktxdjZKMm1icnR4Wm5wN1czS1pZLUI4cUQ1ck1aS3ZiU0tMcjQ0T3FCemVFR01TcWp3OGswSWVDUzlQclFpMVp6dmdaUWI1bGF0Y0lKanBvVXEzMVA2WXNPcTVIMnpZRGRvVlVNN0lQb2l5T0JTU21mbEVzRGdISTRJRVNtYUVQc0hTSnZWTHRsU09DYnFQRUhHN0w1ci1CMmQ1ckpwcWFz?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://xiaomitoday.com/pulsar-susanto-x-review-lightweight-esports-mouse-designed-with-f0rsaken-and-xccurate/",
            "query_players": [
                "f0rsakeN"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Click",
                "Elite",
                "f0rsakeN",
                "Life",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "The Pulsar Susanto-X gaming mouse enters the market as more than just another lightweight design. Born from the collaboration of professional players f0rsakeN of Paper Rex and Xccurate of T1, it represents a unique project shaped directly by the demands of competitive esports. Unlike conventional models built around general user data, this mouse was refined around the brothers’ exact grip styles, hand sizes, and playing preferences. Their rivalry and shared drive pushed the development process, resulting in a product that blends performance engineering with the practical needs of high-level play.\nThe Susanto-X takes on the growing trend of ultra-light mice while maintaining durability and structure. The symmetrical shell, carefully adjusted grip angle, and curvature are designed to minimize fatigue while maximizing control during extended sessions. Pulsar pairs this with its XS-1 flagship sensor, support for up to 8,000 Hz polling, and optical switches rated for 100 million clicks. On paper, these specifications place the Susanto-X firmly within the upper tier of competitive gaming mice, where precision and reliability can often define performance outcomes.\nIn this review, I’ll put the Susanto-X through a series of real-world tests covering both competitive titles and general use scenarios. Beyond raw specifications, the focus will be on responsiveness, tracking stability, build quality, and long-term comfort. The goal is to see whether Pulsar has successfully translated professional input into a mouse that not only meets the standards of elite players but also delivers consistent value for a broader audience of gamers seeking dependable gear.\nSpecifications\nUnboxing\nThe Pulsar Susanto-X gaming mouse comes in packaging that reflects its competitive design focus with a matte black finish and a centered image of the mouse, showing its compact shape, black body, and pink accents. The front includes the “Pulsar Pro Series” logo at the top, the “Susanto-X” name in bold pink at the bottom, and a note crediting the Susanto Brothers for the design, along with a holographic signature that adds a personal detail. On the back, the specifications are clearly listed in white text, followed by a short description of the Pro Series philosophy, which highlights input from professional players and focuses on practical elements like shape, weight, and performance rather than marketing.\nOn the right side of the box, the mouse is displayed in black with pink accents, alongside hand diagrams showing claw and relaxed claw grips. This makes it clear how the design supports different grip styles, especially for competitive games like Valorant. On the left side, the emphasis shifts to its collaboration with Kevin “Xccurate” and Jason “f0rsakeN” Susanto from Paper Rex. Their photos, autographs, and short background story highlight their journey from rivals to teammates, tying the product to professional-level gaming use.\nTurning the Pulsar Susanto-X wireless gaming mouse box over shows a clean bottom panel with the main specifications clearly listed. Inside, the mouse is held in place by a compact plastic insert, keeping the unboxing simple and tidy. The mouse itself comes in a matte black finish with pink accents, carrying the “Susanto-X” branding designed by the Susanto Brothers for competitive play.\nThe box includes the wireless gaming mouse finished in black with a pink Pulsar logo on the scroll wheel, designed in collaboration with Valorant professionals f0rsaken and xccurate. Alongside the mouse, there is a pink paracord USB-C cable for charging or wired use and a pink 8K wireless dongle that ensures responsive, low-latency connectivity. Pulsar also adds 16 UHMW-PE dot skates, giving players the option to fine-tune glide and handling. Completing the package is a signed “f0rsaken X” card featuring the Susanto brothers, together with a custom user manual that ties the collaboration together.\nDesign\nThe Pulsar Susanto-X wireless gaming mouse comes in a compact, symmetrical design with dimensions of 118.7 x 62.3 x 38.6 mm. It is well-suited for medium-sized hands and works best with claw or fingertip grip styles. At only 50 grams, it is lightweight and easy to move while still feeling solid in use. The matte black shell has a smooth finish that resists fingerprints and stays comfortable during long sessions. Its shape supports both right- and left-handed users, offering a natural feel that adapts easily to different grip preferences. Claw and relaxed claw grips in particular benefit from the balance between control and comfort.\nIn terms of design, the mouse features pink accents on the scroll wheel, side buttons, and base rim, along with a “Susanto Brothers” logo on the side to mark the collaboration. The build quality is firm with no flex or creaking in the shell, and the buttons provide sharp, consistent clicks. These details give the Susanto-X a dependable character, pairing a clean look with practical features that make it suitable for extended gaming use.\nThe left and right buttons feature a gentle inward curve that helps the fingers rest naturally, improving grip and control. Both are fitted with Pulsar’s 100M-click optical switches, which provide light, responsive clicks with consistent tactile feedback. During use, the buttons showed no pre-travel or side movement, and inputs registered quickly, even with repeated presses. The right button is marked with the “Sustano Brothers” branding, giving it a small but noticeable design detail. The main buttons perform reliably and are well-suited for both everyday use and competitive gaming.\nThe scroll wheel on the Pulsar Susanto-X is designed with a black rubberized surface and a pink zigzag pattern that improves grip while matching the overall look of the mouse. In testing, it provided smooth movement with clear, stepped feedback, making it useful for switching weapons in games or scrolling through menus. Pulsar’s Blue Encoder keeps the wheel precise and quiet, holding up well during longer sessions. It’s a practical addition that blends everyday usability with a distinct visual detail unique to this model.\nThe left side of the mouse includes an indicator LED along with two pink side buttons placed just above the grip line, positioned for quick access during gameplay. Next to them, the “Forsaken” signature appears in black, giving the design a distinct touch. On the right side, the surface is smooth, and a pink accent along the base edge and the “Xlu” signature complete the look.\nThe mouse includes a top-mounted USB Type-C port that lets it charge while in use. When plugged in, it shifts to wired mode with a standard 1000 Hz polling rate, but it also supports up to 8000 Hz for quicker input and improved responsiveness during fast-paced gaming.\nThe bottom of the Pulsar Susanto-X is arranged with a focus on usability and a tidy finish. Its pink base gives the mouse a distinct identity, while the centrally located DPI button and power switch are placed for easy access. The white PTFE feet deliver a smooth glide across a variety of mousepads, supporting precise tracking during play.\nPulsar’s 8K Wireless Dongle\nThe Pulsar 8K Wireless Dongle makes a strong first impression with its translucent pink shell, a refreshing change from the standard black or gray peripherals. At 24.3 mm x 35.5 mm x 19.4 mm, the dongle is compact enough to fit cleanly into a USB port without taking up unnecessary space. The see-through housing reveals the internal components, giving it a unique look, while the “Susanto Brothers” logo on both sides highlights its design origin. It feels well-built with a smooth surface, though the glossy finish may show fingerprints over time. A USB-C port is positioned on one end for firmware updates, and the included black base keeps the dongle stable when not in use.\nIn daily use, the dongle blends style with utility. Its size and shape allow it to sit neatly alongside other devices, minimizing any chance of blocking adjacent ports. The lightweight pink casing feels durable and is designed with portability in mind, while setup requires only a single dongle connection, reducing the chance of signal conflicts between devices. The black rubberized base provides a steady grip during longer sessions, though the small form factor means placement matters to avoid bumps. With its mix of design details and reliable function, the Pulsar 8K Wireless Dongle caters well to competitive gamers looking for both performance and a distinct look.\nFeatures and Performance\nThe Susanto-X is built around the XS-1 sensor, supporting up to 32,000 DPI, 750 IPS tracking, and 50g acceleration. With an 8000Hz polling rate and a 1ms response time, inputs register quickly and remain consistent, which is important in competitive play. A 32-bit ARM processor manages data flow to maintain accuracy during fast movement, reducing the chance of tracking loss under heavy load.\nThe sensor integrates PixArt’s Motion Sync, a feature that synchronizes signal output with the PC’s data extraction cycle. This minimizes delay and improves tracking precision, measured at under 1ms. Motion Sync can be toggled through the Pulsar Fusion Software, giving players control over how the sensor behaves.\nThree LOD options—2mm, 1mm, and 0.7mm—are available for players who prefer different sensor cut-off points. This helps maintain cursor accuracy when repositioning the mouse, particularly for low-DPI users.\nTurbo Mode fixes the sensor at 20,000 FPS, reducing initial micro-delays when movement begins. This ensures smooth pixel tracking from the start, which is valuable for precise aiming. The feature is tuned for low-DPI gameplay, maintaining stability across different DPI and polling rate settings.\nDPI can be adjusted in fine steps, polling rates can be configured, and button remapping with macros is supported through the software. This level of control makes the Susanto-X adaptable across different game types, from fast shooters to slower-paced strategy titles.\nThe mouse uses Pulsar optical switches rated for 100 million actuations, providing consistent actuation and long-term reliability. The Pulsar Blue encoder adds accurate scroll steps, which improve weapon switching and precision adjustments.\nIn testing, the mouse tracks smoothly across both rapid flicks and small aim corrections. The high polling rate reduces input delay, resulting in responsive control even in demanding situations. Combined with durable switches and broad customization options, the Susanto-X maintains steady performance for competitive gaming.\nConnectivity\nThe Susanto-X Mouse lets you switch between wired and 2.4GHz wireless modes with a simple toggle on the bottom. The 2.4G wireless connection uses the included 8K Wireless Dongle and provides a stable, low-latency link, making it suitable for gaming. Bluetooth isn’t supported, but the wireless performance remains consistent, whether for gaming or everyday tasks.\nIn wired mode, the mouse connects through a detachable USB-C cable that also charges the internal battery, so you can keep using it while it charges. Switching between wired and wireless is quick and reliable, and the mouse performs consistently across both modes. Its design and connectivity make it a practical choice for gaming setups as well as general use.\nPulsar’s Software\nPulsar’s software for the Susanto Wireless mouse is designed with a clean layout that makes setup and adjustments simple. The starting screen highlights the mouse with a clear label, allowing users to quickly access settings without distractions. Key options are divided into four sections—Main, Button Assignment, Performance, and Macro—covering everything from firmware updates and pairing to general system preferences. The Main tab also includes a battery level indicator, useful for keeping track of power status, while additional options such as startup behavior and quick access to Windows Mouse Properties provide practical control. Firmware reminders are also present, ensuring the device runs on the latest version for stable performance.\nThe Button Assignment tab provides full remapping of all inputs with a helpful visual layout of the mouse, making customization easy to follow. Features like Turbo Mode, Motion Sync, and adjustable debounce timing give players room to fine-tune their settings, while the Auto Sleep function helps extend battery life. The Performance tab expands control with six DPI stages reaching up to 32000 DPI, polling rates that scale up to 8,000 Hz, and adjustable lift-off distance. Sensitivity and double-click speed sliders further enhance precision, while the absence of lighting effects keeps the focus on performance features. Overall, the software delivers a functional set of tools that support both everyday use and competitive play.\nBattery and Charging\nThe Susanto-X comes with a 300mAh rechargeable battery, almost twice the size of what many other lightweight gaming mice offer. In testing, it handled long gaming sessions and regular desktop use easily, lasting around 5 to 7 days on a single charge, even with the 8K polling rate enabled. For a mouse this small and light, that kind of battery life is convenient, letting users focus on gaming without worrying about frequent recharges.\nCharging is done through a USB-C port and takes about two hours from empty to full. The battery performs well compared to larger wireless models, offering a good mix of power and efficiency. It keeps the mouse ready for extended gaming or work sessions, so running out of charge in the middle of use isn’t a concern.\nUse Experience – Daily Work and Gaming\nUsing the Pulsar Susanto-X daily over a two-week period showed how well it balances gaming performance with regular work. The mouse feels very light in the hand, making it easy to move across the desk with minimal effort. The symmetrical shape and low profile supported longer sessions without hand strain, which was noticeable during hours of browsing, typing, and multitasking between applications. The optical switches kept clicks consistent and sharp, whether it was for gaming inputs or routine productivity tasks.\nDuring desktop work, the PTFE feet provided a smooth glide on both cloth and hybrid pads. Even on a plain desk surface, the sensor tracked reliably without stutter or drift. Battery performance also held up well: with the polling rate set to 8,000 Hz, the mouse lasted nearly a week before charging was necessary. Dropping to 1,000 Hz stretched battery life even further, which is useful when the mouse is used more for work than gaming.\nIn competitive shooters such as Valorant and Counter-Strike 2, the Susanto-X delivered the precision expected from a product shaped by professional input. The XS-1 sensor maintained accurate tracking through fast flicks and smaller aim corrections, and the higher polling rate reduced input delay to a point where actions felt immediate. The grip angle and shell curvature gave reliable control during extended play, with no comfort issues after multiple hours.\nOutside of shooters, the mouse also performed well in strategy and MMO titles. Switching DPI profiles through Pulsar’s software allowed quick adjustments for different game types, while the side buttons were positioned within easy reach for assigning skills or commands. Throughout two weeks of use, the buttons and scroll wheel retained a firm, consistent feel, with no looseness or change in performance.\nAfter extended daily use, the Susanto-X proved to be dependable across both gaming and general work. Its lightweight, stable tracking, and efficient battery performance made it practical for day-to-day tasks, while its professional-level design translated effectively into competitive play. The mouse held up as a reliable option for users who divide their time between gaming and regular desktop activity.\nConclusion\nThe Pulsar Susanto-X Gaming Mouse is available now. For more information, visit the official Pulsar website. For detailed tech reviews, click here.",
            "content_length": 16043
        },
