            "article_id": "12cbeef603c25c75",
            "title": "Valorant PH’s Patmen goes abroad, joins PRX - Inquirer Esports",
            "published_at": "Mon, 17 Mar 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Inquirer Esports",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports.inquirer.net/35887/valorant-phs-patmen-goes-abroad-joins-prx",
            "query_players": [
                "PatMen"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "kellyS",
                "Papi",
                "PatMen"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant PH’s Patmen goes abroad, joins PRX\nEx-NAOS Esports player Patrick “PatMen” Mendoza has signed with Singaporean esports organization Paper Rex (PRX) as part of its roster for the coming Valorant Champions Tour (VCT) Pacific Stage 1.\nMendoza will serve as a sixth man after having a successful season last year with the previous Philippine-based team Zol Esports.\nAfter winning the VALORANT Challengers 2024 Philippines: Split 1 against NAOS Esports, Split 2 saw ZOL lose to the same team last July. Mendoza got his first Predator League trophy after defeating collegiate-based team De La Salle Viridus Arcus in the Predator League Philippines 2025.\n“As Valorant reaches its fifth year, it’s no surprise that the VCT circuit is more competitive than ever, and we must energize and reinvigorate ourselves,” Paper Rex chief gaming officer Harley “dsn” Örwall said in a statement.\n“An injection of new blood with young eyes brings fresh perspectives to our game. Adding PatMen to the PRX family allows us to widen our experiments with a greater breadth of play styles,” he added.\nThe sixth man will travel to Seoul after the necessary documents are acquired, and he will be the ninth Filipino to play actively in VCT Pacific.\nFellow Filipinos include the majority of the Global Esports squad that houses former NAOS Esports player Federico “Papi” Evangelista, Former Oasis Gaming Kelly “kellyS” Sedillo, and substitute Mark “patrickWHO” Musni.\nThe last six are the all-Filipino “Adobo gang” squad Team Secret.\nVCT 2025: Pacific Stage 1 will take place from March 22 to May and will feature 12 teams. Mendoza and the entire PRX squad will compete for a slot to play in VCT Masters 2: Toronto, which will be held in June.\nSubscribe to INQUIRER PLUS to get access to The Philippine Daily Inquirer & other 70+ titles, share up to 5 gadgets, listen to the news, download as early as 4am & share articles on social media. Call 896 6000.",
            "content_length": 1934
        },
        {
            "article_id": "d1afa108bf35cf17",
            "title": "Ludwig Reacts to MxS Roster Getting Poached by Partnered Teams - India Today Gaming",
            "published_at": "Mon, 17 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "India Today Gaming",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.indiatodaygaming.com/news/story/ludwig-reacts-to-mxs-roster-getting-poached-by-partnered-teams-7316",
            "query_players": [
                "brawk"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "brawk",
                "flyuh",
                "mada",
                "something",
                "v1c",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Ludwig Reacts to MxS Roster Getting Poached by Partnered Teams\nLudwig reacts as MxS loses top players to VCT-partnered teams, exposing the harsh reality of esports.\nThe esports scene is a ruthless battlefield—where survival doesn’t necessarily belong to the strongest, but to the wealthiest. Ludwig is now facing one of the harshest realities of running an organization: if you’re not a partnered team, you’re essentially a talent pipeline for those who are. Moist x Shopify Rebellion (MxS), the VALORANT squad co-owned by Ludwig and Shopify Rebellion, has seen its roster picked apart by VCT-partnered teams swooping in to claim its best players.\nMxS: A Talent Factory for Partnered Teams?\nFounded in September 2023, MxS was built on the combined efforts of Moist Esports and Shopify Rebellion, with the goal of fielding a competitive roster capable of challenging VCT Americas teams.\nFor a time, the project was a success. MxS proved to be one of the strongest non-partnered squads, pushing top-tier opponents and proving their worth. But success in this ecosystem comes at a cost—especially when deeper-pocketed teams are always on the lookout for fresh talent.\n😭😭competing in tier 2 valorant is so hard bro\n— ludwig (@LudwigAhgren) February 16, 2025\njust in the last year weve lost\nv1c to C9\nFlyuh to KOI\nOdeshima to Apeks\nmada to NRG\nand now brawk is also going to NRG 🥲\nobviously im not mad at the players or the orgs but ngl its tough to keep rebuilding https://t.co/0fMD3cnB4e\nThe Poaching Begins\nOne by one, MxS players were lured away by VCT-partnered organizations, as Ludwig detailed in frustration:\n- v1c → Cloud9 (October 8, 2024)\n- mada → NRG (October 10, 2024)\n- flyuh → KOI (November 7, 2024)\n- brawk → NRG (February 16, 2025)\nLudwig acknowledged that MxS must be doing “something right” if their players keep getting picked up by top-tier teams. However, he couldn’t help but express frustration at what feels like building a championship-caliber squad—only to have it stripped away by organizations with bigger budgets and better opportunities.\nAlso Read: Rainbow Six Siege X Reveal: March 13 Showcase, Leaks & New Features\nThe Harsh Reality for Non-Partnered Teams\nMxS isn’t alone in this struggle. The current VALORANT esports structure makes it nearly impossible for non-franchised teams to hold onto their best talent. Without a spot in the VCT partnership program, organizations like MxS will always be vulnerable to wealthier teams poaching their star players.\nSo, where does MxS go from here? If they want to stay competitive, they’ll need to find a way to keep their core talent intact—or risk becoming a revolving door for future VCT stars.\nOne thing’s certain: this fight isn’t over, and we’ll be keeping a close eye on what comes next.",
            "content_length": 2765
        },
        {
            "article_id": "89cc5e0d2d2332c7",
            "title": "Waived by Paper Rex, PatMen joins Global Esports - Philstar.com",
            "published_at": "Mon, 15 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Philstar.com",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.philstar.com/sports/2025/12/15/2494453/waived-paper-rex-patmen-joins-global-esports",
            "query_players": [
                "PatMen"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "PatMen",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Waived by Paper Rex, PatMen joins Global Esports\nMANILA, Philippines — Filipino Valorant player Patrick \"PatMen\" Mendoza was released by Paper Rex and is now playing under the mantle of Global Esports as both organizations made the announcement Monday, December 15.\nIn a social media post by Paper Rex, it said it does not make this decision lightly and knows how leaks have caused concerned for Mendoza, who might have seen his window to continue playing in VCT Pacific close with the transfer windows set to end ahead of the 2026 season.\n\"We also want to acknowledge the patience shown by our fans during this period. Due to unexpected complications arising from premature information leaks, the negotiation timeline was extended, and we understand the concerns this created. We appreciate the understanding and trust our community has shown as we worked diligently throughout the process,” said Paper Rex.\nMendoza first joined the organization back in March and has seen success. Since then, he has won a Masters Toronto trophy, a VCT Pacific Stage 2 championship, and two individual citations in the VCT Pacific Awards with Rookie of the Year and Initiator of the Year.\n\"Recently, we have made a very difficult decision to release our rising rookie and key initiator, Patrick ‘PatMen’ Mendoza. PatMen has been an exceptional competitor and a valued member of the squad. Since joining in March 2025, PatMen adapted quickly to the pace and requirements of a Tier 1 player, helping the team secure podium finishes in both VCT Pacific Stage 1 and 2 as well as securing our trophy at VCT Masters Toronto. His contributions will always be part of Paper Rex’s story. Stay hungry, Pat, we wish you only the best for your future endeavors,” said Paper Rex.\nAt the same time, Global Esports, which announced its roster the day before, said that Mendoza would join their squad. He joins fellow Filipino and Valorant Challengers Philippines vet Xavier \"xavi8k\" Juan, who Global Esports announced last night to be joining the 2026 roster.\nBoth Juan and Mendoza had a brief stint as teammates when Mendoza was with NAOS Esports during the first quarter of the year.\n\"I'm forever grateful to u guys [Paper Rex], especially to my teammates and Paper people who supported me during my journey,” said Mendoza on X, formerly Twitter.\n- Latest\n- Trending",
            "content_length": 2338
        },
        {
            "article_id": "465364dbab034785",
            "title": "Team Liquid Adds Wayne To 2026 VALORANT Roster Alongside MiniBoo And Purp0 - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Mon, 15 Dec 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMib0FVX3lxTFAwLW03MjA2dXlodWpmRjZmcVNzLTJjNUZvazI1LVNIcUZEMnlEMnRpUEpHQU42bl9VRkZ5RTFsZnJQOW82RFJ6NnVSbXluTTJyWmxJMERBTzZBUThRRzhBdk5fdHJ6YzlqcEZPeFh0MA?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/team-liquid-2026-roster",
            "query_players": [
                "kamo",
                "MiniBoo"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "BerLIN",
                "kamo",
                "keiko",
                "MiniBoo",
                "nAts",
                "purp0"
            ],
            "content": "Team Liquid has completed its 2026 VALORANT roster with the signing of Singaporean controller specialist Wayne “wayne” Chang Wei Jie, bringing in one of the Pacific region’s fastest-rising young talents ahead of the next EMEA league cycle. They also confirmed the signing of ex-Heretics player Dominykas “MiniBoo” Lukaševičius, as well as Semyon “purp0” Borchev. The three join returning Liquid staples Ayaz “nAts” Akhmetshin, and Kamil “kamo” Frąckowiak, forming a refreshed lineup for Berlin. Wayne’s arrival also comes in the wake of roster turbulence during the offseason, including the high-profile departure of Liquid’s star Duelist Keiko, who transferred to NRG.\nWayne’s career trajectory across Asia has been marked by rapid improvement and high-pressure experience despite his age. After early development across regional teams such as X10 Esports, Kizuna Esports, and the international project Disguised, he broke through in 2025 with Motiv Esports. Motiv became the standout squad of Southeast Asia, finishing first in both of the region’s Challengers splits and securing qualification to that year’s Pacific Ascension event.\nIn the Ascension tournament, Motiv placed fourth overall—an impressive finish considering the depth of competition and the growing strength of the Pacific circuit. Throughout this period Wayne carried much of the team’s controller burden, managing mid-round utility and maintaining consistency in matches where Motiv often relied on structure rather than raw firepower.\nHis role specialization became one of his defining strengths. Most of his appearances were on Omen, which showcased his timing, smokes management, and discipline around space creation. He frequently filled in on Yoru when Motiv required more unpredictable mid-round pressure, and he produced several strong showings on Fade in maps that called for defensive information control. In rare circumstances he even stepped into Jett, mostly in compositions where Motiv needed an additional burst threat. This diverse agent pool made him a compelling pickup for an organization like Team Liquid that has regularly relied on layered tactical flexibility.\nTeam Liquid’s 2025 journey cut across every major tier of the VALORANT season, producing a blend of promising regional performances and frustrating international results. At the year’s EMEA Kickoff, Liquid made a strong first impression by fighting through the lower bracket, defeating multiple league rivals and reaching the Grand Final before falling to Vitality. It was a statement opening, one that suggested Liquid could challenge at the top of the region.\nHowever, their momentum faltered at Masters Bangkok, where the team competed in the initial Swiss stage but failed to progress to the playoffs. A mixed set of matchups, including a strong win over Sentinels offset by losses to EDward Gaming and G2, left Liquid outside bracket contention.\nIn EMEA Stage 1, Liquid maintained a competitive presence with a steady run through the league phase, yet their playoff push ended abruptly after a one-sided defeat to Team Heretics, ultimately coming third and making it to the second international that year. Liquid returned to international play at Masters Toronto, where a familiar pattern emerged: they earned an early victory against MIBR but were knocked out of Swiss after losses to Bilibili Gaming and eventual champions Paper Rex. Another international exit before playoffs added pressure heading into the summer.\nThe team’s strongest period came during EMEA Stage 2, where they pieced together one of their most complete regional stretches of the year and ended the event with a trophy and finally a 1st place finish Liquid qualified for Champions 2025 off the back of their Stage 2 results but the final event of the year followed a familiar script. A 1–2 record in groups ended their run before playoffs once again, an outcome that sharpened the organization’s desire for structural change and ultimately contributed to the roster overhaul ahead of 2026.\nKeiko’s move to NRG emerged as one of the biggest offseason storylines. His mechanical output and duel-opening presence had been valuable for Liquid throughout 2025, but the organization opted to reset its approach to roles and long-term development. By releasing Keiko and integrating Wayne, Liquid has shifted toward a more flexible control-oriented roster with room for aggressive plays through MiniBoo and kamo.\nWe are proud to announce that our 2026 roster is locked in – stronger, sharper, hungrier.\nWelcome to the next chapter of Team Liquid VALORANT! @purp03 @wayneeeVLR @nAts__ss @M1niBoo @kamoyeess @LohaN_vlr @yaotziN_twitch #LETSGOLIQUID #LETSGOLIQUID pic.twitter.com/fHsKd3yCt4\n— Team Liquid VALORANT (@LiquidValorant) December 12, 2025\nTeam Liquid enters 2026 with a five-man lineup that balances high-level experience with fresh, region-spanning talent. The team fields nAts as its defensive anchor and in-game leader, kamo and MiniBoo as the primary fragging engines, purp0 as a hybrid flex who can shift between aggressive and supportive roles, and wayne as the new controller core. The coaching structure remains centered around Ivo “LohaN” Albino as head coach.\nNAts remains the foundation of Liquid’s style, offering unmatched consistency on Sentinel roles and serving as the team’s primary voice in mid-round adjustments. kamo continues to be one of the roster’s most explosive entry threats, complementing the newly signed ex-Heretics player MiniBoo, whose experience and timing have long earned him a reputation as a high-impact duelist.\nPurp0, who has played for the likes of Team Gambit in the past, is particularly valuable in a meta that rewards flexibility, and his experience shows he can pivot between controllers, duelists, and mid-round initiative roles depending on Liquid’s needs. Wayne rounds out the roster with steady impact and the ability to shift into secondary roles when compositions require it. His combination of agent expertise flexibility gives Liquid a fresh set of tactical levers heading into 2026.\nTeam Liquid’s roster for the 2026 VCT season now stands as:\nAyaz “nAts” Akhmetshin\nKamil “kamo” Frąckowiak\nDominykas “MiniBoo” Lukaševičius\nSemyon “purp0” Borchev\nWayne “wayne” Chang Wei Jie\nSupport Staff:\nIvo “LohaN” Albino: Head Coach\nDaniel “yaotziN” Roczniak: Assistant Coach\nBerke “Mol” Mol: Manager\nAfter a year defined by regional success and international frustration, Team Liquid has chosen a measured but significant rebuild for 2026. The addition of Wayne strengthens their tactical depth while opening up more flexible map-specific compositions, addressing several gaps exposed in the 2025 international cycle. With the team preparing to debut this lineup at the EMEA Kickoff in Berlin, Liquid enters the new season with a clearer identity, a diversified roster, and a tangible opportunity to push beyond the limitations that defined their previous year.",
            "content_length": 6936
        },
        {
