            "article_id": "89ec06c965fa1619",
            "title": "Have you peaked it? - The New Indian Express",
            "published_at": "Mon, 11 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "The New Indian Express",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.newindianexpress.com/cities/chennai/2025/Aug/11/scaling-new-heights-how-peak-turns-simple-game-narrative-into-endless-fun",
            "query_players": [
                "coconut"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "coconut",
                "something",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Like most people who play videogames, I like my habits. I enjoy starting my day with my daily Geoguessr challenge, and shutting off a long, hard day at work with a stressful game of Valorant. It’s hard to break this toxic cycle unless something truly ground-breaking comes my way. And it is definitely too early to say it, and I’m almost certainly jinxing it right now, but I think my friends and I may have finally found a way out with Peak — a new cooperative game.\nThere’re actually so many reasons why Peak shouldn’t work, but despite that, it is incredibly fun. You see, this game is ridiculously simple. You find yourself at the bottom of a mountain, and you must climb to the top. That’s it. The controls are as bare-bones as the premise — find a ledge, hit the climb button, haul yourself up. You scan the landscape for your next move and repeat. There are only a handful of peaks to conquer in the game, following which you may unlock the higher difficulty. In theory, this sounds like a game you’d play just once and forget. So, how does a game like this possibly be something you could replay with friends? Well, things do get interesting.\nReason 1: The hunger-o-meter\nThe longer you wait, the more hungry you get. Hunger implies stamina loss, which means that you can’t scale more challenging ledges. Combatting hunger requires carrying food items you might pick up along the way, but you only have so many carry slots, which are probably spent on taking more important items. So what do you do? Here, survival often hinges on the one friend who holds a backpack. You will often find yourself yelling across the mountain for that one mule-friend just so they can bring you half a coconut to keep you moving. But watch out for suspicious-looking mushrooms and oddly coloured fruits. Food poisoning at halfway up a mountain isn’t the most ideal thing to happen in this game.\nReason 2: Proximity chat and ‘Ghosting’\nThe game has in-built proximity chat, which implies that you will hear exactly where your friends are, even if you don’t immediately see them. A blessing if you’re the type to get lost easily. If I’m being honest about it, in this game, proximity chat is mostly comic relief. There’s something frustratingly hilarious about catching the faint, echoing remains of someone’s voice as they plummet into a cavern or wander miles away from you. And when a player dies, they actually haunt the bubble around you and hover as a ghost. Watching your friends’ voices come out of these rag doll-type figures, stumbling over a rock and struggling to climb when you can do nothing about it, can be absolutely hysterical.\nReason 3: The real summit is the friends you made along the way\nSure, you could try to scale the whole peak alone — offline is a completely available mode if you’re looking for a challenge and finding the correct route to the top. But the actual fun is in the chaos of failing miserably with three other incompetent people. Coordinating lifts, sharing food, and rescuing people from ledges is half the fun. Teamwork here isn’t about conquering the summit, but arguing with each other about the optimal route to the top while you gradually build hunger and wait for the all-consuming fog to hit you all.\nReason 4: A new challenge every day!\nThe game has a new procedurally generated map every day. It has an almost Wordle-like quality to the whole thing, where you kind of want to just pick it up to see if you can find the best possible route in the simplest way.\nPeak is currently topping the charts with its simplicity. It’s available to play on the PC via the Steam Store. It’s currently priced at Rs 359.\nYou should play it only if you can get a few other friends to buy it with you.",
            "content_length": 3721
        },
        {
            "article_id": "49c96060610fa7ec",
            "title": "VCT EMEA Stage 1 2025 Primer: Format and Key Dates - VALORANT Esports | Schedule",
            "published_at": "Mon, 10 Mar 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "VALORANT Esports | Schedule",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikgFBVV95cUxOS1pFTVVUVDFlTDhmZG12Wl9NbWJURUpGWEE2MW9DeUJubzFRWGIxMDY2U0diUU85MWx4YzJaNlNENEtaOXNWZWVUYlp0ZVBwbmlfLUpPX1RBM2o1UU1vd29pSXdjWFVZRHJMTDJjM0pZWkRZTG5acXdPdzBPWjlCeHdUM2hLYzN1cV9YbW8zMnFXZw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://valorantesports.com/en-gb/news/vct-emea-stage-1-2025-primer-format-and-key-dates",
            "query_players": [
                "Kicks"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "BerLIN",
                "Kicks",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "VALORANT Masters Bangkok set the stage for an electric start to the season, and now, all eyes turn back to our International Leagues (ILs). Thankfully, you won’t have to wait long—VCT EMEA Stage 1 kicks off on Wednesday, March 26.\nTeam Liquid returns to the region with a point to prove. After an impressive lower bracket run at VCT EMEA Kickoff and a gutsy international showing, they fell just short of a Playoffs spot at Masters Bangkok. Now, they’ll be hungry to reassert themselves.\nMeanwhile, Team Vitality enters Stage 1 with a target on their backs. They claimed their first VALORANT title at Kickoff and carried that momentum into Bangkok, where they stormed into the Playoffs without dropping a single series, securing a top-four finish.\nAs teams regroup and figure out their roster’s strengths on the road to Toronto, competition is going to be even fiercer for the Stage 1 title. Like you, we can’t wait to see how our teams will improve and perform so in preparation, here’s everything you need to know about VCT EMEA Stage 1.\nStage 1 kicks off with a high-stakes single round-robin group stage, where teams will battle it out in two groups of six—Group Alpha and Group Omega. With fierce rivalries reignited and fresh grudge matches on the horizon, every game matters. The top four teams from each group will advance to the Playoffs, where the competition only gets tougher. Missed the draw? Check out the results below to see how the stage is set!\nThe Playoffs will follow a modified double-elimination bracket, where teams' performance in the Group Stage determines their starting position:\n#1 seeds from each group earn a bye into Round 2 of the Upper Bracket.\n#2 and #3 seeds will face off in the Opening Round.\n#4 seeds have no second chances—they start in the Lower Bracket and face elimination immediately, playing against the losers of the Opening Round.\nAs the competition progresses, the final eight teams will battle through a mix of best-of-three series, escalating to best-of-fives in the Lower Final and Grand Final to determine the ultimate Stage 1 champion.\nThis time, it's not just the top two teams advancing internationally—three teams will qualify for Toronto, raising the stakes even higher.\nWe’re shaking things up in Stage 1 with a refreshed map pool:\nRemoved: Bind & Abyss\nAdded: Icebox & Ascent\nThese maps will remain in play for the entirety of Stage 1.\nBeyond Stage 1 glory and Toronto qualification, teams are also competing for Championship Points—which will determine not just the third but also the fourth EMEA team to qualify for VALORANT Champions Paris.\nHere’s how the standings look so far:\nWith plenty of time left in 2025, there’s still room for major shifts in the leaderboard.\nStage 1 kicks off on Wednesday, March 26, with a 5 PM CET start time to avoid any late finishes. The Group Stage will run for five weeks, with matches played across three days each week, culminating on April 25.\nAfter a short break for teams to prepare, the Playoffs will begin on May 8 with a special four-day game week, featuring matches from May 8-11.\nThe stage will conclude the following week with three days of huge games from May 16-18. May 16 will see both the Upper Bracket Final and Lower Bracket Semi-Finals, followed by the Lower Finals on May 17, then we’ll crown our champions in The Grand Final on Sunday, May 18 which promises to deliver yet another unforgettable showdown!\nFor the rest of 2025, we’re focusing on making VCT EMEA’s biggest moments even more special for fans online and in-person. To focus on these peak moments, we’re no longer hosting fans at the Riot Games Arena on Wednesdays during the Regular Season, allowing us to reinvest in delivering unforgettable experiences when they matter most.\nTo grab your tickets for any other showdays and be part of the action, join us in the Riot Games Arena in Berlin by securing your tickets here!\nKeep up to date with all VALORANT Esports news and updates on our socials:\nTwitter/X: @valesports_emea\nFacebook: /VCTEMEA\nInstagram: @valorantesportsemea\nTikTok: @valorantesportsemea\nTwitch: https://www.twitch.tv/valorant_emea\nYouTube: https://www.youtube.com/@VALORANTEsportsEMEA\nPhotos from Events: https://www.flickr.com/photos/valesports/\nOfficial VALORANT Esports News: valorantesports.com/news\nWeb: http://valorantesports.com\nVCT EMEA Tickets: https://emeatickets.valorantesports.com/",
            "content_length": 4390
        },
