            "title": "nAts secures visa in time to try to save Team Liquid at Valorant Masters Toronto - Esports News UK",
            "published_at": "Mon, 09 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports News UK",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMilwFBVV95cUxPYW0wMVFsa0doY3hRaTlsNjVIaGs2RlptWFJxTm1NQTF3dXNzTDZpLTlwX2VTakpXNGdaRG1pelB1ZWIzVmZwU0tEYzdyTnZqX0lJWXk0X3NXRGhFUXk1MXhZNWl3VUZwbVNXS0V3a2kyOVBBRERyZENjdG8yQXJBWmNya3ZiOGUzaVNkSGJMa3Y5d3hpMTlZ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports-news.co.uk/2025/06/09/team-liquid-nats-visa-sorted-valorant-masters-toronto/",
            "query_players": [
                "nAts"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "nAts",
                "penny",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Team Liquid’s in game leader (IGL), Ayaz ‘nAts’ Akhmetshin has finally arrived at Valorant Masters Toronto.\nAnd his timing couldn’t be much better, as defeat in their next series will spell elimination from the tournament for Team Liquid.\nAfter losing to Bilibili Gaming (BLG), Liquid now have to deal with Wolves Esports in Round 2 on Tuesday, June 10th.\nThere were fears that nAts could miss Valorant Masters Toronto entirely due to visa issues.\nHowever, the Russian confirmed on X (formerly Twitter) that he has received his visa and was immediately heading to Canada.\nWithout him, Team Liquid enlisted the help of Canadian pro Erik ‘penny’ Penny to compete in front of a home crowd.\nWith only a few days of practice together, Team Liquid performed surprisingly well against China’s second seeds BLG.\nIt was an entertaining series that was closer than most expected. But after some back and forth, BLG ultimately won 2-1.\nThis was a big step up for penny competing at this level of event for the first time but he excelled.\nThe Canadian starred in Liquid’s victory on Haven, helping the team win 16-14 in overtime with a 30-18 KD on Phoenix.\nSpeaking in the post-match conference, penny said he felt comfortable playing at the event:\n“This might sound a bit crazy to say, but… I honestly think that playing in Tier 2 with how it is, is honestly much harder than playing in a Tier 1 system with Tier 1 coaching staff.\n“When you have an idea of how to play the game, you have the game plans, the anti-strat, all this stuff makes the game a lot easier.\n“And in Challengers, everything is completely random. Teams are playing twelve different playstyles, everyone has full confidence, and… no care, honestly.\n“Like, you’ve probably seen some of the drama that happened with Tier 2. There’s not a lot of people with, like, passion or care for the game. Everyone’s playing however they want. They don’t care.”\nJack Stewart, Senior Editor\nIn my seven years of esports writing, I've introduced esports coverage to newspapers, interviewed some of the biggest names in the industry, and driven viewers mad with the puns in my YouTube scripts. I'm most proud of the latter.\nFrom breaking news and in-depth match analysis to exclusive interviews and behind-the-scenes content, we bring you the stories that shape the esports scene in the UK.\nMonthly Visitors\nEsports Covered\nYears experience",
            "content_length": 2382
        },
        {
            "article_id": "60fbeeeb2b00c92f",
            "title": "Zellsis: “If I hate the player enough, I play with anger” – Sentinels beat Bilibili at Masters Toronto - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Mon, 09 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMid0FVX3lxTFBYemt2RWpBVVZVSlNESWFxaGduRGp2RXE2WEV4WDNyMGt4bkh6RFdkYnVuc0xTdFpiZGl3Y2kxMjlwWDdkdFRKVzJQdmRUWW9JY0p5ZmgyWF9hTDhmOWNNTzAteWxwanRMU1puSktQLUlNYWgyelJ3?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/zellsis-anger-masters-toronto",
            "query_players": [
                "Zellsis"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "bang",
                "Less",
                "nAts",
                "nephh",
                "YOU",
                "zekken",
                "Zellsis"
            ],
            "content": "Sentinels became the first Swiss stage team to book their playoff spot at Masters Toronto with a 2-1 win over Bilibili Gaming. The victory sees SEN progress with a 2-0 win-loss record, while BLG will have one more attempt to qualify.\nChina’s second seed, Bilibili was widely thought of as one of the worst teams coming into the tournament, and were fortunate to pick up a win against nAts-less Team Liquid in their first series.\nIt was clear that Sentinels — one of the top 5 teams at Masters Toronto — would be a completely different challenge. But Bilibili stunned the home favourites to start the series 1-0 with a 13-9 win on their Icebox pick.\nSentinels fought back, levelling the score through a one-sided, 13-5 Sunset performance, before clinching the deciding map with a 13-9 win on Lotus.\nSpeaking to the media during the post-match press conference, BLG’s Marcus “nephh” Tan was asked about the Sunset defeat and whether his side were surprised that SEN picked the map after banning it in their win against Wolves.\n“We kind of expected it because they are a team that likes to anti-strat. And I think they did a very good job by picking our Sunset because we played it the day before or two days before against Liquid.\n“And yeah, I think they played really well. Maybe we are not that experienced on that map as well, we failed to adapt to their methodical playstyle and it really cost us the second map.”\nThe Singaporean was later asked to speak on his experience IGLing in a second language while playing in a predominantly-Chinese roster.\n“I think at the start, when I first joined, it was kind of hard because my Mandarin was not that proficient at the start. But over time, getting used to it, I think it got pretty much [to] where my Mandarin, in the game sense, is kind of as proficient as them really.\n“And compared to English, honestly, there’s not really much difference. The things that we comm in games using Chinese is what I would normally say when I was in English to myself. So that’s not really much difference.”\nThe press conference was cut short after this question, with Bilibili having only appeared for six minutes, as media were informed that the side was not in a state to continue taking questions following the defeat.\nFor Sentinels, it was job done. While it’s the second Chinese opponent in a row whereby SEN did not win in quite the manner that was expected, at the end of the day, their 2-0 record has seen them move on to the playoffs at the first attempt.\nIn their press conference, coach Adam “kaplan” Kaplan was also asked for his thoughts on the Sunset pick and whether Bilibili’s prior match against Team Liquid had an impact.\n“There was definitely some stuff we saw as a pattern between Liquid and the last time they played it two weeks previously. And we definitely put some of that to use. And yeah, definitely played into our comfort in picking that map.”\nThe coach was also requested to comment on the significant meta shifts coming into the tournament following the much-anticipated Tejo and Breach nerfs.\n“I think you need to focus on your own game a little more. Obviously there’s no footage in a lot of these cases of opponents in this new meta yet, because they haven’t played yet. And even when they have played, it’s usually only one rep. You can assume they’ve- it’s pretty easy to cook more than one match worth of stuff.\n“So I definitely think it’s pushing teams to innovate and focus on their own game a lot more. And yeah, you just have to be ready for anything. You need to adapt more during the game because of it, I think.”\nThe press conference had started with a bang, prior to kaplan’s comments, with Jordan “Zellsis” Montemurro asked to comment on whether he harnesses anger to him compete.\n“If I hate the player enough, I play with anger”\nNow a core part of the roster with a growing sense of responsibility, Duelist Zachary “zekken” Patrone was questioned on his evolving role within Sentinels, in particular the differences between his performances so far in Toronto in comparison to Masters Bangkok earlier this season.\n“I would say the main difference between me as a player in Bangkok versus me as a player now is mainly just focusing on how I can impact the team versus how I’m individually playing.\nI focus a lot more on communicating to my teammates — making good macro plans, trying to win the round versus trying to, you know, play for myself and set myself up.“I’m more supporting and trying to set up my teammates. And if I’m really feeling it, obviously I’ll put myself in really good positions to do that. But other games I’m just purely focused on the team and trying to just set everyone up so we have a smooth game versus side questing and just doing my own thing.”",
            "content_length": 4752
        },
        {
            "article_id": "602b15b9cc8a2a72",
            "title": "Riot Games Drops Spanish Esports Partner Over Streaming Dispute | Outlook Respawn - Outlook Respawn",
            "published_at": "Mon, 08 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Outlook Respawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://respawn.outlookindia.com/gaming/gaming-news/riot-games-drops-spanish-esports-partner-over-streaming-dispute",
            "query_players": [
                "nobody"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "nobody"
            ],
            "content": "Gaming giant cites contract breach as it removes KOI from premier Valorant league\nHighlights\n- Riot Games kicked KOI from its premier Valorant league over a contract breach.\n- The breach stemmed from co-founder Ibai Llanos failing to meet his co-streaming duties.\n- KOI’s Valorant players are now free agents, and Riot will replace the team for 2026.\nRiot Games Inc. severed ties with Spanish esports organization Movistar KOI, stripping the team of its coveted spot in the Valorant Champions Tour EMEA league just months before the 2026 season begins.\nThe Los Angeles-based gaming company announced the termination on Saturday, citing an unspecified \"breach of contract\" that immediately ejected one of the league's 10 founding partners from competitive play.\nAt the heart of the dispute lies a streaming requirement tied to KOI co-founder Ibai Llanos, one of Spain's most prominent content creators. When Riot selected KOI in 2022, the contract reportedly mandated that Llanos co-stream VCT matches to tap into his millions of followers, a key factor in the organization's original selection.\nThat arrangement unraveled as Ibai scaled back his streaming schedule over the past year, citing concerns about his mental health and overall well-being.\nAccording to industry publication Sheep Esports, Riot flagged KOI's failure to meet streaming obligations multiple times throughout 2024, issuing a formal warning in January and conducting follow-up discussions as recently as May.\nOrganization Claims Riot Blindsided Leadership\nAdam Adamou, chief executive of KOI's parent company, OverActive Media Corp., disputed Riot's decision, characterizing the disagreement as centered on \"technical details.\" He said KOI had proposed alternative streaming arrangements at its own expense, which Riot rejected.\nThe timing caught KOI off guard. In a 10-minute video response, Ibai said the organization was summoned to an \"urgent meeting\" on Friday, just one day before the public announcement, and told the decision was final.\n\"Nobody saw it coming because no one warned us at all,\" Llanos said, noting that KOI had already hired a coach for the upcoming season.\nIbai speculated that the move might be tied to the relegation of Gentle Mates. He suggested that by removing KOI from the league, Riot may be making room for M8’s potential return to VCT EMEA.\nKOI's complex corporate structure may have complicated matters. The organization formed in January 2024 through a three-way merger of separate Spanish entities, including the original KOI founded by Ibai and soccer star Gerard Piqué.\nThe decision affects only KOI's Valorant operations. The organization will retain its spot in Riot's League of Legends European Championship, where it won the title last year. All KOI Valorant players will become free agents, with Riot granting special exemptions allowing them to sign with other teams despite the closed transfer window.\nRiot said it will select a new permanent partner to fill the vacant league slot ahead of the 2026 season.\nAuthor\nKrishna Goswami is a content writer at Outlook India, where she delves into the vibrant worlds of pop culture, gaming, and esports. A graduate of the Indian Institute of Mass Communication (IIMC) with a PG Diploma in English Journalism, she brings a strong journalistic foundation to her work. Her prior newsroom experience equips her to deliver sharp, insightful, and engaging content on the latest trends in the digital world.\nKrishna Goswami is a content writer at Outlook India, where she delves into the vibrant worlds of pop culture, gaming, and esports. A graduate of the Indian Institute of Mass Communication (IIMC) with a PG Diploma in English Journalism, she brings a strong journalistic foundation to her work. Her prior newsroom experience equips her to deliver sharp, insightful, and engaging content on the latest trends in the digital world.\nRelated Articles",
            "content_length": 3905
        },
