            "article_id": "4bd81896da7549bc",
            "title": "Valorant community celebrates Boaster after heartbreaking Champions loss - Esports News UK",
            "published_at": "Mon, 06 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports News UK",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMisAFBVV95cUxQNFhNUDBBZ281RkRCZGVpTF9RUjVkTWpfaXNKdDhXdXhCa0tiRUQ3RHc4TzVyRkd6ZkhnaGoyVWtnbzByUk1Qd3ZqZGJLa2FIVnBhNnV0MkpoV2NvX3NQZFdneEhjRnFEbWJMWERpX2M4S2NLeG54ekYzY0pGSTM1LVEwb2hZdlZBeHZXMzh2WERmN1NST1JHZVRwNWlGQWxzT2JUNDFCS3ktTDFZRUZGaw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports-news.co.uk/2025/10/06/valorant-community-celebrates-boaster-after-heartbreaking-champions-loss/",
            "query_players": [
                "Boaster",
                "JonahP"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Boaster",
                "ComeBack",
                "JonahP",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Members of the Valorant community have come together on social media to celebrate Britain’s Jake ‘Boaster’ Howlett after an agonising 2-3 Grand Final loss saw NRG down Fnatic at Valorant Champions 2025.\nA historic comeback saw Fnatic fight back from 11-1 down on map three to go on to force a fifth and final map, but NRG showed their mental resilience to take the fifth map and become VCT Paris winners.\nBoaster, a player who always wears his heart on his sleeve, was visibly emotional after the loss, but the Valorant community have celebrated that vulnerability and the meaning it gives to the scene and its competitions.\nCommunity celebrates Boaster’s impact on Valorant’s esports scene\nEsports influencer, Jake Lucky was amongst those to celebrate Boaster’s career and personality, stating, “The emotion says it all from Boaster, but what this guy has brought to the Valorant scene can never be downplayed. Everybody is watching him entertain when it’s a grand finals like this. And to be so close so many times this year, he will get his.”\nG2’s Jonah ‘JonahP’ Pulice was amongst those moved by Boaster after the Grand Final loss, saying “We love you @OfficialBoaster.”\nNA CS player, and member of Team Mythic, Austin ‘Cooper’ Abadir echoed those statements, saying, “I hope Boaster keeps being himself and being entertaining. I never watch Valorant, and I tuned in for the final and honestly was super entertained.”\nPhotographer Yicun Liu shared a story about Boaster, who encouraged the photographer to take photos of the sad moments after Fnatic’s Masters Shanghai 2024 defeat.\nThe story garnered more praise from Valorant fans, thankful for Boaster’s openness and honesty with one fan stating “Thank you, Boaster, for sharing your sadness with us. A very mature human being and great personality I am truly inspired. Let’s win next year!”\nBritish broadcast talent, Tom ‘Tombizz’ Bissmire celebrated Fnatic’s year despite defeat, posting, “I really hope this isn’t the last time we see this version of Fnatic. The year didn’t end with a trophy but 3 back to back finals is very impressive.”\nBoaster becomes star of Ohnepixel co-streams\nBoaster became the co-star of Ohnepixel’s co-stream of the playoffs, with the German streamer stepping away from his usual Counter-Strike streams into the world of Valorant.\nOhnepixel’s co-streams have become some of the most watched in esports, with his streams contributing to the growing trend of co-streams cumulatively surpassing official streams in Q2 of 2025.\nDespite streaming Valorant rarely, Ohne’s stream at times rivalled the viewership of the official stream.\nBoaster’s antics made him a significant part of Ohnepixel’s co-streams, with Boaster notably shouting Ohne out during the Grand Final, saying “Ohnepixel, zestify” in response to Ohne’s previous reactions to Boaster’s all-singing, all-dancing on-stage performances.\nOhnepixel stated on stream, “without Boaster, I would only have half as much fun [watching Valorant].”\nFollowing the final, British Valorant broadcasting legend, Yinsu Collins, Boaster’s partner, revealed that Ohnepixel had sent Boaster a message following the final, asking the Brit not to retire. Yinsu shared the detail on an Instagram story as part of a Q&A with her fans.\nIn a later Instagram story, Yinsu put questions of Boaster’s potential retirement to rest, stating he will not retire and adding, “It is sometimes a bit exhausting for people to clickbait his retirement. He’s never said he’s gonna retire.”\nThose words will be reassuring to fans of Fnatic and Valorant’s community in general who hope to continue to watch the energetic IGL dancing on stage for years ahead.\nFrom breaking news and in-depth match analysis to exclusive interviews and behind-the-scenes content, we bring you the stories that shape the esports scene in the UK.\nMonthly Visitors\nEsports Covered\nYears experience",
            "content_length": 3882
        },
        {
            "article_id": "ab6db6f17de04a9d",
            "title": "NRG Wins Valorant Champions 2025 ⇒ Recap & Highlights - Pley.gg",
            "published_at": "Mon, 06 Oct 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Pley.gg",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiakFVX3lxTE1oZ0lXdUVQcmJfNGNsYlFhLVM3a3RwSTJqSkltdzlrWFdGNi1haGc2SXFDeTh3Ylp2UWljTk1RZlhRM2dsdTRha2htRzQ4NXdoVjdhcGRQVW1fWmRLR1dvUFlDTnVyczZwOXc?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://pley.gg/valorant/nrg-wins-valorant-champions-2025/",
            "query_players": [
                "brawk",
                "Ethan"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "brawk",
                "crashies",
                "Ethan",
                "kaajak"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant Champions 2025, the final event of the year’s Valorant esports season, has concluded in Paris with NRG claiming the title after a 3-2 victory over Fnatic in the grand final. The event at the Accor Arena was packed with fans, and the livestream drew impressive numbers, peaking at over 1.47 million concurrent viewers according to preliminary reports from Esports Charts\nPro players mention in the article\nTournament Overview\nHeading into the tournament, few analysts expected NRG to win. Only 3.9 percent of Pick’Em predicted them as champions. The team had a modest track record internationally, never finishing in the top three, except for team captain Ethan Arnold, who previously won VALORANT Champions 2023 with Evil Geniuses\nDuring the group stage, NRG topped their group after victories against EDward Gaming and DRX. In the playoffs, they defeated GIANTX, won a tough overtime match against MIBR, and then took down Fnatic in both the upper bracket final and the grand final to secure the championship. Ethan Arnold became the first player in VALORANT Champions history to lift the trophy twice with two different teams\nKey Highlights\nEthan Arnold of NRG displayed exceptional gameplay, particularly on Sunset, controlling rounds and making crucial plays that set the tone for the finals. Brawk contributed with aggressive entries and wallbanging, allowing NRG to dominate map control. Fnatic’s Kaajak and Crashies performed well under pressure, but NRG’s pace and strategy ultimately overcame their opponents\nSunset proved to be a decisive map. NRG adapted their tempo, gaining early map control and punishing slower rotations from Fnatic. Across Ascent and Abyss, NRG demonstrated strong teamwork, utility management, and clutch plays, consistently leveraging map knowledge and coordination to secure rounds\nPerformance Across the Series\nThe finals were closely contested with several maps featuring tight rounds and high-level tactical plays. Fnatic remained resilient, but NRG’s coordination, adaptability, and clutch plays gave them the edge. Critical moments included afterplant control, default positioning, and smart use of abilities to suppress Fnatic’s options\nNRG’s roster synergy allowed them to dominate while leveraging each agent’s strengths and adapting dynamically to Fnatic’s strategies. Individual performances from Arnold and Brawk stood out, but it was the teamwork, strategy, and map knowledge that ultimately secured the championship\nEvent Atmosphere and Viewership\nThe organizers created an engaging experience with a musical opening ceremony and a striking stage setup. Riot Games revealed the new agent Veto ahead of the grand final, scheduled for in-game release on October 7\nDespite the packed arena, the tournament struggled to attract certain international viewers. The grand final occurred around 4 a.m. in Los Angeles, limiting North American viewership, while fans in China and the Pacific regions had no local teams in the final\nNRG’s victory at Valorant Champions 2025 demonstrates their skill, coordination, and resilience. With Ethan Arnold leading the team and key performances from Brawk, NRG successfully executed strategies and map control across multiple maps, cementing their place as a top-tier Valorant team\nFor more Valorant news, tournament coverage, and pro-level guides check out Pley.gg\nSources:",
            "content_length": 3362
        },
