            "article_id": "cea68c7a15a62064",
            "title": "G2 Beat Sentinels 3-1 in VCT Americas Grand Finals - India Today Gaming",
            "published_at": "Mon, 05 May 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "India Today Gaming",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.indiatodaygaming.com/news/story/g2-beat-sentinels-3-1-in-vct-americas-grand-finals-8550",
            "query_players": [
                "leaf",
                "trent"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "leaf",
                "trent"
            ],
            "content": "G2 Beat Sentinels 3-1 in VCT Americas Grand Finals\nG2 Esports defeats Sentinels 3-1 in the VCT Americas Grand Final, both teams qualify for Masters.\nIn a much-awaited rematch, G2 Esports won 3-1 against Sentinels in the VCT Americas Stage 1 Grand Final, ending a dominant playoff campaign. With this victory, G2 wins their second major title of the year and solidifies their position atop the North American Valorant landscape.\nBoth Sentinels and G2 have now secured spots for the next Masters event in Toronto, with MIBR joining in as the third seed of the region.\nG2's Road to Redemption\nBefore facing G2 in the grand final, MIBR played a nail-biting lower bracket final against them. In spite of already qualifying for the Masters, G2 went to the server with determination and firepower, defeating the Brazilian team 3-1. Among their top players were leaf and trent. Leaf dropped an impressive 87 kills throughout the four-map series, while Trent's overall play on Tejo made him the highest-rated player throughout the playoffs.\nG2's resilience in the face of economic pressure was also a clinching factor. Mastering low-buy rounds, especially with pistols and Stingers, they aborted MIBR's momentum at pivotal moments. On the deciding map of Ascent, they converted frugal buys into round victories, which sealed the game 13-8.\nMIBR Secures Historic Qualification\nWhile MIBR came up short in the lower final, their third-place finish was sufficient to earn them their first-ever qualification to a Masters-level event. As the Americas' third seed, they will have to make their way through the Swiss Stage in Toronto, but the achievement is a significant milestone for the team after another top-three finish that is similar to their VCT Kickoff performance earlier this year.\nThe grand final once more pitted G2 and Sentinels against each other in what has proven to be one of the region's most heated rivalries. G2 held it down for much of the match despite Sentinels' best efforts to reclaim their previous loss, taking the series 3-1 and keeping Sentinels from having a chance at their own first title of the year.\nAlso Read: Valorant: How the Coming of the Replay System Will Change the Meta\nWith both teams now headed to Toronto, fans can look forward to this rivalry carrying on internationally as G2 looks for more glory and Sentinels look for revenge in a future rematch.",
            "content_length": 2382
        },
        {
            "article_id": "1ca9556e2227876f",
            "title": "Sentinels Reveals 2026 Roster Rebuilt Around Johnqt And N4RRATE - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Mon, 03 Nov 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMibEFVX3lxTFAxd3NSckN5anY3RjBOLVRrSmhjNW5XUzNVQ1NuNDdnUUFSWk9uaVBuSzZBU2tWQnFtbkpmY0dEc1I4a0pfbXNUcHVsaXZvd0NXMXVxTVRmbWNuWmlYTjBLSk9ocU5vWjNRbHJXcg?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/sentinels-2026-roster",
            "query_players": [
                "cortezia",
                "johnqt",
                "N4RRATE"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "bang",
                "cortezia",
                "johnqt",
                "N4RRATE",
                "pANcada",
                "zekken",
                "Zellsis"
            ],
            "content": "Sentinels have officially launched a full rebuild of their VALORANT lineup ahead of the 2026 competitive season, signaling the end of a defining era and the start of a new chapter for one of North America’s most storied organizations. Following a difficult 2025 where they failed to make deep runs at any Masters event, did not win any domestic trophies, and then faced an early exit at Champions Paris, the team made sweeping changes, parting ways with long-standing pieces that shaped their identity in previous campaigns. This is a strategic reset aimed at restoring Sentinels to the forefront of global VALORANT.\nBoth Zachary “zekken” Patrone and Jordan “Zellsis” Montemurro have already departed SEN for rival teams. Zellsis has already announced joining C9 while, according to sources, zekken will be a member of MIBR’s 2026 lineup. Sentinels also released Sean “bang” Bezerra, leaving three spots left to be filled.\nOnly one member from the iconic 2024 Masters Madrid championship squad remains: IGL Mohamed “johnqt” Ouarid, underlining the scale of the transformation. According to the organization, this restructure reflects a desire to move on from past struggles and invest in a blend of promising academy talent and international experience as they “begin anew.”\nThe new roster is anchored by johnqt, and his continued presence represents the competitive backbone and leadership Sentinels hope to build around this season. Johnqt carries extensive tier-one experience and his retention provides stability and veteran insight as the new lineup finds its footing.\nAlongside him remains Marshall “N4RRATE” Massey, who returns to once again play a key role in the team’s structure. N4RRATE brings further familiarity with the organization and an established rapport with johnqt, and before joining SEN in 2025, he had played in EMEA for Karmine Corp, showcasing multiregional experience.\nA significant component of this rebuild is Sentinels’ commitment to internal development, demonstrated by the promotion of two standout prospects from their academy program, Cubert. The first of these players, Yassin “Reduxx” Aboulalazm, joins the starting roster after excelling within the academy system and previously performing at a high level in North America’s tier-two scene with Oxygen Esports. He’s finally 18, which means he can now play in VCT; Reduxx had already been the sixth man for Sentinels’ main lineup before.\nAlongside him, Mirel “Kyu” Braco Hrustemovic also graduates from Cubert. While Kyu has limited top-level experience, his performance in Stage 3 swiss, where the academy team did not lose a game and took down eventual Ascension winners ENVY, proved his readiness to compete at the highest level. His background includes a brief stint with M80, and his promotion marks another step in Sentinels’ effort to shape a new identity through rising talent.\nRounding out the roster is Gabriel “cortezia” Cortez, a Brazilian player who arrives from MIBR and reinforces the international element that has often been a part of Sentinels’ VALORANT story. Cortezia carries experience from two major global events — Masters Toronto and Champions Paris where he stood out with his consistent fragging and sentinel gameplay. His addition continues Sentinels’ tradition of incorporating Brazilian talent, placing him in the company of notable predecessors such as Sacy and pANcada.\nTo new beginnings pic.twitter.com/UIzPTXiJ34\n— Sentinels (@Sentinels) November 1, 2025\nThe coaching and support staff remain led by head coach Adam “kaplan” Kaplan, who will guide this young roster through the early stages of their new campaign. The organization’s willingness to elevate academy players and combine them with experienced talent reflects a long-term approach that aligns with a modern competitive model across top-tier esports.\nThis rebuild clearly marks a shift in direction. Instead of relying on established superstars, Sentinels are prioritizing high-upside youth, stability through veterans, and meaningful international experience. With johnqt serving as the connective thread between championship history and future aspirations, and with new talents like Reduxx and Kyu set to define the next era of Sentinels VALORANT, the 2026 campaign represents a chance for reinvention and resurgence. Cubert Academy’s final split in Challengers showcased their potential and has proven to be a good building ground for Sentinels.\nThe CEO of Sentinels, Rob Moore, has also stated that the entire roster is under contract until 2028, showing that SEN is willing to invest in their new talent and the new roster is built around a fresh competitive vision that balances young talent and experience.\nBy assembling a squad that blends a proven core with ascending academy talent and internationally seasoned players, the organization has made a clear statement about its competitive identity moving forward. As the new lineup prepares to enter the season, all eyes will be on how quickly these new pieces can mesh and whether this bold rebuild can propel the organization back into title contention.",
            "content_length": 5093
        },
        {
