            "article_id": "219afb8141ee97c0",
            "title": "Final four prepare for climactic weekend at VCT EMEA: Stage 2 Playoffs - Esports News UK",
            "published_at": "Fri, 29 Aug 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports News UK",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMirAFBVV95cUxOV2s5MG9vQ01jdlU5SkNDbkxSaV9RY3ZtT1RBN1Bwb3FYVmtwQ25qQ0FmRFlGVEp4QVNIb2F6VXFZNmVueEdVcV8tRWpuT2Ytd251SFFqX0VCWnVwZE51dzNHVGVEdWJMRkM1ZmNHbGc2YWhnTG9pZmFhUnM0OU92dnhjWmdVQ1Z1aGJwclJ0V0lXMFJaWU9jUGliSEhLbWVkTVoyanAyZlEwM2xh?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports-news.co.uk/2025/08/29/final-four-prepare-for-climactic-weekend-at-vct-emea-stage-2-playoffs/",
            "query_players": [
                "ANGE1",
                "Jamppi"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "alexiiik",
                "ANGE1",
                "Boaster",
                "Jamppi",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "GIANTX, Liquid, BBL Esports, and Natus Vincere (NaVi) will face off in a climactic showdown at VCT EMEA: Stage 2 Playoffs, which will decide not only where the EMEA Stage 2 trophy will end up, but who will attend VCT Champions in Paris.\nLiquid, favourites for the title with esports betting sites, will play BBL Esports in the Upper Bracket Final, before GIANTX take on NaVi in the Lower Bracket Semis.\nVCT EMEA Stage 2 Playoff picture\nWhile Liquid are undoubted favourites for the crown, BBL Esports, featuring former ENCE CSGO player Elias ‘Jamppi’ Olkkonen, have consistently surprised pundits this year, notably defeating Liquid 3-1 to qualify for the Esports World Cup.\nLiquid are the last remaining favourite for the title, following the early exits of Stage 1 Champions, Fnatic. Vitality and Karmine Corp also fell early, but while their brands remain strong in Valorant, they both had middling splits, finishing 2-3 and 3-2, respectively, in their groups.\nFnatic were shockingly eliminated 2-0 by GIANTX in the Lower Bracket Quarter-finals, but Britain’s Jake ‘Boaster’ Howlett and his boys were already assured of a place in Paris through EMEA points.\nLiquid are also assured of a place in Paris regardless of the weekend’s results, however, it is all to play for for the other remaining Split 2 teams, with 2 spots still to be claimed between Heretics, BBL, NaVi and GIANTX.\nCan NaVi continue to surprise?\nAfter a strong start in Valorant, which saw NaVi competing for the top prizes during Valorant’s initial esports surge, NaVi have since regressed to being a bit-part player in the scene. The team have often been good enough to make playoffs, but have rarely done damage within them.\nYet there is a distinctly different air around NaVi in the Stage 2 playoffs. It is unclear whether it is simply fire to make the Paris Champs or stronger synergy within the roster, but their 2-0 victories over Vitality and EWC 2025 champions, Heretics, have given them all the feeling of a dark horse for the event.\nNaVi seem to be gaining strength with each official following the addition of Alex ‘alexiiik’ Hawlasek, with the youngster under the storied leadership of IGL Kyrylo ‘ANGE1’ Karasov, who has a history of talent development in CSGO and Valorant, having been one of Oleksandr ‘s1mple’ Kostyliev’s first IGLs.\nIn an interview with Sheep Esports, ANGE1 said, “I’m very proud of Alex. He’s progressing much faster than I expected when moving into Tier 1.”\nThe roster must make the Grand Final to make Valorant Champions, but must do so by moving past a GIANTX roster they have already lost to in playoffs after a 2-1 defeat in the upper bracket quarters.\nFrom breaking news and in-depth match analysis to exclusive interviews and behind-the-scenes content, we bring you the stories that shape the esports scene in the UK.\nMonthly Visitors\nEsports Covered\nYears experience",
            "content_length": 2880
        },
        {
            "article_id": "22f24522883b3a02",
            "title": "Wolves shock the Valorant world | Wolves Esports | News - Wolverhampton Wanderers FC",
            "published_at": "Fri, 27 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Wolverhampton Wanderers FC",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiiAFBVV95cUxQcTR5V1FDa3VIZUNlOEE1Z1R1MThQQnBmMmh0ZFI0VUk0TWl5MEZwNGdWN2kxSTNMYlh1d291THdvLTN1MDJqZDNIUU1fTUJFSXlvT1RHOS10WGF6VXViLTRnalBfdGd2OHVuOVN3Y3g1cl8zMGFfMUxLUkw1eDZJTzczV3NGVzY1?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.wolves.co.uk/news/esports/2025-06-27-wolves-shock-the-valorant-world/",
            "query_players": [
                "nobody"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Juicy",
                "nobody",
                "something",
                "Spring"
            ],
            "content": "The Wolves Valorant team shocked the Valorant world as they claimed a 3rd place finish at Masters Toronto.\nThe team, which finished 3rd in their regional league (VCT CN) to qualify for the Masters, was heavily predicted to be the first to exit the competition.\nFast forward a couple of weeks and Wolves were in the upper-bracket final and fighting for a spot in the grand final proving everyone wrong.\nThe tournament started as many predicted, with a loss to North American titans Sentinels. However, the scoreline did not do the game justice as Wolves ran the Americans close to the wire on both maps.\nThe next game was do or die for Wolves as they came face to face with another giant of the game in the form of European powerhouse Team Heretics. However, Wolves did the unthinkable and beat them in straight maps, 2-0 sending Heretics home and sending Wolves to another do or die match.\nAgain, Wolves did! They took down local rivals Bilibili Gaming 2-0 to secure their spot in the playoffs.\nThis feat alone was something nobody had predicted, and the Valorant world was now forced to sit up and take notice of this team out of China.\nThe first round of the playoffs saw Wolves up against Rex Regum Qeon from the Asia Pacific region. Again, many predicted Wolves to fall here but they continued to defy expectations and emerged victorious, 2-0.\nNext came arguably the biggest challenge this Wolves team had ever faced, a game against tournament favourites and in-form team Gen.G Esports.\nTo everybody’s shock Wolves brushed the favourites aside in a straight 2-0 victory and booked their spot in the upper-bracket final against Paper Rex.\nThis match was one step too far and Wolves suffered a 0-2 defeat that knocked them down to the lower-bracket final where they eventually lost to one of the best and most decorated teams in Valorant Esports, Fnatic.\nThis tournament was more than about simply winning or losing. It was about showcasing the talent and personalities of Wolves. SiuFat, in-game leader for Wolves, captured the hearts of thousands with his honest opinions, heart felt encouragement for his teammates as well as his on-stage energy.\nJuicy caught the spotlight as being the youngest player at Masters Toronto and one of the most talented to boot.\nSpring announced himself as one of the best shots in the game and featured in many top 5 lists for most successful and deadly players in Toronto, with the stats to back him up.\nFor a long time, the Chinese Valorant league has been seen as EDG’s playground. Not anymore. It’s now the Wolves’ hunting ground – and the pack are hungry.",
            "content_length": 2597
        },
        {
            "article_id": "e9c0b65f760dfb75",
            "title": "Benjyfishy Gets Shout Out In Mr Savage Fortnite Skin - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Fri, 26 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMid0FVX3lxTE5nekdrVjRWRnBueWtHWmtxQVo2VFhRWE5VUlRIRFdRUk1QN0dLeGJ4c0c3a3h0WTVEcVR0UDJETTBLeUxLdFhqVTB4RnZoRms4NTFmd3hlU2J6cHNBd1l1M09oNHB1V0c2SExISFhFVzZvdFZQMndZ?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/fortnite/news/benjyfishy-fortnite-mr-savage",
            "query_players": [
                "benjyfishy"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "benjyfishy",
                "something",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Fortnite competitive has seen tons of players move in and out over the years, but one of the best remembered is Benjyfishy. He’s now retired, but up until 2022 he was a major player in Fortnite esports. He was a constant in Finals lobbies in Europe. Eventually, Benjyfishy moved on to Valorant. And has become an incredible player in that game. But in the MrSavage Fortnite skin he’s got a little shout out in Fortnite again too, he’s still got a presence even in Chapter 6 of the game!\nIn Valorant, Benjyfishy has gone on to great success. Being one of the rare examples of a player who can excel in multiple games. Maybe because he started in Fortnite so young. Playing with Team Heretics in Valorant, Benjyihsy is performing really strong in that game right now. Including being part of the winning team at the Esports World Cup for Valorant.\nIf anyone is missing him in Fortnite though, this Mr Savage Fortnite icon skin has a little easter egg for you. Something to commemorate one of the top players in Europe for Fortnite.\nThis week, we’re getting a MrSavage Fortnite Icon Series skin! The special series where Epic includes real world figures in Fortnite as skins. The MrSavage skin features a pickaxe of a red axe. But hanging off the axe pole is a little keyring. A small fish! Benjyfishy on Twitter confirmed the fish was a shout out to him.\nThe player might have moved on to Valorant, but it’s great to see him still being included in Fortnite. By way of another European pro’s new icon skin. The former Fortnite pro said of the fish:\n“Got a little fish in Fortnite dedicated to me, thanks to the goat MrSavage. Love u my bro congrats so well deserved”\nMrSavage and Benjyfishy were always a friendly Duo. Since they both played in older Fortnite seasons, it’s fun to see the pickaxe still included this easter egg. Now that the Cup or the skin has wrapped up, we’re expecting the skins and other cosmetics to come to the item shop this weekend.",
            "content_length": 1956
        },
        {
            "article_id": "845a76744ea4435f",
            "title": "Boo: Finals losses “didn’t make me think that we’re going to lose again. So winning one doesn’t mean we’re going to win again” – VCT EMEA Stage 2 Interview - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Fri, 25 Jul 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiZkFVX3lxTFBYMDJkNVhKMUZYU0Z4dVRIZ0Y1bHMtT0J4MjNXbFNGZWMzenR6SEZiand2OHBVOGlHb2NfOTlZdFFUdkExZ0o1THpPOWszQkNkMXk1ZjZod2VzZ08xOHRQUTQ2ajMzZw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/th-boo-interview",
            "query_players": [
                "Boo"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Boo",
                "ComeBack",
                "MiniBoo",
                "paTiTek",
                "RieNs",
                "something",
                "Veqaj",
                "Wo0t",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Team Heretics’ Esports World Cup win saw them finally overcome their grand finals curse at the sixth time of asking. Now back on home soil for VCT EMEA Stage 2, Los Niños are continuing their solid form with opening wins against BBL and Gentle Mates. The victory against the latter featured two hectic opening maps, both of which Heretics trailed by a large distance before turning around to close the gap. Hotspawn spoke to Team Heretics’ IGL Ričardas “Boo” Lukaševičius for an interview after the series.\nHotspawn: The first two maps both saw massive comebacks from TH today.\nBoo: The first one, Corrode — we did practice the map, but not as much as them, I am quite sure. We knew that we wre going to leave it open, but we were not looking to pick it at all. But yeah, they picked it, so we had to play.\nI think we played three scrims only, and it honestly was the same thing as it was in official. We were getting like 2, 3, 4 rounds on attack, but we had like 11, 10 on defence. I think we won every scrim, but we had crazy bad attacks.\nAnd yeah, I think it’s just like an experience on the map, just not enough time to prepare. Everything went [the] same as scrims, but we were fine with losing Corrode because we know that’s not our strength.\nBut Ascent, again, it’s a defence-sided map in our opinion, but two rounds is a failure in my eyes. We should have gotten like 4 or 5. It’s hard on attack, but we should have gottenway more rounds.\nI think yeah, we underperformed on Ascent attack for sure. And then on defence, both rounds we lost was mistakes from us, basically. So yeah, just two very different-sided maps, at least for us. They played well. Veqaj, I think, just kind of destroyed us throughout the whole best-of-three. But yeah, it was a grind.\nAnd I think the main takeaways: our resilience that started in Saudi where we were honestly down a lot, not just in finals against Fnatic, but even in other games. We were coming back and we kind of learned how to comeback, because usually we are the ones getting reversed from the last year.\nSo just, like, a little bit of new map, a little bit of new mental strength from us. And I mean, they played well. I think they played really well.\nTouching on the EWC final, and it’s finally a grand final win for your team after the finals losing streak.\nI think it’s two main things. One thing is experience. Our previous finals, we had one [versus] Fnatic and we were winning 2-0, and we were winning like 7-2 on the third map, but we still choked it — we got reverse swept. Just a lot of best-of-five finals where we got a lot of experience.\nEverything is possible, basically. We experienced it ourselves that everything is possible, even though it was not in our favor. But we knew that we can also do it, it’s possible. That’s one thing.\n[The] second thing is our team chemistry/energy, in general in EWC, it was always growing and and the energy that [the] guys were giving, especially RieNs, like he was trash talking the crowd. He was like genuinely mad at them. There was emotions that were fueling us: from benjy, MiniBoo and RieNs mostly.\nThere’d been a feeling for a while that, one Heretics finally wins a final, then the pressure will be lifted and they’ll win many more titles afterwards.\nFor the team, I think so. For me personally, no, because all the losses in the finals before didn’t make me think that we’re going to lose again. So winning this one doesn’t mean we’re going to win again.\nI think every single final is [an] individual final. It depends on us in the moment, our form, enemy form. You know, sometimes we play really good, but there is EDG that are just playing better and there is nothing you can do. Or sometimes we are getting completely lost mentally, like against, I don’t know, Fnatic in EMEA Stage 1. We just got lost.\nSo each of them is different. And yeah, maybe for others it has some, you know, lifting the weight from the shoulders. For me, it’s more like, I know how long the path is and we need to do it again. And there is nothing easy about it.\nGoing back a little further and your run was very short at Masters Toronto, losing to the eventual third and first place teams.\nIn theory, yeah, it’s unlucky. We always lose to 1st and 3rd. But you know, while the tournament was still going, while the group stage was still going, we didn’t know that they were going to win. So, you know, it’s like hindsight.\nBut no, like, I don’t think it’s an excuse. I don’t think it’s unlucky seeding. I don’t think it’s anything. Teams are building throughout the tournament. And the Wolves that we lost to was not the Wolves that got to the third place. They improved five times. Paper Rex improved as well during the tournament.\nThat’s what happens when you go on a run. So I think we were supposed to still win the games if we played well. So, you know, it’s not an excuse.\nAnd for the problems, I mean, there was a lot of problems, I’m not gonna lie. But I think the main one was like lack of responsibility in terms of like- we were all kind of chilling and waiting. Waiting for someone else to do the most important things, like the round-winning plays, round-winning calls. A player was waiting to get hyped instead of hyping others.\nWe were fine, but we didn’t do the things that we were supposed to do. We just disappeared, kinda. And then during the games when it happens and we’re losing, there is no chance to come back.\nWhy did we come back against Fnatic? Because people stepped up. They, instead of waiting for others to do something, did it themselves. And then you can start building. You get rounds, then [it] becomes easier to play because you’re actually winning rounds — economy is in your favour, ults are in your favour. But if you’re losing and there is nothing being done to break out from the cycle, you’re just going to keep losing. So that was the main issue during Toronto.\nYou’ve not had long to get ready for Stage 2, especially considering there’s a new map as well as a large number of roster changes.\nI think now it’s fine, but before the first game, after EWC, we had 2 scrim days And the only positive from that is that the team we played against had exactly the same situation: BBL. So that’s why we didn’t get punished in any way. So thanks to Riot for scheduling it that way. I think [the] same for KC/Fnatic.\nSo there was a lot of disadvantage, but in reality it didn’t matter. And now when we’re back to just regular weeks, the field is equal. We have the whole week to prepare for the game.\nI think that’s our strongest state to be in. I think with this roster, without paTiTek, with Wo0t, we are so far 15-0 in group stage. Stage 1 [2024] we had 3-2, but both games we lost were with paTiTek. Then Stage 2 [2024] 5-0, Stage 1 [2025] 5-0, and so far 2-0. So it’s 15 wins in a row in group stage. And that’s our comfort zone. That’s where we can prepare as much as we want. We can change comps, we can change map pools, we can anti-strat, we can prepare for enemies’ anti-strats.\nWe have so many smart people working with us that can build entire gameplans and players that are capable of executing it. So now when we’re back to the season, just regular season, I feel extremely comfortable.",
            "content_length": 7225
        },
        {
            "article_id": "3c4eadeffc6f35f7",
            "title": "Valorant pros call out EMEA broadcast as “unacceptable” tech issues ruin league - Dexerto",
            "published_at": "Fri, 25 Apr 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Dexerto",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiuwFBVV95cUxOWVk4SUE3NGxDU1dpN192QzhzZV95QWs5QWNPazhvUnJIczc0YXpUUWd5QnM5ekZPYkxZeDhSZVpPcW56NXZwZVJxWERwX3ZpSUJnS194MVlBaF92TXUtakE2NnlKdkNndHdrbE5UR2czTXlTLTVjUERIS2xETVBQN0dvUkhJajRsb1AyRDR4YVZaS3FIeGpyaERuRVM3OEhfS0J4VlVEMTFvdUwycjh3dFJvNHFvTW5iOVYw?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.dexerto.com/valorant/valorant-pros-call-out-emea-broadcast-as-unacceptable-tech-issues-ruin-league-3185989/",
            "query_players": [
                "paTiTek"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Derke",
                "nAts",
                "paTiTek",
                "SUYGETSU",
                "TenZ",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Valorant’s premier EMEA League had to shut down the broadcast after tech pauses made matches genuinely unplayable for the Team Liquid vs. Karmine Corp match on Week 5 Day 3.\nThe match went for over 3 hours before Riot pulled the plug on it, with that time being made up more of tech pauses than actual gameplay.\nMultiple rounds had to be rolled back in order to make things fair for teams, broadcast talent had to do their best to fill several minutes of empty air, and the players were visibly frustrated. This resulted in Riot issuing a formal apology to fans and postponing the day’s matches until further notice.\nHowever, pros didn’t take this sitting down. Big names like TenZ and Derke stepped in to comment on it, calling out Riot and rallying for change in order to create a better competitive environment.\nRiot ends broadcast after disastrous VCT EMEA match\nThings didn’t start strong, with 3 separate tech pauses being called by the time round 2 was done. Things didn’t improve much from there, with constant tech-related interruptions eventually resulting in the match being called off.\nEven the pros who were playing in the match took jabs at Riot, with TL’s nAts saying “Won the 1st map, but the next map in a week,” on X right after going up 1-0. He was unintentionally correct considering they had to postpone the broadcast, making the next map literally next week.\npaTiTek, another Team Liquid member, simply said, “what a f*cking joke”, telling Riot to “step it up”.\nKarmine Corp’s SUYGETSU commented on their loss to TL in the first map, saying “Sorry guys sh*tshow, nothing we could do or change, we tried to keep our mental good and etc. But there some things which you cannot handle sometimes,” after the result of the match.\nFortunately for Karmine Corp, the result of that first map is being entirely rescinded with matches being postponed, giving them a shot at beating TL in a better environment. However, Vitality star Derke directly called out Riot, claiming their infrastructure is a huge part of the reason why things like this happen.\nRelated\n“A lot of the issues we’re dealing with now have been ongoing since the beginning of franchising. In the first year or two, we raised concerns and were told to ‘be patient.’ But now it’s year three, and nothing seems to be improving,” Derke claimed.\n“I’ll be honest: the gear we’re playing on could be a lot better. We’ve been giving feedback on the monitors for ages, and I honestly don’t even know what the reasoning is anymore—because every time we bring it up, we’re given a different answer.\n“The PCs aren’t optimized, settings change constantly, and most tech pauses are due to the game stuttering so badly. IT checks it and if they see it or not – we just keep playing. No fixes. This week, we were told we’d be playing online on stage with 30 ping (I get 7 at home) to combat performance issues and tech pauses. But that’s not going to solve anything if the core issue—the hardware isn’t fixed. Everyone, LAN or online, is still experiencing stutters.”\nHe also claimed that the EMEA players have it especially bad and are using PCs meant for League of Legends rather than Valorant. LoL is a much lighter game than Valorant in terms of how hard it is on your PC. And, with pros requiring a smooth experience in order to maintain competitive integrity, he claims that Riot just hasn’t done enough to give them viable setups. No amount of tech pauses will fix that sort of hardware issue.\n“We’ve raised these concerns every split, every year, hoping for progress. I truly hope that now with everyone aware of these issues real improvements can finally happen,” Derke concluded.\nTenZ also got involved, saying that tech issues like this are a huge part of why he retired from pro play.\n“One of the most disappointing things about making it to Tier 1 is realizing that the best players and teams aren’t always given the best conditions to perform at their highest potential. What’s most disheartening isn’t that it happened once, it’s that this has been a recurring issue since the inception of the VCT circuit. It’s absolutely unacceptable that we’re still facing the same problems after all this time, despite players constantly raising these concerns with seemingly no resolution,” TenZ said.\n“This was definitely one of the biggest contributing factors that led to my decision to hang up the mouse. I’ve been hearing horror stories about the EMEA league for a long time now. What’s even the point of franchising and hosting matches on LAN if they’re going to be played on online servers? It’s unreal, and Riot needs to do better.”\nRiot Games apologized for tech issues on this day specifically, offering in-person attendees a full refund for their tickets and promising to reschedule matches. However, it isn’t clear what Riot plans to do in order to address scathing criticism from some of their biggest pros.",
            "content_length": 4890
        },
