            "article_id": "e0d8e15c3f7664f1",
            "title": "Tdawgg Calls Out Hypocrisy in the VALORANT Scene: Bootlicking and Bandwagoning - India Today Gaming",
            "published_at": "Fri, 14 Feb 2025 08:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "India Today Gaming",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.indiatodaygaming.com/news/story/tdawgg-calls-out-hypocrisy-in-the-valorant-scene-bootlicking-and-bandwagoning-7299",
            "query_players": [
                "JonahP"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "hype",
                "JonahP",
                "nobody",
                "YOU"
            ],
            "content": "Tdawgg Calls Out Hypocrisy in the VALORANT Scene: Bootlicking and Bandwagoning\nTdawgg calls out hypocrisy in the VALORANT scene, exposing how critics switch sides once players succeed.\nThe VALORANT community has always been a battleground of shifting narratives, but Sentinels' coach Tdawgg recently called out what he sees as blatant hypocrisy. He pointed fingers at players, streamers, and fans who once mocked up-and-coming competitors only to praise them once they found success. In his eyes, it’s just another case of people hating until there’s clout to chase.\nFrom Mockery to Praise: The Hypocrisy Exposed\nOn Twitter, Tdawgg highlighted players like Inspire, Cane, Ion, Nightz, and Poppin—once dismissed as \"stream snipers\" and \"ranked trolls\" by pros, casuals, and streamers alike. Many doubted their aggressive playstyle could translate to the professional stage. But once these players proved themselves by winning an offseason tournament, those same critics suddenly changed their stance—acting as if they had supported them all along.\nFake Supporters or Just Proven Wrong?\nTdawgg didn’t hold back, calling out those who ridiculed players in the past but are now eager to align with them. He reflected on his own experiences with teams like METRO, SR, QoR, and TSM, where they were dismissed as \"not good enough.\" Yet now, under a \"new three-letter tag,\" those same critics are conveniently rewriting history.\nHe also criticized those who \"play both sides\"—people who are dismissive when it’s convenient but quick to latch onto rising stars once they gain traction, all in an effort to \"climb the social ladder.\"\nJonahP’s Take on the Matter\nTSM’s JonahP also chimed in, backing Tdawgg’s sentiments. He emphasized how the moment you start winning, people suddenly switch up—highlighting the importance of recognizing who’s been real since day one versus who’s just riding the hype wave.\nAlso Read: Marvel Rivals 2025 Invitational: Here's Everything You Need to Know\nA Reality Check for the VALORANT Scene\nTdawgg’s rant isn’t just about hypocrisy—it’s a reminder of how quickly narratives change in esports. One day, you're a \"nobody\"; the next, the same people who doubted you are acting like they were in your corner all along. His words serve as a wake-up call for the community, exposing how easily history is rewritten when success enters the equation.",
            "content_length": 2366
        },
        {
            "article_id": "0c64a17ef50c5101",
            "title": "PatMen: “We improved our strengths and didn’t overthink” – Paper Rex stuns G2 at Masters Toronto - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Fri, 13 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifEFVX3lxTFBKTHlwUnFpZGIzeGZGVE15RHdhcFlfWVpNMXFFakMzZlhYTWFOMjNyQmlCUUhob3NzU2t6U2FGRE1TaTJMUGlUWlUtRldLbDg5RzVONEZ2ZDRFMTFBdHNUSm91SFBpOExKWDdVSFhQN3lpcGVMYnlNbGs2WXc?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/paper-rex-beat-g2-masters-toronto",
            "query_players": [
                "PatMen"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "d4v41",
                "f0rsakeN",
                "jawgemo",
                "Jinggg",
                "mindfreak",
                "PatMen",
                "something"
            ],
            "content": "Thanks to Paper Rex, the majority of players’ Masters Toronto Pick’Ems are already out of the window after just a single playoffs series. The Pacific side followed up their dramatic win against Team Liquid with a 2-0 win over Masters Bangkok runners-up and tournament favourites G2. The result means that G2 are immediately resigned to the lower bracket, while Paper Rex’s train continues full steam ahead in the upper bracket.\nTaking questions from the media in their post-match press conference, multiple Paper Rex members were quizzed on the matchup with G2, with callbacks to their last match against the Americas side — a loss in Masters Shanghai in May of last year.\nHead coach Alexandre “alecks” Sallé was asked for his thoughts on the difference between the two games and how his team prepared this time around.\n“We prepared as per usual, we watched the games a little bit together and then after that we made sure to correct any mistakes and tried to adjust and prepare for what G2 might do. To be honest, I don’t really remember the match from Shanghai… but I think we prepared the same way. But I think we’re just a little bit older and wiser now. That’s what happened.”\nWang “Jinggg” Jing Jie was also asked to compare the matches, and explain how he felt getting revenge over G2 and Alexander “jawgemo” Mor specifically.\n“[It] definitely feels great because I think last time when we played against them, we didn’t really play at our best. We hesitated a lot because we had a lot of pressure. But I think today, even though we had pressure, we still managed to overcome the pressure and pretty well. So yeah, I’m really happy and very proud of my whole team.”\nElsewhere in the press conference, in-game leader Jason “f0rsakeN” Susanto was quizzed on his side’s calling, and who was mainly responsible for their proactive plays during the series.\n“I think we read them well as a team and we call together. I guess mostly like Ilya (something) and me, or d4v41.”\nMuch of the press conference centred around Patrick “PatMen” Mendoza, arguably Paper Rex’s standout performer on the day while still competing in his first-ever global event. The Filipino player recorded a 34/29/20 score across the two maps with 205 ACS.\nDespite playing in his first tournament of this magnitude, PatMen has looked unfazed. He was asked how he’s been preparing for matches and how he’s found handling the pressure of the step up.\n“I prepare really, really hard and then my team tells me like everything I can do to help the team. Especially alecks, he always tells me a lot of things like before the match and he’s boosting my confidence as well. So everyone on the team — it really matters to me, because I know if they’re trusting me, I can play really well, and I don’t hold back a lot. So I think my team is really, really boosting my confidence a lot, especially right now.”\nEarlier in the tournament, PatMen had spoken publicly of having felt nervous ahead of some series, and was asked how he’s managed to tackle them.\n“For my nervousness, I turn it into excitement. Before we start the match I’m so nervous, but when I step on the stage and I feel the vibes on my team, I gain confidence. And then it’s like, the show must go on, so no need to hold up.”\nGiven that Masters Toronto is PatMen’s first global event, this series also marked his first time playing against G2. PatMen highlighted the need to not overthink things as one of the key factors PRX had to consider ahead of the series.\n“G2 was almost Masters Bangkok champions, and they’re also the Stage1 champion in the Americas. So I knew it was going to be a tough game and they were going to be prepared for us. And our preparation, we just improved our strengths and didn’t overthink a lot, because [of] what alecks said earlier that maybe they changed a lot of things that we didn’t expect. So we just watched a little bit.”\nWith the player currently benched in order to accommodate PatMen’s arrival, Aaron “mindfreak” Leonhart has been seen in the coaching booth alongside alecks throughout the tournament so far, often displaying far more muted reactions in comparison to his notoriously fiery coach. During the press conference, alecks was asked about the benefits of having mindfreak’s input in a coaching capacity during Paper Rex’s matches.\n“I think specifically someone with experience in competing knows how it feels like at the moment. He does a lot of individual talking to people. Like, in between maps, he goes to Jason, tells him what he can do better. For example, today he was going to prepare Icebox, he was going to tell the boys what to do and he was very confident of what was going to happen.\n“So I think being a player and then after that stepping back gives him a unique perspective, something that I will never have. And yeah, it’s definitely valuable in my opinion. He’s acting is a very good assistant coach.”",
            "content_length": 4895
        },
        {
            "article_id": "d38e84f7b0e6a15e",
            "title": "Jemkin Finally Arrives On The Global Stage - VCT Toronto Day 7 Preview - Hotspawn",
            "published_at": "Fri, 13 Jun 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Hotspawn",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMiigFBVV95cUxPS3oyWm5idWx6VnAybDVESGFTcDlUUjM4MG1ObzBwU1oteUZQUm53UkhqZ1lnbEN6LVdmRjBOTW5aOXJOVWxNOGI3RmtTeGdGVmQzM20wVjVpUnFVY0lVZmRLUnh5SS13Wmp3WHRsX0dYbklTSVp2bkxKcXB5QXViekV0Nkxlek15SkE?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/valorant/news/jemkin-global-debut-masters-toronto-preview",
            "query_players": [
                "Jemkin"
            ],
            "matched_players": [
                "Boaster",
                "Elite",
                "Jemkin",
                "SiuFatBB"
            ],
            "content": "The playoffs of Masters Toronto have begun, with its first set of games seeing Paper Rex and Sentinels progress through the upper bracket while G2 Esports and XLG Esports are already resigned to lower bracket runs. Next up is an all-Eastern clash between RRQ, making their international debut, and fan favourites Wolves, followed by Fnatic making their return to the global stage against the in-form Gen.G.\nKicking off at 12:00 local time (18:00 CEST) is the day’s first matchup between RRQ and Wolves. Ahead of the tournament, this draw would easily see RRQ as the favourites, however their opponents have all the momentum coming off their unexpected Swiss run.\nLike Wolves, Rex Regum Qeon are attending a global event for the very first time, but received a bye to the playoffs after winning VCT Pacific Stage 1. Their grand final victory over Gen.G was largely in thanks to their star Duelist Maksim “Jemkin” Batorov. A player who has been a top performer in Pacific for some time and enters as one of the top 5 players at Masters Toronto, Jemkin has the chance to cement his name amongst the elite of VALORANT if he performs at this event.\nHis play this year has been undoubtedly world-class, but he lacks the big-stage pedigree that’s necessary to be regularly discussed as one of VCT’s top pros. Finally finding some domestic success was a start, but a significant global event run would go some way further.\nAgainst Jemkin and RRQ are Wolves Esports, a side written off entirely before the tournament, who have silenced the doubters to reach the playoffs. The team’s vibes are infectious, led by the joyful Pong “SiuFatBB” Ka-hei, and it’s no surprise that they’ve gathered a raft of new supporters during their Toronto run already.\nRRQ will likely be favourites to win the series, though they’d be foolish to underestimate Wolves as the Chinese side has more than shown that they are far above the level that the community thought of them coming into the tournament.\nThe second series of the day, due to begin at 15:00 local time (21:00 CEST), is between EMEA Stage 1 winners Fnatic and Pacific runners-up Gen.G. Two sides that have been in great form in their most recent matches, their clash should be one of the best so far in Toronto.\nFor Fnatic, it’s their return to the global stage after missing out on Masters Bangkok qualification earlier this year. At that time, their shuffled roster had struggled to get things together, and their poor Kickoff run resulted in the benching of coach Chris “Elmapuddy” Tebbit.\nSince then, and following the promotion of Milan “Milan” de Meij to head coach, Fnatic is back to their EMEA-dominating ways, with team vibes at their all-time best according to superstar IGL Jake “Boaster” Howlett. The side continued to ramp up throughout Stage 1, finally hitting their peak during back-to-back victories over Team Heretics, who, at the time, were the in-form team in the league.\nMasters Toronto is the chance for Fnatic to prove that they’re back to top level once and for all. Not only would it be a return to the top for the organisation, but it would show that their new-look lineup can form its own legacy and allow the team move on from its 2023 glory days.\nUp against Fnatic is Gen.G, a side that has gone from strength to strength in this tournament. It should come as little surprise to see them perform at an elite level, given that they still hold 3/5 of the 2024 Masters Shanghai-winning roster. They would have emerged as Pacific champions in Stage 1 were it not for Jemkin and his ridiculous 89-kill performance in the Stage 1 grand final.\nThey were the side that claimed the most convincing wins during the Swiss stage of Masters Toronto, winning both of their ties with 2-0 scorelines. Their opening series against MIBR saw the Brazilian organisation win a measly 6 combined rounds across the two maps. Then, Gen.G’s second matchup against familiar opponents Paper Rex brought one closer map, a 13-11 win on Ascent, before Gen.G again crushed their opposition by winning the second map 13-3.\nThis momentum could prove pivotal for Gen.G as they enter the matchup full of confidence and full of on-stage practice on the current patch. Fnatic, on the other hand, come in cold, having played no official games since the EMEA final on May 18th — nearly a full month ago. However, that also means that there are no official VODs for Gen.G to review, meaning Fnatic enter the series with all of their new strategies under wraps.",
            "content_length": 4483
        },
        {
            "article_id": "f5e6956b5e521be3",
            "title": "VALORANT Champions 2025 Paris: Prize money, Format, Dates - Esports News UK",
            "published_at": "Fri, 12 Sep 2025 07:00:00 GMT",
            "source": "Esports News UK",
            "google_news_url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMimAFBVV95cUxQN1g1QmZWMFpydlpuX3VPSkJvcWRfX0piYklqVkstNWp5OUk1Uk5Dd2l4WnhIa2RXelJjRkQ1VS1YblBWZndIVUdQclJZSjg2VldxT1BQY2tHejhKektYMzlHc0sybk54dms1M2NYclJKX1IzeVRLNkRDRWlBck45UHNLalVWdHFkWWlfN3hxWmQ3RDA3dlpHMA?oc=5",
            "article_url": "https://esports-news.co.uk/2025/09/12/valorant-champions-2025-paris-prize-money-format-dates/",
            "query_players": [
